      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>We have a long way to travel before we get back to what Dickens meant; and the passage is along a rambling English road, a twisting road such as Mr. Pickwick travelled. . . • The inn does not point to the road; the road points to the inn. And all roads point at last to an ultimate inn, where we shall meet Dickens and all his characters: and when we drink again it shall be from the great flagons in the tavern at the end of the world."—G. K. CHESTERTON.</p>
        <p>The Christmas season with us once again, once again our mind reverts, in eager haste, to the beloved high-priest of that joyous festival— Charles Dickens. As is our custom at this time, we shall seek, in those immortal writings of his, that bright and practical philosophy which never yet has failed to cheer the heart and mind; and, reluctantly, shall lay down at length the well-read volumes, to find our whole being pervaded with a happy optimism which, in view of existing circumstances, might easily have been lost in the vast cataclysm of doubt out of whose midst we are at present so earn- estly endeavouring to escape. We may say "in view of existing circumstances," because, to all intents and purposes, our festive season will this year—and, who knows? next year also— be non-oxistent. In a day when the true civilisation should, indeed, have been nearing its acme, we have found ourselves precipitated without warning into, a maelstrom of events unparalleled, perhaps, in all the repulsive annals of barbarism. Consequently we shall this time seek to discover what Dickens would have had to say upon this unprecedented situation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"WAR IS FOOLISH."</head>
        <p>"WAR IS FOOLISH."</p>
        <p>Knowing the. immeasurable hatred in which our great reformer and novelist held individual warfare even of the most paltry nature, we may safely conclude that his views, had it been his lot to witness the terrible happenings of the last few months, would have been in no 'wise Neitzscheian. That he would have condemned whole-heartedly the present war he has stated in no uncertain terms, through the medium of Lawyer Snitchey in "The Battle of Life," using his inimitable satire with the following effect: "Granted if you please that war is foolish. There we agree. For example. Here's a smiling country once overrun by soldiers— trespassers every man of 'em—and laid waste by fire and sword. . . . The idea of any man exposing himself, voluntarily, to fire and sword! Stupid, wasteful, positively ridiculous: you laugh at your fellow-creatures, you know, when you think of it !" He introduces a characteristic idea: • "Why, • • the great battle was fought on this ground. On this ground where we now sit, where I saw my two girls dance this morning, where the fruit has just been gathered for our eating from these trees, the roots of which are struck ip Men, not earth—so many lives were lost, that within my recollection, generations afterwards, a churchyard full of bones, and dust of bones, and chips of cloven skulls, has been dug up from underneath our feet here. Yet not a hundred people in that battle knew for what they fought, or why; not a hundred of the inconsiderate rejoicers in the victory, why they rejoiced. Not half a hundred people were the better for the gain or loss. Not half- a-dozen men agree to this hour on the cause or merits; and nobody, in short, ever knew any- thing distinct about it, but the mourners of the slain. Serious, too! Such a system !" Yet there is a genuine trait of optimism underlying this whimsical sentiment. War, the novelist realises, is not quite hopeless: from the very dust of the slain will rise faces fairer yet to look upon, and cities more noble than those which are fallen. This reminds us of Omar Khayyam: "I sometimes think that never blows so red The Rose as where some buried CEesar bled; That every Hyacinth the garden wears Dropt in her Lap from some once lovely Head." Here, at least, optimist and pessimist may meet on common ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TWO SIDES TO THE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>TWO SIDES TO THE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>In the matter of war—as, indeed, in all mat- ters to which he turned his attention—Dickens' views are in no degree narrow-minded. There is right on both sides, he declares. At the very outset of "A Tale of Two Cities"—which depicts war hitherto at its worst, presenting ias t does a vivid picture of one of the bloodiest campaigns in all history—he thus calmly sur- veys the period: "It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it *as the age of wis- dom, it was the age of foolishness, it was the epoch of belief, it was the epoch of incredulity, it was the season of Light, it was the season of Darkness, it was the spring of hope, it was the winter of despair, we had everything before us, we had nothing before us, we were all going direct to Heaven, we were all going direct the other way. . . ." Bellona, he would say, has her moods; and it is quite possible for her at times to exercise an influence which, in the long run, will prove beneficial to mankind. "There are seasons," he said upon another occasion, "when the evils of peace, though not so acutely felt, are immeasurably greater, and when a powerful nation, by admitting the right of any autocrat to do wrong, SOWS by such com- plicity the seeds of its own ruin, and over- shadows itself in time to come with that fatal influence which greater and ambitious powers are sure to exercise over their weaker neigh- bours. Therefore it is that the tree has not its root in English ground from which the yard wand can be made that will measure—the mine has not its place in English soil that will supply the material of a pair of scales to weigh —the influence that may be at stake in the war in which we are now straining all our energies." Nevertheless, in war there is, after all, only the possibility of good; the evil side of the question is only too palpably a fact. And we know how scathingly the novelist, whilst approving the ambitions of the revolting Freniu peasants, denounced the methods by wiiiili they sought to realise them. A thought also has Dickens voiced which might have been written expressly in reference to the Arch-Min: "That war is, at any time and in any shape, a most dreadful and deplor- able calamity, we need no proverb to tell us; but it is just because it is such a calamity, and because that calamity must not for ever be 444.444444+4101.014 44++ Pig 014 ++44 +</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>impending over us at the fancy of one man against all mankind, that.-we must not allow that man to darken fronflour view the figures of peace and justice between whom and us he now interposes. If ever there were a time when the true spirits of two countries were really fighting in the cause of human advancement and freedom—no matter what diplomatic notes or other nameless botherations"—our precious Blue Book !—'' from number one to one thousand and one, may haVe preceded their taking the field—if ever there were a time when noble hearts were deserving well of mankind by exposing themselves to the obedient bayonets of a rash and barbarian tyrant, it is now, when the faithful children of England and France are fighting so bravely." We may add the names of Belgium and Russia. And yet, although he was thus opposed to any idea of war; and although he followed, during his lifetime, the courses both of the Indian Mutiny and the Crimean campaigns (in addition to half-a-dozen occurrences, of univer- sal importance, abroad—of which importance, as affecting international relations, he was by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ROV;AI, CHRISTMAS GREETLN</head>
        <p>ROV;AI, CHRISTMAS GREETLN</p>
        <p>NU EDGAR ROWED ON THE DEE BY EIGHT VASSAL KINGS. [As supplied by Messrs. Raphael Tuck.]</p>
        <p>no means oblivious—vide "American Notes" and "Pictures from Italy "), we find practically I no reference to the question in his works. His 1 policy, after all, was the teaching of content- ment and peace to the individual—all else . would follow; and, a true politician, he sought to impress upon the countless multitudes of his : followers tile advantages of hia own ideal rather : than the fallacies of those of his opponents. TROUBLES ABROAD AND AT HOME. From his letters and speeches, however, we gather many valuable references to the matter- in-hand, for herein Dickens did not hesitate to give full expression to his opinions. Thus, in the course of a letter sent on November Ist, 1854, to the lion. 'qrs. Watson, the novelist declares himself to be full of mixed feeling about the war—admiration of the valiant Eng- lish, and yet something like despair to see how " the old cannon-smoke and blood-mists obscure the wrongs and sufferings of the people at home." "When I•consider the Patriotic Fund on the one hand," he continues, "and on the other the poverty and wretchedness engendered by cholera—of which, in London alone, an infinitely larger number of English people than are likely to be slain in the whole Russian War have miserably and needlessly died—l feel as if the world had been pushed back live hundred years. . ." And with the dread possibility of war hang- ing continually, like the sword of Damocles, over the head of a nation so responsible as England, it certainly is hardly to be expected that—except by the ignoring of the danger— sufficient time and money can be devoted to internal reform. Happily, the particular con- ditions deplored by the novelist have by now almost ceased to exist; yet, for many, other urgently required improvements, our country is sadly in need of the forty millions (or there- abouts) which would be liberated, for her dis- posal, by the accomplishment, after the war, of that desired—indeed, vital—reduction in the proportion of armaments. In view of that fact, we cannot but conclude that, in this day, Dickens would have been a prime advocate of the International l'eace Congress. Neither can we doubt that the "Business as Usual" policy- would have appealed most strongly to Dickens. "It does not require any extraordinary sagacity in a commercial • assembly," lie pointed out to the Com- mercial -Travellers' Schools at the end of , 1854, "to appreciate the dire evils of war. The great interests of trade enfeebled by it, the enterprise of . better times paralysed by it, all the peaceful arts bent down before it, too pal- pably indicate its character and results; so that far less practical intelligence than that by . which I am surrounded would be sufficient to ; appreciate the horrors of war." In a letter written to M. de Cerjat at the beginning of the same year, he says: "The absorption of the</p>
        <p>with Charles Knight early in 1854, he tells him : "My satire is against those who see figures and averages—the representatives of the wickedest and most enormous vice of this time— , the addled heads who would take the average of cold in the Crimea during twelve months as a reason for clothing a soldier in nankeens on,a night when he would be froxen to death in fur." The return of Joe Willett, in "Barnaby Budge," from the never-to-be-forgotten " Salwanners," calls forth a remark or two upon the interest displayed by the public in wounded soldiers home from the wars—of which we are at present having so many striking examples : " It was a long time, too—for Never, as the proverb says, is a long day—before they forgot to take an interest in wounded soldiers at the Maypole; or before Joe omitted to re- fresh them, for the sake of his old campaign; or before the sergeant left off looking in there now and then; or before they fatigued them- selves, or each other, by talking on these occasions of battles and sieges, and hard weather and hard service, and a thousand things belonging to a soldier's life."</p>
        <p>That however: is in the—cla^; 3,;r1-e'n suffering is over. And in a long article upon "The Real Horrors of War," which appeared at the end of 1859 in "All the Year Round," Dickens, after referring to the "human shambles" which will not for a long time "be made to take • the smooth, decent, tranquil aspect of a graveyard," says: "But for the people outside, who stood round watching the fight, with bated breath and senses painfully strained,' it seemed a gloriqus, thrilling spectacle. . . . But this is all no more than the fine colouring of a con- sumptive cheek, or the bloom of a rotten apple. There is net, of all things existent, a more re- pulsive, coarse, untheatrical business than war, and what it brings with it. The delicate film of gaudiness rubs off in an hour. . . . The real horrors of war are played out to the utmost on the hospital pallet when ' the the:Atm.:a business is all over."</p>
