      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>4444144 u</head>
        <p>4444144 u</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE BRIGADE. Presentation of Certificates at Social Gathering.</p>
        <p>The members Of the nursing division of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade held a very successful social in the Junior Conservative Hall on Wednesday even- ing, with the object of assisting the funds of the division and providing uniforms. There was an excellent attemlance, and an enjoyable even- ing was passed in dancing, Mr. Charles Sinclair acting as M.C. The intervals were filled by musical items, given by the members of the divi- sion and their friends; whilst selections were also provided by a gramophone kindly Thi by Assistant-Scoutmaster Cartwright (Ist Warring- ton Troop).</p>
        <p>TOKENS OF NURSING PROFICIENCY, The evening was also made the occasion of an interesting presentation of certificates and other tokens of nursing proficiency to the ladies who had earned them. The distribution was made by Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, First-Officer Williams occupying the chair. There were also present: Supt. J. lose (Crosfield's Division), Second- Officer Spunn (Headquarters Division), 'Lady.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>.444.0144014 444401444+ 44 44+014 ++,14014,141</head>
        <p>.444.0144014 444401444+ 44 44+014 ++,14014,141</p>
        <p>Warhurst, and Lady-</p>
        <p>Superintendent Mrs. Secretary Mrs: Fisher. The Chairman said Mr. Fulton-Smith had been a good friend, and would always remain so. With reference to the work of the St. John Ambulance during the present crisis, the reports issued by the authorities contained valuable tributes which spoke for themselves. The St. John order was not behind any of the others. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>NO BETTER OR NOBLER SERVICE.</head>
        <p>NO BETTER OR NOBLER SERVICE.</p>
        <p>_ Before distributing rie certificates, Mr. J. Fulton-Smith said members of the Ambulance Brigade were in a position to save many lives; and they could give no better and nobler service to God than the relief of suffer- ing. They were doing a great work in this country, and he wished them every success. (Applause.) Mr. Fulton-Smith then presented certificates of proficiency in first aid to: The Misses Apple- ton, Ayres, Bannon, Clark, Crawley, Dobbins, Davies, Edwards, Firth, Gregory, Garnett, lioultram, Jones, Johnson, Leah, Malkin, Malone, Madden, Newns, Rose, Stevens, Spivey, Terry, William d and Walker, and Mesdames Berry, Boulton and Monks. He also handed to Miss Begg a voucher; to the Misses Banning and Williams, medallions; to ,Mesdames War-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ETERNAL MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>THE ETERNAL MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>Such, briefly then, were the broadminded and diverse opinions which our great novelist would have held upon the titanic struggle at present raging. No fitter conclusion could be given here than that eternal message wh'ch will ring down, through all the ages, to those nations upon whose honour and integrity depend the happiness and security of the smaller ones: "So may the New Year be a happy one to you, happy to many more whose happiness depends on you! So may each year be happier than the last, and not the meanest of our brethren and sisterhood debarred their rightful share, in what our Great Creator formed them to enjoy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>*41,1444</head>
        <p>*41,1444</p>
        <p>iii4,14)14%410101440•D01i01i,1444,140H4</p>
        <p>hurst and Fisher, labels; and to the Misses Appleton, Banning, Burgess, Williams and A. Williams, nursing certificates.</p>
        <p>A GIFT FOR DR. MANSON.</p>
        <p>Later, as a token of the esteem of the nurs- ing division, Dr. T. S. Manson (lecturer) was presented with a beautiful leather writing-case, and First-Officer Williams (instructor) with a large inkstand. The recipients suitably replied to the eulogistic speeches which were delivered; after which a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Fulton-Smith and .the Chairman was carried with acclamation. Before the assembly dispersed, First-Officer Williams expressed appreciation of the services of Mesdames Warhurst and Fisher, and all the other ladies who had contributed to the success of the function.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>U LT I MAT U M.</head>
        <p>U LT I MAT U M.</p>
        <p>Mr. Punch begs to remind the 1,793 corre- spondents who have lately sent him delightful plays upon the word "wet" [De Wet the man and "do wet" the rain (ha, ha)] that the same idea had already occurred to 15.825 correspond- ents during the Boer War. Time is a great healer, but twelve years is not long enough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>English mind in the war is, to me, a melan- choly thing . . . I fear I clearly see that, for years to come, domestic reforms are shaken to the root." Yet the present—greatest— struggle had been in progress for weeks when the Home Rule Bill was placed upon the Statute Book: at least the nation comprehends that lesson of experience. We may also note that he adds: "For all this it is an indubitable fact • . that the future peace of the t‘orld renders the war imperative upon us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LIFE OF A .SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>THE LIFE OF A .SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>_ Well may Air. T. W. Hill, the well-known Dickens" authority, exclaim: "How Dickens, with his punctilious methods, would have de- lighted in the smooth working and mastexly efficiency shown by Earl Kitchener's arrange- ments during the present crisis of 1914"! Once having decided to perform any task, the thor- ough manner in which the novelist would carry it into execution is too well-known to demand explanation at this stage. The larger plan resolved upon, he would immediately enter into innumerable details. Thus, corresponding</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>USEFUL and ORNAMENTAL XMAS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GIFTS FOR ALL.</head>
