      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BACON.</head>
        <p>BACON.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Old Spirit of Goodwill Strengthened by National Ordeal. THE CHEERY MESSAGE OF THE SHOPS.</head>
        <p>Old Spirit of Goodwill Strengthened by National Ordeal. THE CHEERY MESSAGE OF THE SHOPS.</p>
        <p>To " Business as Usual" as a motto for the times it would appear, from abundant signs on every hand, that we are making ready to add "and Christmas as usual "—or, at any rate, as near to usual as the terrible European paroxysm of war and rapine, amid which the 1914th anni- versary of the World's Greatest Event is being ushered in, will permit. The Spirit of Christ- mas Past, his torch emitting the incense of happy, beautiful memories of bygone Yuletides, is already abroad, bidding us prepare for the advent of the latest-to-be-born of his numerous brothers—a coming now near at hand. Yes, signs of the coming of Christmas sur- round us—greet our eyes at every turn as we walk down the busy streets, where the shop- keepers vie with each other in stocking their windows with such good things to eat, to wear, to look at, and to use, as set us longing for a duplicate of the Purse of Fortunatus; greet our eyes as we scan the columns of our diurnal and weekly newspapers—columns wherein are displayed in the most seductive type at the command of the printer and in the most per- suasive language at the service of the astute advertisement manager, mouth-watering an- nouncements as to the good things that may be had, if ; greet our ears as we nightly sit at home at our ease, day's duty done, and listen to the blithesome carol-singer on the front door- step chanting of the trio of ships that come sailing home, but, despite a plenitude of verses, never seem to arrive; or singing us of the Holy Babe; of the joys that Mary had, of the condescension displayed to the humble, but doubting, page-let by "Good King VVenceslas." It is, in the main, somewhat painful vocalism— but it is well meant, and, thus, it accords with the Spirit of the Season that is upon us, and we do not grudge the humble "copper," for is there not much value for so little money in the cheery " Thank-you-sir-merry-Chrissinass-when- it-comes-good-night " of the grubby-fingered little artiste who so gratefully receives the trifling coin proferred? "Eli ! mon, but there's more than a muckle o' the sperrit of Christmas-time i' the bawbee given t' caroller," says a character somewhere in some one of Barrie's kale-yard stories. And so there is! _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Take a walk _young the shops and you find</p>
        <p>the windows full of the Christmas suggestion— chock-full, brimful of possibilities of pleasure-- both for yourself and others, for Christmas is a time for giving as well as receiving; indeed there be those, and their number is legion, who always experience more of the giving than the getting—but theirs is the altruistic compensa- tion, theirs the realisation of the truth that it is always, and especially at Yuletide, more com- forting to one's "amour propre "—to put it on no higher plane—to give than to get. A detailed description of the shops will appear next week.</p>
        <p>of the Royal Family, including their Majesties the King and Queen. Auto-stationery, boxed cards and post cards specially adopted to the needs of the season are submitted in great pro- fusion.</p>
        <p>For calendars Messrs. Raphael Tuck have laid the best artists and authors under tribute, while children's gift books, toy books, and games open up a perfect paradise to the young mind. "Father Tuck's Annual" is a rare treasure- house, perpetually new and unceasingly enter- taining. If anybody ever showed just cause why busi- ness should be conducted as usual in war time, Messrs. Raphael Tuck have done so in respect of the business of wishing our friends and neigh- bours well at Christmas time.</p>
        <p>problems with which we are confronted are treated in a humorous vein. If ridicule really kills, the Germans should not be long about dying as a militant nation after " Punch's " Christmas onslaught. "We hear from Buckingham Palace," says the charivariety artist, " that while King George cannot undertake to keep the Kaiser's Christ- mas dinner hot for him, he will certainty do his best to cook his goose."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CIIILDREN'S GIFT BOOKS.</head>
        <p>CIIILDREN'S GIFT BOOKS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Cassell and Co. have placed on the market this year, as in other years, a remarkable set of Christmas Gift Books for both boys and girls. A special note accompanies them to the effect that all the publications of this noted firm are printed in this country. From their quality it is apparent that this is so, but it is as well to bring the fact plainly before the public. "The British Girls' Annual" and "The Brit- ish Boys' Annual" are substantial books for young people of the maturer class, and are pub- lished at ss. each. Each is brim full of the stories dear to the hearts of older girls and boys. The Girls' Annual has nearly 250 pages of attractive stories and charming illustrations, coloured and in black and white. The Boys' Annual is on a similarly elaborate scale, and contains, among other special features, a long complete school story, entitled "Shoulder to Shoulder" and au exciting adventure story, "The Night Air Mail," by Claude Grahame- White and Harry Harper, and many school and adventure tales by well-known writers for boys. For the smaller children there is a wonderful collection of wonderful stories and pictures in "Little Folks," Which has a place in the affec- tions of the children of this country, and a reputation that is all its own; and " Casseys Annual for Boys and Girls." Here we have More adventures of Edward, the Red Teddy Bear; stories by Dorothea Moore, Olaf Baker, Murray Fisher, Agnes Gyozier Herbertson, &amp;c., about 200 colour pictures, and a splendid paint- ing competition. The price in these cases is 3s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHRISTMAS NUMBERS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS NUMBERS.</p>
        <p>" The Queen," the lady's newsaper, is rich irij, seasonable illustrations and articles, the pres: entation plate this year being a novelty in the I way of a remarkably clever imitation of an oil painting, "Christmas in the Britains Overseas," "The Christmas Decoration of Churches," "Christmas in Paris 1870-1914," "Christmas _ . ..</p>
        <p>_ in Belgium," "The First All. British Christmas Tree," are titles of articles which give some idea of the manner in which "The Queen" has taken occasion by the hand to the best advantage. There are the other features—fashions, games, &amp;c.—for which the paper has at high and de- served reputation. "Woian at Home" has a new Sherlock Holmes story by Conan Doyle, " The Valley of Fear," and a readable article by Annie S. Swan on "Some New Aspects of Life." There are also many attractions of special interest to women—cookery for Christmas, working for the war, and the health and toilet being entertain- ingly presented. "Holly Letkves," the Christmas number of "Sporting and Dramatic News," is a splendid shillingsworth of first-class paintings, drawings and articles, presented in the vigorous manner that belongs to "Holly Leaves." "A Red Christmas" and "A Day's Fishing" are articles that indicate the extent of the ground covered by its writers, and the illustrations are similarly diverse. The photogravure supplement is a capital refiroduction of A. C. Gow's famous painting "Farewell to Nelson." Christmas would be incomplete without a "Punch Almanack," in which the many serious lopypi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHRISTMAS CARDS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CARDS.</p>
        <p>The Christmas card trade has assumed such gigantic dimensions that it has to be anticipated by those who cater for it. Christmas cards that will be sent from friend to friend within the next few weeks were designed and printed before the war was expected, and so they will, happily, have no bearing upon the struggle that now holds a pleat part of the world in its grip. It , is well that this should be so for the season and its customs that war cannot altogether mar will ' be all the better for an occasional absence of all reference to a question that has a tendency to absorb our every thought. Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons have sent out a charming selection of Christmas cards, and, as usual, they have succeeded in producing several novelties of outstanding attractiveness. The chief of these is the pot-pourri card, which contains a " raised " vase filled with a delicious scent, a devise that is at once clever and accept- able. Next in originality and impressiveness are the choice water-colour and Gobelin series, followed closely by others, the bare mention of which would make a very long list. Among older friends are the reproduction of Royal Christmas-cards, as supplied to many members</p>
        <p>iii.÷.l44-/-Er.44.44.1-1-14.144-1.4.14.1,444-re+444-1-1444 44+4.144-14, A RIIN THROUGH THE The registered letters are semen up in a POST OFFICE. gre.en bag, and placed with the ordinary ones in the 'appropriate mail-bag. When this bag goes out it is accompanied h 3 the letter bill, a copy of which is kept at the despatch office. It is signed by the person who sends and by the person who receives the bag, and contains a note of the regis- tered letter bag, duplicates of receipts fur registered letters, and amounts of unpaid or underpaid postage. The bag is sealed, taken to a platform outside the Post Office and sent away by carts to the railway stations or to various district and suburban offices. IN THE HOSPITAL. In the General Post Office there is an in- teresting department, known as the hospital where the letter doctor sits in authority to deal with letters which are in various stages of dilapidation. Here on heavily laden tables are loose letters, labels without pack- ages, packages without labels, and a be- wildering assortment of all kinds of articles in a state of exquisite confusion. Bad wrappers are mended if possible; if not, they are replaced by new ones and redirected, and the fact that this has been done is noted on the wrapper. When there is no mark left by which the destination can be deter- mined the letter is sent to the Dead Letter Office there to await a claimant. Another fascinating division is the "Blind " department where almost illegib:e and foolish directions are deciphered. This office contains directories of every conceiv- able description, and a set of officials who run down the persons to whom these appar- ently unmanageable documents are sent, with a skill and ingenuity that is little short of marvellous. A letter was once sent to George Augustus Sala with his photo, and the word England on the envelope. Another had a picture of some rye growing up over a gate, and got correctly to Reigate. One let- ter, the smallest ever handled, was written on the back of a pentty stamp, and was duly delivered.</p>
        <p>People pop a postcard or a letter in a let- ter-box, think little more about the matter, and go away in the certain hope that the missive will reach its destination. The mat- ter has become so commonplace that we rare- ly stop to think of the methods of handling the letter from the time we throw it away till the moment when it lies upon the break- fast table of the person to whom it is ad- dressed A visit to the General Post Office in London opens the eyes. There are so many rooms and so many employees that one be- gins to wonder how a letter ever reaches its destination at all,</p>
        <p>The present buildings in St. Martin's-le- Grand are comparatively new, but they stand on the site of the ancient monastery whose name they bear, a monastery that once possessed the privilege of sanctuary where any thief, murderer or other variety of scoundrel was safe once he had placed his foot within the church doors. If the letters that pass over the same spot to-day could be opened and their contents revealed to the public, they would probably show upon paper the same blending of the good and the bad, 'the saintly and the vicious. In these new and ever-growing buildings, work of the most complicated kind is carried on, and only the highest degree of organisation can pos- sibly prevent everything getting into the most hopeless muddle. The work is, on a large scale, pretty much the same as is done smaller offites throughout the kingdom, but here the magnitude of the task is appal- ling.</p>
        <p>THE LONDON LETTERS</p>
        <p>The London letters are sorted under special heads, of which the " Suburban " pile is further divided into districts, and last of all into " walks. " A " walk " comprises the combined rounds of a batch of postmen vary- ing in number from four to nine. The post- men of each walk perform the last of the sorting operations which is known as "set- ting in." A man takes up the letters for his own division, arranges them according to streets, and ties each street up in a separate bundle, with the letters placed according to the numbers of the houses. The length of time taken to complete delivery on any " walk " should not exceed one and a half hours, from the time of leaving the office. Owing to the extensive correspondence of some of the largest firms special mail-bags are kept for their use. Many very curious things are and have been despatched by post. These articles in- clude not merely inanimate dangerous ob- jects like lucifer matches, and imperfectly packed glass bottles, but active offenders of human peace like bees and live lobsters. France has sent us frogs, and Germany sans- ages through the same means, while one en- terprising gentleman in South• Africa, des- patched a stolen diamond as "Hosiery." Foreign letters are first divided into " Continental " and " Colonial " groups. The Continental letters are sent for reclassi- fication ts their respective countries, but the Colonial lettess are divided in London, and arranged according to towns and cities. There is a resident doctor to attend to the bedily health of the huge staff employed. There are interpreters to assist foreigners in the transaction of their business, great shoots and lifts for dealing with the newspapers, Pneumatic tubes for blowing telegrams and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SORTING DEPARTMENT.</head>
        <p>THE SORTING DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The letters as they arrive are dealt with on the ground floor of a big hall which is filled with tables. At each table there are eight or ten clerks. They are supervised by an overseer who business it is to preserve dis- cipline and obtain rapidity. The overseers are under a superintendent, and behind a glass apartment is another batch of clerks concerned with checking attendances and en- suring punctuality.</p>
        <p>Each table is fed by a messenger. The packets, newspapers and large letters are placed scarateiy. The ordinary letters are arranged in piles with the directed side up- permosr. When these operations are over another messenger carries away the comple- ted. piles to be stamped. The " stamp- ing " marks the day and the hour and can- cels the postage stamp. It is performed by hand or by machine. Many different kinds of machines are employed, the speed of some of them being wonderful; hundreds of let- ters can be dealt with in a minute.</p>
        <p>The next operation is " sorting,"—under such heads as " Rail Ways," "Large Towns," "Badly Defaced," "Blind." The two first of these groups are then gone over again and divided up into various "Roads," which have some reference and likeness to the old coaching routes. Some divisions consist of a parent town with three or four others allied to it, a great deal depending on the popula- tion of the district in question. On the "Road." table a further separation into towns is effected. As an illustration we may note that the Carlisle " Road table" re- ceives and sorts the letters for Windermere, Ambleside, Carlisle, Kendal and a Aumber of other places. Finally, the last sortings are all checked to prevent letters being de:ayed by being sent along the wrong road. The ordinary letters are tied in bundles, put into a wicker work protector, and sZipped into a mail-bag. The registered letters ar‘ dealt with in a similar way to the ordinary letters but by a separate department, and at each stage the sorter has to sign an ac- knowledgement of the receipt of the letters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CHRISTMAS, 1914.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS, 1914.</p>
        <p>O Christmas, merry Christmas! We welcome thee once more As we have done so often In the glad days of yore. Those happy hours are over, But memories remain: O Christmas, blessed Christmas, Bring happy hours again. O Christmas, holy Christmas! Thou birthday of our Lord, Though visiting us rarely What joys (lost thou afford ! How many homes are brightened Of high or low degree, In our beloved Motherland And lands across the sea. But, oh! beloved Christmas! How many a vacant chair Reminds us of the heroes On land, on sea, in air. Who perished for their country: Now wipe away the tears Of those who mourn, and bring them Sweet Peace with all that cheers. ARTHUR J. THORP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>CHRISTMAS CHEER I</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CHEER I</p>
        <p>Vicar of Burtonwood on Yuletide Atrocities.</p>
        <p>In the December issue of his parish maga- zine, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burton- wood, has a tilt at the flesh-eater, under the heading "Slaughter and Feasting." "December is with us," he writes; "Christ- mas is near.</p>
        <p>Christmas comes but once a year, And when it comes it brings good cheer.</p>
        <p>So sing the flesh eaters; of good cheer they have no idea apart from baked meats, boiled flesh, and other delicacies! Good cheer! What is it to be this memorable Christmas, when is rag- ing the most horrible war within the memory of man? Plum pudding will be eaten in the French and Belgian trenches with German sauce! Herein this Christmas differs from the ordinary: it is marked by a double blood-shed- ding, human and sub-human. Will the former counteract the latter? Nay, the slaughter of the animal will be much as usual, the blood of Christmas victims will flow in red streams, as at normal times. Business as usual !'</p>
        <p>"Slaughter and Feasting; Feasting and Slaughter." " A bloody war and a blood-soaked Europe will make little difference to the letting of animal blood. Beasts and birds will first be exhibited for admiration, then slaughtered to provide juicy meats for the keeping of the Feast —the great Feast of that glorious Incarnation which meant the Advent of Life, not death—life for man and beast — .</p>
        <p>" Atrocities' at 'the Front' win not inter-1 fere with home ' atrocities ' in the sub-human world—bullocks still will be pole-axed, pigs still will be knifed, birds still will be stabbed and decapitated, just the same as it has been for ever so many generations. "No! the War will make little or no differ- ence to the annual Christmas holocaust of beast and bird; we shall hear the usual bellow- ings and shrieks of the victims, we shall see again the gruesome sights of prize carcases in our streets. Christmas history will repeat itself, it will be as of old. Slaughter and feasting, feasting and slaughter !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>1.14014 q4,140144914,14,14014440H.014+,1014014010]</head>
        <p>1.14014 q4,140144914,14,14014440H.014+,1014014010]</p>
        <p>other Messages ttro-agri rrom Iloor to nor, and room to room, and a host of appliances and inventions for the facilitating of the rap- id handling and transmission or postal ma- terial that fairly bewilder the beho:der by th,gir number, variety and ingenuity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>EX-GERMAN CONSUL SENTENCED TO</head>
        <p>EX-GERMAN CONSUL SENTENCED TO</p>
        <p>DEATH:</p>
        <p>At Durham Assizes this week the ex-German Consul at Sunderland, a naturalised German named Milers, who admitted aiding German reservists to rejoin the Army and pleaded that he discontinued his efforts as soon as he knew that England was involved, was found guilty of high treason and sentenced to death. The Judge intimated that the prisoner had a right of appeal on two points raised for the defence. If on appeal the sentence is confirmed the Home Secretary, it is officially announced, will consider the question ca commuting it to one of penal servitude or imprisonment.</p>
        <p>AN IRISH ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Convention Candidate Defeated.</p>
        <p>Polling in the by-election in the Tullamore or North Division of King's County, rendered necessary by the death of Mr. Edmund Havi- land-Burke, took place on Tuesday. Result E. J. Graham (Ind. Nat.) P. F. Adams (Nat.&gt;</p>
        <p>Majority 79 Mr. Adams was the official candidate of the Irish party, having been selected at a Conven- tion held at Tullamore. Mr. Graham does not belong to Mr. William O'Brien's party, and the issue was clearly one of the right of the Convention to select the candidate for the constituency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>GUNNER'S ANXIOUS JOURNEY.</head>
        <p>GUNNER'S ANXIOUS JOURNEY.</p>
        <p>Four Days' Leave to Visit a Sick Child. An Eastbourne telegram says that Gunner Fleming, of Western-road, received a letter telling him that his child was dying, and he was granted four days' leave of absence from the firing line. The lieutenant of the gunner'. battery happened to be the son of an East- bourne lady, and was able to confirm the man's story. Gunner Fleming came home, and, to his intense relief, found that his child was then out of danger. He returned at once to the trenches full of gratitude for the sympathy of the authorities.</p>
        <p>A FALMOUTH MAN'S FAIRY TALES.</p>
        <p>Imprisoned for Spreading False Reports.</p>
        <p>A sentence of eight weeks' imprisonment has been passed on Harold Wearing, a civilian, who was tried by a court-martial at Falmouth for spreading a report that German warships were in St. Ives Bay, West Cornwall, and advising people to draw their window blinds at night. The prisoner's father at the court-martial said his son appeared to have "war on the brain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PRINCE ALBERT'S HEALTH.</head>
        <p>PRINCE ALBERT'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>The following announcement is published in the Court .Circular The Prince Albert has been examined by a Naval Medical Board, and they do not consider that his loyal Highness is yet in a tit state of health to return to his ship.</p>
      </div>
