      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RAISING THE NEW ARMIES.</head>
        <p>RAISING THE NEW ARMIES.</p>
        <p>OUR TERRITORIALS. The " Keen" Reserve Battalion at Bleockpool.</p>
        <p>Warrington, considering all the surrounding circumstances, has done remarkably well in the matter of providing recruits for the Army, and doubtless the town will do better still. That is the prevailing spirit that is going to carry us to final success. Lord Kitchener says he has noth- ing to complain about at a time when he is getting men at the rate of two divisions a week, but this flow of recruits must be kept up. Last week we made alpecial appeal on behalf of our local "Terriers." The original Battalion is at full fighting strength, and the reserve bat- talion is now nearing completion. More men are still required, and the Officer Com- manding at the Drill Hall ill be pleased to hear from all those who would like to identify themselves with what is essentially an " all- worrington " contribution towards the defence of the Empire. The Reserve Battalion is stationed at Black- pool, and the conditions in which the men are living are of the best. They are billeted in the lodging-houses for which the Brighton of the North is so famous. Not only are the men well fed and well housed, but, their discipline and behaviour being most exemplary, they are allowed a good number of privileges. It is a common thing, we understand, to find recruits voluntarily studying their drill books in their leisure time, the result ,of all this devotion to duty being that they are rapidly developing into the best of military material. As there is necessarily a great want of non-com- missioned officers in our newly-created forces, promotion comes very rapidly to men of real ability and merit, these qualities being the only passport to advancement. Already several men who joined the Reserve Battalion as privates are colour-sergeants. Naturally, the distinguishing note among both officers and men is a combination of cheeriness and keenness. All are happy in their work, and consequently surprising progress is being made. A former adjutant of the Battalion (Major- General Adam) recently visited the Reserve Bat- talion, and expressed his pleasure at seeing such worthy successors of his Warrington lads of the olden days. He also remarked upon the Makerfield ,members of the Battalion, specially referring to the fine, deep-chested men the collier class had provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
