      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>Photographs As Christmas Cards. If you want to send your friend a Christmas reminder that he, or she, will appreciate, why not let Mr. Walter Burrell, of St. Elphin's Studio, near the Parish Church, take your photograph, and enclose it in a suitable eard?— a service he will render, and help you to confer, for a most modest fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>ROADS BAD IN FLANDERS— AND ELSEWHERE. We learn from Berlin that the roads in Flanders are bad, and great difficulty is experienced in moving German troops. The roads are indeed remarkably bad from a German point of view, though, we should imagine, British bayonets and Belgian courage Shave had more to do with the difficul- ties of travel in those parts than bad weather. When the Germans set out for Paris they knew there was always some kind of weather in Flanders, but they made the fatal mistake of forget- ting the possibility of meeting with us and our stout Allies. The truth is that the Germans are travelling back- wards in Flanders, and they must find that a bitter experience, all things con- sidered. The roads in Northern France are also bad, so had in fact that in many vital localities the Germans have slipped from them, and are being, squeezed slowly but surely in the direction of the Rhine. On the other side of Germany the tremendous effort that HINDENBURG has made to throw off the Russian attack has come to nought before the cool and resolute front presented by our brave and invincible Allies of the East. The roads hereabout have been so bad that the Germans have had to hide their bedraggled appearance behind a show of bunting in their capital, which was set rejoicing over a lie that Lodz had fallen, after the Russians had suffered tremendous losses. We learn officially from Petrograd that the Russians retired from Lodz for strategical reasons, without a single man being lost ; in fact, fifteen hours before the cowed Germans, who had lost 10,000 men in an assault upon the Russian position, had discovered that.. the Russians had left. So far as we have gathered, the Germans do not blame the weather for the British naval victory near the Falk- land Islands; off the coast of South America. The Admiralty, ADMIRAL STURDEE, who was selected to lead the attack upon the German squadron that recently sank the Good Hope and the Monmouth, and the officers and men who served under him, are entitled to the best thanks of the Empire for having so swiftly and so completely brought retribution upon the heads Of those who had ventured to dispute our ability to hold the seas against all- corners. ADMIRAL STURDEE was at his desk at the War Office in London on November Ist, when the disaster at Valparaiso happened. Inside six weeks he was away in the Southern Seas with a force strong enough to seek out the enemy arid send him wholesale to the bottom. The exploit, which is quite in 'keeping with the glorious annals of our Navy, has caus- ed unbounded enthusiasm throughout the Empire, has heartened our Allies, and has re-established our prestige for having an arm that can reach round the world—even when Germans try to bar the way. This has been a good week for us in many other ways. Perhaps the most important event of all has been the collapse of the ill-starred rebellion in South Africa, where the good sense of the people, capably led by GENERAL BOTHA, has prevailed, and, the way has been cleared for undivided attention to the German enemy. The Persian Gulf Expedition has defeated the Turks and gained complete possession of the delta formed by the confluence of the Tigris and Euphrates. Servia has inflicted a crushing defeat .upon Austria, who, a week ago, were cele- brating the fall of Belgrade. We are doing well, remarkably well, but we must keep for ever in mind that a suffi- cient number of officers and men must be found and maintained to ensure that the war shall be carried over- whelmingly into the enemy's camp. Only complete victory will serve, or, indeed, be safe for our future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>INSURED WARRIORS. Payments to Panel Doctors to be Reduced.</head>
        <p>INSURED WARRIORS. Payments to Panel Doctors to be Reduced.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday, the Mayor (Alderman p. Peacock) presiding, the following recommen- dation from the Medical Benefit Sub-committee was approved :—" That an insured person who has failed to pay a fine imposed on him within two weeks of the date on which it was so im- posed may be 'suspended from medical benefit for a period not exceeding one year." The Clerk, in his financial statement, esti- mated that £l7O us. 2d. would be available to carry forward to 1915. The minutes of the Finance Sub-committee, which were approved, contained the following item: "A lengthy dis- cussion took place as to what deduction, if any, should be made from payments to doc- tors, &amp;c., in respect of the 4th quarter of 1914, in view of the fact that a considerable number of insured persons have joined His Majesty's Forces, the exact number not being ascertain- able as yet. It was ultimately resolved: 'That a deduction of 10 per cent., equal to 2,500 men, be made from payments falling due on the Ist January.' In accordance with the resolution the Clerk was authorised to make the follow- ing payments as early as possible after let January next, viz., Doctors, £1,565; chemists (not to exceed), £447; approved institutions, £242."</p>
        <p>Licensed Victuallers' Delegate. The half-yearly meeting of the Licensed Victuallers' Defence League of England and Wales was held at Leeds on Tuesday and Wed- nesday. Alderman John Evans (who is also a member of the Council of No. 3 District) repre- sented the Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association, of which body he is president,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY, APPEAL FOR FUNDS. – Now then, Lads, Get a Hustle 0111E1,500 for Treatment of Wounded hoidias.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY, APPEAL FOR FUNDS. - Now then, Lads, Get a Hustle 0111E1,500 for Treatment of Wounded hoidias.</p>
        <p>Last week 586 men who have joined the South Lancashire Reserve Battalion were sent out. This draft will help to make the regiment up to something like its proper strength. A wounded officer, recently returned from Prance, computes that the South Lancashires have lost 46 officers and over 2,000 men, the figures, of course, in- cluding killed, wounded and missing. Many vacancies still remain to be filled, and it is hoped that all fit young men will bear the fact in mind.</p>
        <p>'The largest number recruited in one week in Warrington was 3,220, and it would gladden the learts of the recruiting staff at the Police Sta- tion, &amp;c., to be called upon to deal with a like numbet or one somewhat approaching it, next wpek. ,</p>
        <p>MR. BOTTOMLEY—UNOFFICIAL RECRUIT- INGBFFICER. Next Tuesday, Mr. Horatio Bottoniley, the editor of "John Bull," ex-M.P. for South Hack- ney, and a brilliant platform speaker, will ad- dress a public meeting, arranged in the interests of recruiting, in the Parr Hall. Already the demand for tickets has been phenomenal, and as it is quite obvious' that all who wish to hear Mr. Bottomley cannot be accommodated in the one building, arrangements have been made to hold an overflow meeting in. the Royal Court Theatre. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P.. will take the chair, and Mr. A. H. Crosfleld will speak.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—Since the war began the Board of Man- agement have placed at the disposal of the authorities a fully equipped ward for 24 wounded soldiers, and, in addition, through the gener- osity of Lady Greenall and Mr. Roger Charlton Parr, have been able to accommodate at Walton and Grappenhall 12 of the semi-convalescent cases which are sent in daily for treatment. The Government allowance is 2s. per day, and as our costs per bed average at present £1 Bs. led. per week, a very considerable deficiency is being incurred. Knowing full well that there have been $o many calls upon the generosity of the Warrington and district lately. I have hesitated to issue a public appeal, and contented mYself by writing personal letters or calling upon various ladies and gentlemen. As a result the following donations have been received or promd ise:— people of</p>
        <p>Greenall, Whitley &amp; Co., Ltd 3OO 0 Lady Greenall J. Charlton Parr, Esq., D.C., J.P 5O 0 u Trustees of the late Penketh Penny Bank Working Men's Infirmary Com- mittee H. H. Armitage, £lO per bed, and .28 Ba. dd. extra cost of quar- ter's maintenance Harold Smith, Esq., M.P. John J. Whitley, Esq. Old English Fair Committee lO 0 Warrington Licensed Victualler? Association Warrington and District Teachers' Association L. Henshall, Esq. The Staff at the Labour Exchange Divisional Office, Ist monthly payment for extra maintenance of two beds Miss Bolton, Penketh James Henry Smethurst, Esq 5 0 Warrington Photographic Society 211 Henry Collin, Esq. 'Causeway Lawn Tennis Club 1 10 The amount required for 12 months for maintenance and equipment is at least .21,5°7, and the serious consideration is being given r to the desirability of utilising the present einPir ward of the new pavilion, in order that 0° ordinary patients shall not be aeprived of neces' sary accommodation. At present, in addition to ordinary our private wards are being used for this P°' pose. feel The staff are working splendidly, and I sure that your readers will again assist local institution in this time of stress, an, their best to help us. Donations may be paid to Parr's Bank,lo- to tbe "Infirmary Special War Fund," or sent undersigned.</p>
        <p>Yours truly, JAMES HENRY SMETHURST, Chairman of the Board of Management. December 11, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>COMRADES' BATTALIONS OF "THE KING'S."</head>
        <p>COMRADES' BATTALIONS OF "THE KING'S."</p>
        <p>The success which has attended the formation of the Liverpool " Comrades' " Battalions of the King's Liverpool Regiment and the prestige which those battalions have gained, have en- couraged Lord Derby to suggest to the War Office the possibility of increasing their strength. Much to his lordship's satisfaction. Lord Kitchener has entirely fallen in with the pro- posal, and has formally given his assent to the scheme. It is now arranged that recruiting shall be immediately commenced with a view of bringing up each battalion to a strength of 1,350 men. The offices at Cooper's Buildings, Church-street, Liverpool, will accordingly be opened at 9 o'clock on Monday morning, when applications from men qualified to join the battalions in question will be received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LROTHERS CROSFIELD.</head>
        <p>THE LROTHERS CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Miss Truda Crosfield. MAJOR GEORGE CROSFIELD describing the movements of the Warrington Territorials. in Scotland to his brother, Mr. A 11. Crosfield, who is following every phase of the war with Intense interest.</p>
        <p>Photograph by</p>
        <p>, &gt; , - , , . • • ,„ -7, • • ;, • • • ,„: •4; , , • , , 4,, -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>• LOCAL OFFICER LOST. Captain E. Robson hies of Wounds.</p>
        <p>Captain Edgar Robson, who has been officially reported as having died of wounds, was well known in Warrington. .Before taking up a com- mission he was for several years engaged in the chemical laboratory at Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons' Works, and resided in Manchester-road. He came to Warrington from Newcastle-on- Tyne. Captain Robson was only in his 37th year, and, prior to entering the Regular Army, he was associated with the Volunteer movement. He went to South Africa during the Boer War as a junior subaltern of the South Lancashire Volun- teer Service Company, which was under the command of the late Major F. M. Appleton, whose death has also to be chronicled this week. Captain Robson, then Lieutenant Robson, wrote a most interesting account of some of his ex- periences during the Boer War, and under the title of "A Day's Trek," it is embodied in the book on the work of the Service Company com- piled by Major Appleton.</p>
        <p>Good Service in South Africa. It was whilst in South Africa that Captain Robson received a permanent commission in the let South Lancashire Regiment, with whom he saw active service in the Transvaal, and on the Zululand frontier of Natal, receiving the Queen's medal with four clasps and the King's medal with two clasps. After peace was declared Captain Robson served with his regiment in India. In 1905 he came home to Orford Barracks, but later re- joined his battalion in the East. He was pro- moted to a captaincy in 1909, and last year took duty with the 3rd Battalion (the old 4th Royal Lancashire Militia), returning to the line for the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>KILLED IN ACTION, Warrington Privale's Last Letter Home</head>
        <p>KILLED IN ACTION, Warrington Privale's Last Letter Home</p>
        <p>News was received on Wednesday morning by Mr. C. Johnson, of 176, Liverpool-road, War- rington, that his brother Private Walter John- son, of the Ist Scots Guards, was killed in action on October 26th.</p>
        <p>Private Johnson was only 26 years of age, and, when on furlough, he made his home with his brother. Just prior to the outbreak of the war he was a member of the Liverpool Police Force, which he joined in February last, on complet- ing seven years' service in the Army. He was called back to the colours with the First Re- serve. His army discharge certified him of ex- emplary character, and he was an excellent shot, gaining the marksmanship badge after only a few months' service, and attaining to such proficiency with the rifle that at one time he was reserve man for the regimental team entered for the "Daily Telegraph" Cup at Bisley. The deceased spent two years with his battalion in Egypt, where he actively identified himself with Christian work among the soldiers, and upon his return to this country he interested himself in temperance and anti-gambling work amongst his comrades. Last Letter to His Brother. A_ mournful memento of his dead brother which M. C. Johnson cherishes is a letter, dated from the British lines on October 10th, in which the gallant private expresses his gratitude for a present of tobacco, which, with other things, had been forwarded him. "My word!" he wrote, "I am enjoying this smoke! I've just 'bought' a pipe from a fellow; I've got to give him my biscuits for three days in return for it. My old pipe has a big hole in the bottom of it; and I've had to stick stamps over the hole when I wanted a smoke.</p>
        <p>• Meals to German Music. "We are living pretty well at present," the letter continues. "We are stationed behind a</p>
        <p>hill during the day, so we are able to light fires and fry bacon and make tea for breakfast. The Germans provide the music by sending a few shells over the hill, and when the tune starts we have to make a dive for our holes until they have finished. For tea, we have biscuits, cheese and jam, and just now we are getting a fresh meat dinner in the 'trenches at about 7 o'clock every night. " Tommy " Doesn't Like the Exaggerator. "I see from the papers that several of our fellows are home and that some of the letters they put in the papers are big eKaggerations. . . . A letter written by a chap of this com- pany to a Scotch paper says that there were only a hundred men left in the Battalion, and that these were starving as they were in the it being impossible to get food to , The chaps here don't like that sort of tthreennachcsT, thing, as it is bound to make the people at home anxious. Hot Receptions for Night Attacker. "The Germans opposite our part of the trench have been pretty quiet for a.few days. They made an attack on our right last night, but they were beaten back in about half an hour. They very, often make an attack at night, but we are always ready for them, and invariably give ' them a very hot reception. During the day most of the work is done by the artillery, firing at long range. Then it is that we get the chance of a sleep." In a postscript the decktsed soldier " I see your letter only took six days to comewrote 0: ut here."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Successful "Pound Day" at Raddon</p>
        <p>Court.</p>
        <p>Thursday was " Pound Day" at Raddon Court Hospital, and a very successful affair it proved to be, the gifts brought by the large numbers of visitors being many and varied, special thought naturally having been given to mincemeat and plum puddings, in view of the -approaching fes- tive season. Five of the soldiers in a con- valescent state were sent to Manchest.Fer yester- day, and there are now 28 patients in the hos- pital. These can be visited on Mondays and Saturdays, from 2 o'clock to 3 o'clock, and on Thursdays from 6 o'clock to 7 o'clock. CONCERTS AT THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>At the Infirmary there are 34 wounded soldiers receiving treatment. There have been no new arrivals during the week, and no soldiers have been discharged.. Concerts have been given almost every night, and these have been enjoyed and appreciated by the soldier-patients.</p>
        <p>THANKS!</p>
        <p>The Mayoress has received a letter from Lieut.-Colonel Nathan Raw, on behalf of the West Lancashire Territorial Nursing Service Committee, expressing deep gratitude to her and all in Warrington who are contributing to- wards the Western General hospital at Fazaker- ley. ,1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SANKEY WAR RELIEF COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>SANKEY WAR RELIEF COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>ittee</p>
        <p>The War Relief Commi, at Sankey are working very strenuously, and last night a further handsome amount in aid of the War Fund was raised by means of a hotpot supper, concert and "smoker," held in the Schools, for which about 100 tickets had been sold. The in- habitants had responded to appeals in a very generous fashion, and hotpots were received from the following ladies, who also assisted at the tables: Mesdames W. Furness (Fern Lea), Furness (Highfield House), Long, T. Kilshaw, Cook, A. Crosby, Knowles; Taylor, J. Ray, Daniels, Shaw, Webb, Littler, Clarke, and Miss Jones. The floral decorations had been lent by Mr. Upton, and the effect Was very pleasing. Mr. Furness presided at the concert, and many -entertaining items were given by the follow- ing: Mr. H. Ashley (Widnes), Mr. Walter Moores, Mr. W. Ireland, and Mr. Woodward's quartet of boy artists. A quartet of clever in- strumentalists from the Penketh Tannery Band also delighted the audience. The rtufles of accompanists were carried out efficiently by Mr. B. Smith and Mr. Middltharqt. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL OFFICERS PROMOTED. " Town Clerk's Son "Gets Rio Company."</head>
        <p>LOCAL OFFICERS PROMOTED. " Town Clerk's Son "Gets Rio Company."</p>
        <p>Recent lists of Territorial appointments,Vism, lished in the official "London Gazette ° tamed names that are well known in VirerrTije ton, and intimations, in connection with,,ed. bearers of those names, that will be red° with widespread pleasure and satisfaction. Lieutenant 0. 0. Lyon Whittle, of the 4th ti talion of the Prince of Wales' Volunteers (S,„o",:i Lancashire Regiment), the only son of the'l,c)" s Clerk of Warrington, has been promoted I°fa",4l captaincy, and a similar honour has be:„ his companion-in-arms, Lieutenant W. a son of Mr. W. A. McClure, formerly nrsgter of the Warrington branch of the Mane° and Liverpool District Bank. of ld-Li'ntr It E. L. Frost, a nephew</p>
        <p>econL _deutenan.. .s.en Mr. F. A. Frost, of Grappenhall, has been gl: a full lieutenancy-, as also have Second-Ll. ant B. T. Dickson (brother of Naval Cade„,t s. Angus Dickson, who lost his life when Hawke went down), a son of Mrs. John uv.';', field's brother, Dr. Dickson, of Marlow, 1311"-iio and Second-Lieutenant C. Collingwood, comes of a family well known in EarlesToWl?' Second-Lieutenant James Bainbelager.,°4.l„ste son of Mr. W. Bainbridge, of the Walton Office, and and who is attached to the sth tonal) Battalion of the King's Own Royal IJ'Lo, caster Regiment, has been raised to the terl3l4 rary rank of captain, and is acting as win, at Blackpool, where his company is statione</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PREPARATORY TRAINING COOS. Fairfield and Howley CompanY•</head>
        <p>PREPARATORY TRAINING COOS. Fairfield and Howley CompanY•</p>
        <p>'ortir</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, at the London and Western Railway Company's rifle y best five shots were made b A. Bailie Williams with level scores, and T. H. sertt-fir; W. O'Neill, J. Langhorn, H. Gaunt, F. and C. Dagnall following in the order 0 f t11.!3, No. 2 Section proved the best lot, and 0 Newton and O'Neill 1, Langhorn and Pet2l,,' 2, did splendidly as files. Bailie and 111E0.7ii. were the only file of No. 3 Section to (I°l4o Gaunt and Dagnall, of No. 1 Section, ills:;tbe. ' Williams and Cawley, of No. 1 Section, for fourth position as files. ccorrs, The company drills at Hardy-street ;Wed..: (Norman-street entrance) on Nloml ly3 LT." nesdays at 8 p.m. deserve full attendance' To-day. Meet for Battalion Pantie ilt the Dill at 2.50 p.m.</p>
        <p>0' GREAT SANKEY COMPANY FOR'''EJtivi. On Monday night a welbattended le,r4cl presided over by Mr. W. Furness, NvaS e.` f c Sankey Sunday School, for the purpose °fog P sidering the desirability of inaugllnr,ii: local Preparatory Training Corps. position was eagerly taken up by the 10:1 present, and a committee promptly forril,. that drills can be carried out witlioe'volV, delay. The Rev. J. R. Jones (vicar) veil offered the use of the Schoolroom, at a 111:1314, 0.1 charge, for training purposes. Mr. 31eu,.„,'" stV Penketh, late captain in the Volunt,et", well qualified as an instructor is tenceeds,i recruits in hand on Monday and Weddevetee evenings. Saturday afternoons will be pro' to rifle practice at the Whitecross ralleThirar" the vided it is at liberty. Affiliation to rington district will follow in due course. •</p>
