        <p>Visited the Troops.</p>
        <p>On his return this week from a six weeks' mission to the troops stationed at Northamp- ton, the Rev. R. Parry Jones, pastor of the Welsh Church, Crosfield-street, was accorded a hearty reception by the members of the con- gregation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Warrington Shorthand Association.</head>
        <p>Warrington Shorthand Association.</p>
        <p>The memi;ers of the above association met on Friday evening of last week for speed practice. The Secretary is to be congratulated on the ex- cellent programme which has been arranged for the session. Demonstration lessons, lecture's and high speed competitions, in addition to the usual speed practices, are included.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Address at Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Address at Parish Church.</p>
        <p>At the WERrington Parish Church on Wed- nesday eveuing, the Rev. Edwin H. Day (organ- ising secretary of the Society for the Propaga- tion of the Gospel in the Diocese of Chester. Liverpool, and St. Asaph), delivered a second of a series of missionary addresseo. The sub- ject was the work of Missions in Asia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Market Prices.</p>
        <p>Wheat prices ruled firmer at Wednesday's market, with a slight advance on last week's figures, viz., 6s. to 6s. 3d. per 701 b. Oats were slightly easier, the demand having fallen off lightly., Prices ranged from Bs. 6d. to 3s. 9d. per 451 b. There was no change in the price of *raw, which ran at 21s. to 225. per ton f.o.r. Clover hay fetched ..t3 ss. per ton; meadow hay, £2 15s. f.0.r.; XX flour, 355. 6d. per 2801 b.; and bran, is. 4d. per 20Th. Flour was firm and prices are likely to advance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Trades Council's "Profit " on Muni-</head>
        <p>Trades Council's "Profit " on Muni-</p>
        <p>eipal Contests. At the monthly meeting of the Warrington _</p>
        <p>Trades and Lab Our Council, held on Tue;day evening, the Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) re- ported that the total sum received on account of the fund opened to pay for the three unsuc- cessful contests at the recent municipal elec- tions was £24 15s. The expenses totalled ..t24 Os. 9d., so that, as Mr. Dukes put it, there was a profit of 14s. 3d., whereupon a delegate ob- served: "I think it is a very satisfactory result; much more satisfactory than the polling results, lam sorry to say." (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Queen Alexandra's Letter. Mr. Arthur J. Thorp, HO, Manchester-road, Warrington, whose father; the Rey. W. Thorp, was given the Rectory of Byrne Intrinsea, Dorset, by the late King Edward, has in recent years sent a congratulatory message to Queen Alexandra on her birthday. In answer to his most recent one, on the occasion of Her Majesty's ..70th birthday, on December let, he _</p>
        <p>, received the following "Marlborough House.—The Hon. Charlotte Knollys is commanded by Queen Alexander to thank Mr. Arthur J. Thorp very much for his good wishes and kind congratulations. December Sth, 1914."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Beautiful Caue Bird ShOW.</head>
        <p>Beautiful Caue Bird ShOW.</p>
        <p>Lovers and fanciers of cage birds will find much to interest them in the excellent collec- tion of feathered exhibits assembled at the nineteenth Annual show in connection with the Warrington Cage Bird Society, which is being held in the Cairo-street Schools. There was a large attendance throughout yesterday, but it is anticipated that even more will at- tend to-day. The 500 entries are divided into 47 classes, and four prizes are given for the premier birds in each class. The exhibition of British birds is far better than last year; there are some beautiful Norwich and York- shire specimens, and a feature is an extra- ordinarily good class of dark mules. In the hybrid class " White Bose," the canary bull- finch, was easily first. Linnets are not very conspicuous, but all the other classes, includ- ing chaffinches and, hard and soft billed birds, are very good. Another feature of the show is the exhibition of two white thrushes caught at Latchford. Scripture Examination Successes. In connection with the Primitive Methodist Church Sunday School Scripture examination, the following scholars of Legh-street Sunday School were successful :—Senior Division : Florence Bissell (first class with honours). Middle Division: Lillian llimsworth, Doris Bennett, and Edith Snape (first class with hon- ours), and Mona Archer (first class). Junior Division: Polly Siddall (first class honours), 3laurice Archer and Thomas Edwin Jackson (first class). Mr. A. R Hoyle, one of the teachers of the school, prepared the scholars. The subject for examination was "Jesus, the World's Saviour and King" (Mark ix. 30-41, x. 13-16, xi. 1-11; Matthew xxviii. 16-20; Luke xxiv. 36-49; Isaiah xi. 1-10). Photographic Society. , The weekly meeting afforded an opportunity for Messrs. Butcher and Son, of London, to send down their reading and illustrations referring to the work done by some of their latest cam- eras, especially those of the reflex type. These are the kind most useful to Pressmen, and al- though nearly always used in the hand the Specimens of work shown were very fine. Scenes at sports' meetings were the first and best ex- amples of what can be done with the ,reflex cameras, but the architectural subjects showed</p>
        <p>Have You a Piano to Lend? Mrs. liansome, Shirley, Hill Chile, Warring- ton, writes "May I use yOui colunan3 to appeal for the loan of a piano for the Women's Club-room in Buttermarket-street? This room is open each day from 3 to 9, and is much appreciated by those that come. It is felt that some music would be a great addition, and perhaps one of your readers would be able to help us by lend- ing a piano."</p>
        <p>Oratorio at Legh-street Church. Handers "Messiah" will be sung at the Legh-s'aeet Primitive Methodist Church to- morrow. Part I. will he given in the afternoon and Parts. 11. and 111. in the evening. The soloists will be Mrs. C. Worthington, Mrs. A. E. Hoyle and Miss Jessie Woodland (sopranos), Miss Maud Jones (contralto), Mr. W. J. Booth</p>
        <p>„ (tenor), and Mr: E. 11. Pearce (bass). Mr. F. H. Banner will preside at the organ, and the specially-augmented choir will be under the conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce. Rooks for Christmas.</p>
        <p>Percival -Pearse, Ltd., Sankey-street, have a remarkable stock of books specially suitable for seasonable gifts. They include : Princess Mary's Gift Book, 2s. 6d.; King Albert's Gift Book, 35.; a fine series of Black's colour books, including the one on Belgium; fine illustrated books, topo- graphy, biography and history, travel, sport. Nature, gardening and fiction. For boys and girls there are books in colour, science, story books, and new annual volumes, including Boys' and Girls' "Own Annual," Blackie's, Herbert Strang, Empire, Chatterbox and Rosebud. District Nursing Association. The seventeenth annual report of the War- rington District •Nursing Association—which will be submitted for approval at the annual meeting on Wednesday next—shows that dur- ing the past twelve months 16,226 visits were paid by the nurses to 732 cases. There was an increase of 52 in the number of patients. The Alexandra Day collection brought £7O 16s. 3d. to the funds, which totalled £B2B 16s. lid., £294 2s. of which came from general subscrip- tions and fees, and £297 Bs. 10d. from the Working Men's Committee. The expenses totalled .8729 19s. 3d., leaving a balance of £9B 17s. Bd.</p>
        <p>Over 700 111-treated Children. The annual report of the Warrington and District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children—which, "on account of the war," this year takes the form of a simple fly-leaf—records that, during the year ended March 31st last, 246 complaints were investigated. All save two of the com- plaints were found to be true, and in 233 cases warnings were administered, whilst in the re- maining eleven successful prosecutions were undertaken. The cases dealt with involved 720 children-352 boys and 368 girls (of whom twp died)—and there were 319 offenders (197 males and 122 females). In addition to first visits of inquiry and inspection 1,468 supervision visits were made by the society's inspector, in addi- tion to 21 inquiries made on behalf of other branches. The balance sheet, printed with the report, shows receipts totalling £231 10s. sd. After meeting local expenses, £l9 6s. 6d. was remitted in aid of national work.</p>
        <p>Kinds of Eggs. "Black Eagles," " P.R.'s," and "Large Dirties" were the names given to three classes of eggs mentioned in a case at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, when the Contin- ental Egg Company, Hull, claimed £5O us. from James Sexton, 84, Hume-street, Warring- ton, in respect of eggs supplied. Sexton had a counter-claim of £27 for breach of contract, the eggs, he alleged, not being of the quality ordered.—Mr. E. A. White appeared for the Egg Company, and Mr. A. Browne for Mr. Sexton.—After a somewhat lengthy hearing a mutual agreement was arrived at and judgment for £35, with £7 costs, was given for the plain- tiffs on the claim, and the counter-claim, was withdrawn.</p>
        <p>, wonderful adaptation to what is al-ways a diffi- cult photographic subject. When the pictorial and the flower studies were shown the limita- tions of the hand camera were made manifest. A very profitable evening was spent, and more than one member present was impressed with the suitability of the reflex for many depart- Inents of camera craft. For Benevolent Purposes. _ With the object of raising funds for benevo-</p>
        <p>lent purposes, a social, promoted by the Bewsey Women's Adult School, was held on Monday evening, when Mrs. John Elwell presided over a large attendance, which included the wives of several soldiers serving at the front. Mrs. Elwell gave a brief address, in which she pointed out that the object of the social was to do some- thing towards helping the distressed and, suffer- ing. Good work in that direction had been done by the school in the past, and she hoped that what had been done would be done again. The evening was devoted to music and games, and during an interval refreshments were served. The proceedings concluded with a vote of thanks to the lady-president, to the artistes, and to Miss Hilda Parkes, the pianist, and the sing- ing of the National Anthem. Much praise for the success of the social is due to Mesdames Parkes, Palmer, Howell, Sproston, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>'Warrington's Leather Trade. Writing in the current issue of "The Leather Trades Review," the Warriogton correspondent Says:—" The condition of the leather market leaves tanners little room for complaint, as the demand continues insistent. While it is pro- bable that the amount of fresh business offering during the week has somewhat diminished, this Is only usual at this particular season, and, With holidays probably much curtailed this Year, there should he a revival of strong buying I early in the New Year. In the meantime the Output of contract work is very heavy, and the decreased production brought about by the re- cent bad weather has provoked energetic pro- tests on all sides about slow deliveries. Prices ,I;tre fairly stationary at the moment, but quite nrm. Rides of home production are a trifle easier, except light-weights; salted are firmer, Mil some quotations are startling, although it aY be permissible to doubt if SOMA of the 4110.1 res named are actually obtained."</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</p>
        <p>and who is free from these?—hut it has also kindled in us a noble freemasonry and a deep affection for one another that can never die—an affection which I note lights up afresh and flashes from eye to eye whenever any of us happen now at any time to meet. " That every member of my company, and all those dear to them, may long live in health and wealth, that all of them may always remain the good soldiers and honourable men they have generally proved themselves to be, and may always be loyal to their King and country, still jealous of their own honour and of that of their company and regiment, and, above all, true to their God,• is the dearest wish of one who will always venture to call himself, what for eighteen months he has had the honour of being by right—their comrade and their cap- tain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Entertaining Soldiers' and Sailors' IVives.</head>
        <p>Entertaining Soldiers' and Sailors' IVives.</p>
        <p>A large number of soldiers' and sailors' wives were the other evening the guests of the local branch of the British Women's Temperance Association at a social held in the Working Men's Mission Hall, Mrs: Owen presiding and extending a hearty welcome to those present. Mrs. Dodds, gave a brief, cheery and sympa- thetic address, and songs, &amp;c., were rendered by the following: Mrs. Barrett, Mrs. Lewis, Miss Lily Rigby, Miss Eva Tanner and Mr. J. Bar- rett, Mr. T. Tanner accompanying. During the evening tea and biscuits were handed round, and, altogether, a most enjoyable time was spent. We understand that the Association purpose holding a series of similar entertain- ments during the winter months. "‘ Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>The following pupils of Miss Maud Jennings, 142, The Causeway (diploma of the V.C.M. and prize-winner L.C.M.), passed very successfully at the recent examination held in connection with the Victoria College of Music, London. The successes obtained include :—Piano. Diploma of Associate, Lily Taylor, I+, Buy lie- street (which entitles her to append the letters A.V.C.M. after her name, also to wear the Col- lege cap, gown and hood). Theory, certificates of bronze medallists: May Mather, Church- street, and Hilda Baker, Penketh (each having gained the maximum number of marks, 100). Piano, advanced senior grade: Ellen Miller, Nora-street (95 marks, first class with honours). Senior grade: Alice Futnival, Bewsey-street kB4 marks, first class with honours); Evelyn Fraser, Gaskell-street, Stockton Heath (78 marks. first class). Intermediate grade: Mary Hart, Eustace-street (93 marks, first class with honours). Junior grade: Ruth Loxley, pen_ keth (82 marks, first class with honours); Arthur Harrison, London-road, Stockton Heath (81 marks, first class with honours). Primary grade: Denis Duffy, Froghail-lane (95 marks, first class with honours); Hilda Mary Dyke, The Causeway (86 marks, first class with honours). Preparatory grade: Thomas L. Hatch, Thynne-street (87 marks, first class with honours). Shops and the Christmas Holidays. The Secretary of State has issued a circular letter pointing out to shopkeepers certain ar- rangements which will, in his opinion; he per- missible under the Shops Act, 1912, this year, When Christmas Day and Boxing Day, both of which are Bank Holidays Within the meaning of the Act, fall on a Friday and Saturday respec- tively. The circular states: It is provided in Section 4 (5) of the Act that if a shop is closed throughout the whole of a bank holiday, and the bank holiday does not fall on the day fixed for the weekly half-holiday, the shopkeeper may keep open either on the half-holiday ilumedi- ately preceding or on the half-holiday imme- diately succeeding the bank holiday. If the shopkeeper close's the whole of Christmas Day or Boxing Day, he Will be entitled to keep open either on the half-holiday in Christmas week or on the half-holiday in the week following. If he closes the whole of both days he can keep open on the half-holidays in both weeks. A shopkeeper who closes the whole of New Year's Day will be entitled, if his half-holiday in New Year's week is fixed for Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday, to keep open either on the half-holiday in that week or on the half- holiday in the week following; or, if his half- holiday is fixed for Saturday, to keep open on Saturday, January 2nd." Death of a Devout Wesleyan. We record with regret the death of Mr. Walter Thomas Gregory, which took place on Wednesday morning at his residence, 7, Wilson- Patten-street, Warrington, Mr. Gregory, whose age was 73 years, was born at Boulogne-sur-Mer, where he lived for the greater part of his life, carrying on business in the Grande Rue. He was also instrumental in the erection of a Wes- leyan Chapel—belonging, as he did, to a de- voted Wesleyan family. A few years ago he settled in Warrington, but being of a retiring disposition he took no active part in political or social matters. For several months past he had been in failing health, and the end came quite peacefully.—The funeral service was conducted yesterday afternoon at Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel by the Rev. Hugh Williamson and the Rev. H. A. Inglis .(of Morley), and there were also present: Mrs. Gregory (widow), Miss Gregory and Miss Olive Gregory (daughters), Mr. Edward Gregory (of London, brother), Mr. and Mrs. F. Pendlebury, Mr. G. H. Toft, and Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Toft. Beautiful floral tri- butes were received from: The Widow and family, Mr. Edward Gregory, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Toft, the Misses Toft, and Mr. and Mrs. F. Pendlebury. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons, Church-street, carried out the funeral arrange- ments.</p>
        <p>vices of a man whose principal object in life had been to bring the Volunteers to the highest possible state of perfection, and subsequently to put Lore Haldane's new model, the Terri- torials, upon a solid foundation. The circumstances of his tragic end were nar- rated on Tuesday before the Tunbridge Wells Coroner, a full report of which we give below.</p>
