        <p>Photograph by Birtles.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MAJOR APPLETON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>UNDER FIRE IN NATAL.</head>
        <p>UNDER FIRE IN NATAL.</p>
        <p>Captain Appleton's diary details the doings of the company from the departure to the re- ',urn. On March 29th, 1900, he writes: "Arrived Ladysmith 7 a.m. Joined regiment in time for breakfast. . . . An eventful day in the his- tory of our Volunteers, for the first time being on active service with their Ist Battalion."</p>
        <p>Lad:</p>
        <p>The doings of the company are detailed at length, and one reads of the many long treks in which they engaged before, and the various duties they performed in maintaining the lines of communication, guarding prisoners, &amp;T. It was not until June Bth that the company had its baptism of fire. In his diary Captain Appleton describes the engagement as follows: "Reveille, 6 a.m. "Brigade ordered to attack Botha's Pass, after South African Light Horse had occupied Spitz Kop (without opposition). Pass here runs between two high ranges of Drakensberg. Valley between, along one side of which the road ran; valley about three miles wide. We followed road in column of fours until opposite our por- tion of the opposite range (Boer position), which our brigade was ordered to attack. All this _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>time a tremendous bombardment from 4.7 naval 12-pounders and field batteries was going on all along our range an over our heads, which raked Boer position until we were almost on the crest line, with no reply from Boers. Our battalion was on the right, Royal Lancaster centre, and York and Lancasters left. Our firing line was in two lines, and first extended to ten paces, and we came as third line, or reserves, four companies, under Major Adam, and extended to one pace. We had a very stiff climb, and when just below the crest Major Adam closed up to about 100 yards behind firing linek Just on crest were a Boer trench and gun epaillment, both happily unoccupied, or some of us would have caught it hot:- just as the tring line were well on top, a tremendous musketry, shell and pompom fire opened upon us, and the bullets whistled happily well over our heads. We ad- vanced, and just after our line had got 'right incline' from Major Adam, a pom-pom burst about ten yards in front of our company, which, if it had been advancing straight to the front, would have just caught it. Our Maxim, under Lieut. Trousdale, was now in action, and after a few more minutes' firing the Boers retreated, and fired the g• ass to cover their retreat, accord- ing to their usual custom. The attack was suc- cessful all along the line, and the Pass was ours, the Boers failing to make anything like the stand they had done earlier in.the.war. _</p>
        <p>"We lost one man killed' in. the Battalion</p>
        <p>(Private Duffy). We remained in position till dusk, and after throwing ont outposts, bivou- acked on the same ground. A thick cold fog came on, and as it was 1 a.m. before our wag- gons reached us with coats, blankets and rum, we had a miserable time. We had our baptism of fire without firing h shot, and happily with- out any casualties. We were not a little proud of having received it under the command of our tld adjutant."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PROUD TO SERVE THEIR QUEEN.</head>
        <p>PROUD TO SERVE THEIR QUEEN.</p>
        <p>On June 29th, the following is the entry in the diary: "Company washing (one mile and a half away) in the morning, under Lieut. Lewis. Digging in the afternoon. Tents arrived and were pitched 5 p.m'. First time we had slept in one for weeks. Bread and fresh meat rations. &amp;c.. now valued as luxuries." Under the heading "The Trek Ox," Captain Appleton tells of the work done by that animal, to which Tommy Atkins was introduced in South Africa, and to which, on closer acquaint- ance, he learns that he owes much. • In an article entitled " A Camp Concert," Captain Appleton writes: "hero, on a South African kopje, some 7,000 miles from Old Eng- land, are seated a body of men, all of whom, of their own free will, have come out, proud to serve their Queen and country, and to take their small share in upholding the Empire. Their bronzed, sun and weather tanned faces, lit up by the fire glow, seem to form a picture which could only have been painted by one master—Freedom, the God-inspired freedom of 'our own dear homeland and those greater home- lands across the sea. One song introduces 'Home, sweet home.' Mark the softening of those war-hardened faces! Again, quite unex- pectedly, comes into the song ' Auld lang syne.' Involuntarily all spring to their feet and, with crossed hands, the circle is linked and the chorus twice repeated, and no old acquaintance is here forgot." Referring to the death of the Queen, Captain Appleton says: "We heard of the death of the Queen oft January 24th, at Tin Town, Lady- smith; the feeling was that everyone had lost a second mother, and it was hare to realise we were-no longer wearing the Queen's uniform." In a reference to Spion Kop, Captain Apple- ton states:— _ _</p>
        <p>"No Englishman can think of Spion Kop without a feeling of intense sadness, and none can visit that sacred ground and gaze upon those long graves (formerly trenches) with dry eyes. Someone again had blundered, and at least largely increased England's long roll of honour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PROMOTIONS, DECORATIONS AND</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS, DECORATIONS AND</p>
        <p>MEDALS. For his services in South Attica, Captain Appleton was made an lion, captain in the Regular Army. He received tle Queen's medal with five clasps. In 1903 he was promoted hon. major in the Volunteers. In 1908 he had the Volunteer Decoration conferred upon him, and in 1009, having passed the field officer's examina- tion in the previous year, he was promoted to the rank of major and qualified for command at an examination at Caerwys Camp. On the celebration of the late Queen Vic- toria's diamond jubilee in 1897, Captain Apple- ton was selected to command a detachment of twenty men stationed to guard Westmingter Bridge, for which he received the Jubilee medal. When he WRS promoted major, he still pre- ferred to be known as Captain Appleton, his rank in the Regular Army taking precedence of that of the Auxiliary Forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Major Appleton was a devout Churchman, and nothing better exemplified his character gener- ally than his interest in the Church Lads' Bri- gade. At the time of his death he was a colonel in that religious military organisation. When he resided at Bruche Hall he atten,ded Padgate Church, where he was a warden, and in connection with which he established a com- pany of the Church Lads' Brigade in 1893. A gentleman who takes a keen interest in the Church Lads' Brigade writes : " He was appointed 2nd March, 1910, lieuten- ant-colonel of the 2nd Liverpool Cadet Battalion C.L.8., when the Battalion was re-formed (2nd March, 1910) under the Cadet Regulations. "His example of comradeship and zeal in- spired the whole movement in Warrington. Having "a mind conscious of rectitude to itself," he was ever jealous of the honour of the Brigade, and endeavoured to accomplish its object to make every member a Christian gen- tleman, always cheerfully willing to advise and help everyone who wished for guidance in the duties of his office and rank. "He organised and provided a mess club and, reading room for the warrant officers of the Battalion at his business premises in Rose and Crown-street, where by his frequent attendance he encouraged the officers and members to do their utmost to carry out every detail 'of their duties to the very best of their power, exempli- fying that "comradeship is one of the finest facts and one of the strongest forces in life."</p>
        <p>Being a governor of the Blue Coat School would afford him congenial occupation. In later years Major Appleton attended Grappen- hall Church and the Warrington Parish Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AN ENGLISH GENTLEMAN.</head>
        <p>AN ENGLISH GENTLEMAN.</p>
        <p>Beloved By His Men. BY A N.C.O. 4th SOUTH LANCASHIRES. If a German airship had suddenly appeared over Tunbridge Wells on Sunday morning it would not have caused the sensation that the sad news of our beloved Major's tragic end did. With blanched faces, all, officers and men.</p>
        <p>turned to one another, unable to speak of the unwelcome news. We could hardly realise the fact that we had lost one of the most respected officers that ever took command over a party of men.</p>
        <p>A gentleman to the core, Major Appleton had won the hearts of all who came in contact with him, and his loss will cause a gap in the Bat- talion which will be very difficult to fill. He was always ready and willing to help with kindly counsel anyone who asked, and recruits were not slow to take advantage of his solici- tous kindness. He would always do his utmost to help them in any way, and his smiling coun- tenance soon put them at their ease. I have served under him for the past 23 years, my first experience of him being as a lad in the Church Lads' Brigade, and I have never known him to use his- authority in a manner calculated to tarnish the name of a British officer. He was a splendid example of a "fine old English gentleman." His never-failing courtesy made him beloved of all ranks from the Colonel down to the youngest recruit. I shall never forget his last words to me. One night last week I was in charge of one of the guards, and it was a very cold night. Just before midnight our late Major (who was field officer for the day) came round on his tour of inspection. After inspecting the guard, he rode off with the words, "Good night, boys. Keep yourselves as warm as possible." Boys! Yes, we were all his boys, and we loved him to a man. This was only one example of his kindly thought for the men who had the privilege of serving under him.</p>
        <p>A deep impression was made by the Rector of Rusthall, who made a brief reference to the sad news at Sunday's church parade. Looking round the rows of khaki-clad men, one could see tears in the eyes of men whom one would think in- capable of showing feeling; tears of which they were not ashamed. Yes, we shall miss his cheery smile, and the 4th South Lancashire Regiment will be all the poorer by the tragic end of Major F. M. Appleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Tragic End of a Populae and Enthusiastic Volunteer.</head>
        <p>Tragic End of a Populae and Enthusiastic Volunteer.</p>
        <p>STORY OF AN AUTOMATIC PISTOL.</p>
        <p>Jury Return a Verdict of " Accidental Death."</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Wednesday after- noon at Rusthall Church. Practically the whole Battalion turned out to pay their last tribute of respect to the deceased Major. Lieutenant- Colonel B. Pairclough was in command. The service at the graveside was conducted by the Rector of Rusthall, who was a personal friend of the late Major, and the Rev. W. Bracecamp spoke a few words. A firing party, 200 strong, under the command of Captain C. P. Cary, fired three volleys over the grave, and the buglers of the Battalion sounded the "Last Post."</p>
        <p>The Old English Fair Committee were repre- sented by Mr. R. Dainteth and Mr. W. Ashton, and ex-Sergeants J. Howard and W. Ashton represejated the late Major's Active Service Company which served in the South, African War. A wreath from that company was in- scribed :</p>
        <p>"His life was gentle and the elements so mixed in him that Nature might stand up and say to all the world:</p>
        <p>' This Was a Man.'"</p>
        <p>A large number of floral tributes of every con- ceivable design were placed on the coffin.</p>
        <p>The streets were lined with a numerous con- course of people of the town and district, where Major Appleton had made himself very popular, and the blinds were drawn at all the houses en route.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SAD NEWS AT GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>THE SAD NEWS AT GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>The receipt of the news of the death of Major Appleton was wired on Sunday to the Rector, whose painful duty it was to break the sad in- telligence to his sister, Miss Appleton. Quite a gloom was cast over the parish, as the deceased gentleman was very popular amongst the in- habitants. He was a member of the Parish Council, having been elected at the last triennial election. As a tribute to his memory the bells were muffled at the Parish Church, and after the evening service the organist played the Dead March in " Saul" very impressively, the congregation remaining standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>STORY OF AN ACTIVE AND USEFUL CAREER. Fought in South Africa; Ready to Fight the Germans.</head>
        <p>STORY OF AN ACTIVE AND USEFUL CAREER. Fought in South Africa; Ready to Fight the Germans.</p>
        <p>Major Francis Martin Appleton, 4ho was 50 .ears of age at the time of his death, was one of Warrington's best known and most highly respected citizens. Formerly he lived at Bruche Hall, Manchester-road, with his sister, but a few years ago they removed to Birchfield, Grap- penhall. Tre was the youngest and only surviv- ing son of the late Rev. Richard Appleton, a Lincolnshire vicar, and of Mrs. Appleton, daugh- ter of the late Mr. Joseph Wagstaffe, of the firm of Fitchett and Wagstaffe. solicitors, Winwick- street. He was born at Kirkdale, Liverpool. Having been educated at Louth Grammar School and Haileybury College, he entered the offices of the file works, Scotland-road, Warring- ton, now owned by Peter Stubs, Ltd., but then run by Messrs. Stubs, Jas. Marson and Henry Appleton (Major Appleton's late brother). He had two brothers clergymen, and a second sister is the wife of Major Buckton, late of Bruche Hall, and formerly Colonel of the local Volunteer Battalion. .</p>
        <p>Subsequent to his return from the Boer war Major Appleton began business on his own account in Rose and Crown-street.</p>
        <p>HIS VOLUNTEER RECORD. Major Appleton always took a great interest in the Volunteer movement, and it is not de- tracting from the accomplishments of the many gentlemen who have by their practical interest in national defence made the name of War- ribgton well-known in military circles, to say that no one ever worked more arduously or suc- cessfully for the cause. He joined the old Vol- unteers and was appointed second-lieutenant, December 21, 1887, being posted to A Company, of which Major Buckton was captain. In 1889 he was promoted to lieutenant, and in 1893 (March sth) he was made captain. It was as Captain Appleton that he volun- teered in 1900 for active service in South Africa. When it was seen that the Army in South Africa would need reinforcement, the eyes of the military authorities naturally turned to- wards the Volunteers. Locally, Colonel .T. C. Ridgway, then in command of the local Batta- lion, recommended Captain Appleton for the command of the Volunteer Active Service Com- pany raised by Warrington, St. Helens and Widnes. Within an hour he had decided to allow his name to be sent in, and commenced his soldiering in real earnest straight away. On January 13th, 1900, the men took the necessary oath to the Queen before the Mayor (Alderman 11. Roberts). "The Volunteer Service Company (Ist South Lancashire Regiment) in South Africa During the Boer War" is the title of a most interest- ing book written by Captain Appleton on his return from the war, dealing with the part played by his company in that struggle. The company served under General Sir Redvers Bul- ler in Natal, the Transvaal and the Orange Free State, and the book was dedicated to that brave General. The departure of the active service company, which numbered three officers and 113 non-commissioned officers and men, is described in the book by " An Outsider," who writes : "Captain F. M. Appleton was not the only officer who offered his services to take charge of the company. Several were anxious to go to the front, but the choice of the authorities ultim- ately fell upon Captain Appleton, wlict, besides having been connected with the Ist Volunteer Battalion, South Lancashire Regiment, since . _ _ _</p>
        <p>-, -- 1887, possessed the fullest Zonfidence of the colonel, the adjutant, his brother officers and the men generally, and was looked upon as a most efficient officer." . _ .</p>
        <p>"An Out-</p>
        <p>Writing of the actual departure, sider " says : " On Friday, 23rd February in the early hours of the morning, the company left Warrington amid scenes of great enthusiasm, and later in the day embarked at Southampton, the Avon- dale Castle being the good ship that was to bear them on their fateful journey." Writing of the home coming, which was made in the steamship Custodian, "An Outsider" States: "It was Friday, 7th June, 1902, before _ _</p>
        <p>the Custodian reached England, and the South Lancashire Volunteers received their first wel- come by means of telegraphic messages from friends and relatives, who could not very well make the journey to Southampton. The fol- lov ing day, Saturday, was a day of great re- joicing in Warrington. . . . Nothing was wanting in the heartiness of the reception that was accorded the company when it did arrive, after hours of anxious waiting, owing to the late arrival of the train." PRESENTED WITH A SWORD.</p>
        <p>At a dinner given in Parr Hall, Captain Appleton, the hero of the hour, and Captain Crosfield (who had been out in command of the Manchester Company of Pmperial Yeomanry) were presented with swords. In making the presentation, the Mayor, addressing Captain Ai pleton, said : "On behalf of the people of Warrington I</p>
        <p>_ . have great pleasure in asking your acceptance of this presentation sword. I know you will receive it with great pleasure, and that it will remind you of the manner in which your towns- men appreciate your devotion to your country and your services to the King." Captain Appleton returned thanks for the manner in which the townspeople qf Warring- ton had treated the company.</p>
        <p>HAPPY RELATIONS WITH HIS MEN. Captain Appleton was at one with the whole of his company, and after stating in his book some of the chief characteristics of the company, he went on to says— t` Such close association has certainly made us find out one another's faults and weaknesses—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INQUEST. Interested in A Borrowed Pistol. The coroner's inquiry was held at Tunbridge Wells on Tuesday evening.</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST. Interested in A Borrowed Pistol. The coroner's inquiry was held at Tunbridge Wells on Tuesday evening.</p>
        <p>A BELOVED, KINDLY-HEARTED, CHRISTIAN GENTLEMAN. Lieutenant-Colonel Brereton Fairclough, 4th (Territorial) Battalion, South Lancashire Regi- ment, was the first witness. He said Major Appleton, who was 50 years of age, was a bachelor. They had served together for 20 years, during' which time the deceased had en- joyed good health. The Major was a quiet, kindly-hearted, unobtrusive, Christian gentle- man, without an enemy in the world. He took his work very seriously, and for some time past had been studying hard. When witness last saw Major Appleton alive, on Saturday evening, he seemed to be in good health, although he was, perhaps, not quite so bright las usual. On Sunday morning, about 6.45 wit- ness heard a shot and immediately afterwards the Major's servant ran into his room saying he was afraid something had happened to his master. Witness went to the lavatory, the door of which he found fastened. The door was forced open, and he then saw deceased lying on , his left side on the floor, with a pool of blood ' round his head. The Major was unconscious, and died shortly afterwards. Later, an auto- matic pistol was found on the floor. The weapon was not of the, ordinary service pattern. It was a new make, with which the deceased was not familiar. As far as he knew Major Apple- ton had no worry or trouble, and had not an enemy in the world. Major Appleton was be- loved by all the officers and men in the regiment, to whom the news of his death had come as a great shock. He was quite in his usual spirits when Witness saw him on Saturday night.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>'MAJOR'S SERVANT'S STORY.</head>
        <p>'MAJOR'S SERVANT'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Private Louis Edward Sutton, who had acted as Major Appleton's servant for fourteen years, said he saw the Major on Sunday morning about 6.40 a.m., dressed in a sleeping suit and about to enter the lavatory. Shortly afterwards there was the sound of a shot, and when witness found that the lavatory door was fastened he immedi- ately informed the doctor and the Colonel. The pistol, which was found in the lavatory, was a new pattern weapon, which Major Appleton had never fired.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Ridgway (who represented the officers of the Battalion): He had never fired this revolver before?—No. • As a matter of fact he borrowed itP—Yes. I borrowed it for him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>INJURIES.</head>
        <p>THE INJURIES.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Surgeon Herbert G. Davies stated he examined Major Appleton in the lavatory about 6.45 a.m. on Sunday. He was unconscious and suffered from a wound in the head, which showed that a bullet had passed from under his right ear to his left temple. Death was due to the bullet wound, which, from its position, might have been self-inflicted or accidentally- inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ARRANGED TO ATTEND CHURCH PARADE.</head>
        <p>ARRANGED TO ATTEND CHURCH PARADE.</p>
        <p>Captain Clarence Cary, of the same Battalion, spoke of a meeting he had had with the Major about 4.30 on the Saturday afternoon before the traged,y. The Major was then in unusually good spirits, and they laughed, joked and chatted for about ten minutes in the street. They ar- ranged to meet at a church parade at 9.30 on Sunday morning. Earlier Major Appleton had asked witness to explain the mechanism of a new pistol he had borrowed, which was very different from the mechanism of the ordinary service pattern revolver.</p>
        <p>CORONER'S VERSION AND SYMPATHY. The Coroner, in summing up, said there was not the slightest evidence to show that the de- ceased was at all worried by domestic or busi- ness troubles, or that he had any suicidal ten- dency. Neither had there been anything to suggest that he would take his own life in a pre- meditated way. Dealing with the possibility of accident, the Coroner said the deceased may have had some object in taking the pistol in the lavatory with him; he may have been, afraid to leave it about loaded. The suggestion that any- one else had anything to do with his death could be dismissed, since they had heard that the Major was beloved by all the members of the regiment.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and gave expression to their sympathy with the relatives and the officers and men of the regiment. Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough remarked that it was a great relief to have so many people coming forward to express their sympathy. It helped to alleviate the pained feelings of all concerned.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said at such a sad time words had very little effect. It would, however, be some satisfaction to the relatives and the mem- bers of the regiment to know that the evidence had been of such a nature as to justify a ver- dict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE At the Parish Church To-morrow</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE At the Parish Church To-morrow</p>
        <p>Morning. There will be a memorial service at the Parish Church to-morrow morning. The 2nd Liverpool Cadet Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade, of which Lieut.-Colonel Appleton was the commanding officer, will attend the service. They meet at the Town Hall at quarter to ten in review order dress, and march to the Parish Church. Others will join, including the Free Church Lads' Brigade, Boy Scouts, the Blue Coat School (who will provide their band), wounded soldiers at the Infirmary, and others.</p>
        <p>The "Last Post" will be sounded outside the church after service.</p>
        <p>" Elijah " in the Parish Church. A performance of Mendelssohn's oratorio "Elijah" will be given in the Parish Church by members of the Warrington. Musical Society on Thursday evening. There will be a silver collection—or gold, if preferred by the donor— in aid of the War Funds at an interval. It is justifiably anticipated that a large number of persons will avail themselves of the opportu- nity to hear this beautiful oratorio interpreted by local vocalists, for the work is being per- formed by our townspeople.</p>
        <p>LEATHER IS DEARER!</p>
        <p>Owing to the enormous quantity of Boots required for the Armies of the Allies, practically all of which have to be made in England, there is no possible doubt that Boots are certain to be MUCH DEARER.</p>
        <p>The Manufacturers demand an increase of 10 per cent. to 20 per cent., and, in certain kinds of Boots, up to even 30 per cent. Since the outbreak of War the price of LEATHER has risen by nearly one-half. It is evident that the Public will have to pay largely-increased prices, or accept an inferioi article—one or the other.</p>
        <p>WE ARE, HOWEVER, IN THE FORTUNATE POSITION TO BE ABLE TO OFFER LARGE STOCKS OF BOOTS, SIIOES AND SLIPPERS AT PRICES RULING BEFORE THE WAR.</p>
        <p>We strongly advise our friends to take advantage of these Stocks, which represent the best possible value.</p>
        <p>R. ROBERTS &amp; SON 19, BRIDGE STREET.</p>
        <p>IT IS REMIARKABLE What ease and helpfulness may be derived from a Pair of Glasses which have been specially made FOR YOU.</p>
        <p>It will take us, say, from a quarter to half an hour to test pcuir sight, but the benefit to you will be life-long.</p>
        <p>Johnston Watson,</p>
        <p>SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST., Warrington.</p>
        <p>Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Gold from 8/6. Hours of Business, 9 to 7-30.</p>
        <p>Saturday, 9 to 9-39. Please Note.—Mr. Wstson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday, 11 to 5-30; Thursday, 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday, 11 to 5-30.</p>
