      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DISTRICT PIEWS</head>
        <p>DISTRICT PIEWS</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—The magistrates adjudicating at the Stockton Heath Sessions on Tuesday were Messrs. F. A. Frost (in the chair), R. C. Parr, J. A. Ransome, C. Jackson Holmes, J. Hepherd, J. J. Whitley and W. Fletcher.</p>
        <p>Horses Led Astray by a Pony.—At the Stock- ton Heath Sessions on Tuesday, Charles James, Beech Farm, Grappenhall, was fined 2s. 6d. for having allowed his horses to stray on the high- way in Knutsford-road, Grappenhall.—Defend- ant said that a young frisky pony had jumped over the fence, and his horses had followed.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Missionary Meeting.—The Rev. J. A. Alexander (formerly of China) gave an account of his experiences in the East at the concluding meeting in connection with the foreign missionary celebrations at the Wes- leyan Church on Friday week. Mr. R. H. Meldrum occupied the chair, and the total amount realised from the-se-rvices was .210 7s.</p>
        <p>Monthly Meeting of Parochial Committee.— Councillor Pilling presided at the monthly meeting of the Parochial Committee, which was held in the Council Schools on Tuesday even- ing. There were also present Councillors West- brook, Pearson, J- •Whitley, J. Hallows, W. Mclntyre, F. Warburton, and J. McDermid, with Mr. W. Hough (deputy clerk) and Mr. S. Wright (surveyor).</p>
        <p>Can the Bylaws be Improved?—At the meet- ing of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, the Deputy Clerk was instructed to consider the new bylaws and report at a future meeting.— This decision was arrived' at after Councillor</p>
        <p>Pearson had mentioned that in some adjoin- ing parishes a simpler and better system of by- laws, dated 1914, was in operation. He asked if they could be adopted in that district.</p>
        <p>" Mulberry Tree " Licence Transferred.—Mrs. Shilleock was granted the transfer of the licence of the Mulberry Tree Hotel from her late hue- band, at the Stockton Heath Sessions on Tues- day, on the application of Mr. Jenkins, solicitor, Warrington, who, along with her, is the execu- tor of the will of the late licensee.—Mr. Jenkins said he did not want to be placed in the position of executor, and hoped the magistrates would transfer the licence to Mrs. Shillcock, who was experienced in the business. A Job for the Lighting Committee.—A pro- posal to erect another lamp in Warburton- street was the subject of discussion at the Paro- chial Committee meeting on Tuesday.—Council- lor Whitley stated that already Warburton- street and neighbouring streets were better lighted than other parts of the district. People complained to him that the property of certain persons was lighted and that of others was not. —Councillor Mclntyre: But a lot of streets are covered.—The matter was referred to the Light- ing committee.</p>
        <p>Driving a Motor Car Without a Licence.—At the Stockton Heath Sessions on Tuesday, Alex- ander Ferguson (18), son of Dr. Ferguson, was sammoned for driving a motor car without a licence.—lt was stated that a collision had occurred between the motor car driven by de- fendant and a motor cyclist. When asked for his licence at his home .iefa.ndant produced one that had expired on October 2nd.-51r. Steel, for the defence, said that the defendant attended the Grammar School in Warrington and liked to drive the car for his father on Sunday morn- ings. The accident was not caused through his fault. Unfortunately the licence had not been renewed.—Defendant was ordered to pay ss. costs.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Entertainment.—ln connection with the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath Band of Hope the second entertainment of the session was given in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Mon- day evening. Several members contributed to the programme, and .they were assisted by Miss Smith, of the Latchford Baptist Band.of Hope, who rendered two songs in pleasing fashion. Other songs were given by Hilda Taylor, Isabel Clark, Lizzie Brooks and Emily Williams. Recitations were contributed by D. Gerrard, A. Lonsdale, I. Lynch, A. Clayton, L. Brooks, A. Ramsbottom, S. Gamble and E. Travis. "How the garden grows" was the title of a dialogue in which M. Adamson, D. Summer, M. Wil- liams, E. Todd, B. Gerrard and R. Summer took part, and a second dialogue, "The sad effects of drink," was given by B. Clarke, L. Brookes and J. Clarke. An excellent address by the Rev. H. T. Dixon (Penketh) was much appreciated. The Rev. G. Curtis presided and an efficient accom- panist was found in Mr. G. A. Whitfield.</p>
        <p>Training Corps Marksmen on the Range.—ln charge of Musketry-Instructor A. Shepherd, assisted by Mr. C. J. Smith and Mr. W. J. Featherstone, the shooting squad of the Pre- paratory Training Corps visited the Appleton Rifle Range on Saturday. Ten rounds—five each with miniature and service rifles—at 100 yards were fired, the highest possible score with each class of weapon being 25 points. The scores were:—</p>
        <p>. Minia- Ser- ture. vice. Total. M. A. Piercey J. Towers E. J. Sutton W. Whitley R. Roughsedge E. A. White W. R. Laws J. H. Clarke J. R. Baxter J. T. Scott W. Richardson C. S. Woods E. Worthington S. P. Silcock J. W. C. Taylor A. Millar</p>
        <p>225 176 401 —The full charge of ammunition was given by Mr. T. W. Fletcher, Stretton.</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>That War Again !—lt is understood that owing to the War the parish tea party is not being held this year, or, at any 'rate, is to be post- poned for some time.</p>
        <p>Red Cross Patients.—Dr. Thorp, of Lymm, has performed two operations on inmates of the Red Cross Hospital, and both, we learn, are making satisfactory progress.</p>
        <p>The Uncontrolled Steed.—At the Stockton Heath Sessions on Tuesday, Robert Richards, Pepper-street, Lynini, was fined 2s. 6d. for hay- ing been in charge of a horse and trap without having proper control of the horse.</p>
        <p>A Satisfactory Report.—The annual report of the Lymm Lads' Club has this week been issued, and shows a very satisfactory year's work, upon which the officials are to be congratulated.</p>
        <p>" Tommies " in the Village.—A few Terri- torials and National Reservists arrived home in Lymm on Thursday evening, and will remain for six days—when their leave expires. They give interesting accounts of their training, and hope shortly to go to the Front,</p>
        <p>Recruiting Upsets Football Fixture.—A match had been arranged for this week between the Lymm Wednesday Club and the Manchester Taxi-Drivers. Unfortunately--or, happily?—it had to be abandoned, owing to the enlistment in the Army of most of the members of the latter club!</p>
        <p>Football for Red Cross • Funds.—Of the match arranged to be played on the Pepper-street ground on Christmas Day, between the local Wednesday and Saturdays clubs, full particu- lars will be given .next week. The worthy cause is the assistance of the Lymm Red Cross Hospital Funds, and a crowded "gate" is an- ticipated.</p>
        <p>Yuletide Shopping Arrangements.—The Lymm Tradesmen's Association have—as announced by Mr. W. E. Hillier, hon. secretary—decided as follows with regard to Christmas shop hours : Wednesday, December 23, open till 8 p.m.; closed all Christmas Day. Wednesday, December 30, open until 8 p.m.; and closed all New Year's Day.</p>
        <p>How It Happened.—Harry Goulder, of Booths Hill, Lymm, was fined ss. at the Stockton Heath Sessions on Tuesday for not having a light at the rear of the motor which he was driving, and also for not having his licence in his posses- sion.—Defendant said he thought the light ..as in, and that he had forgotten his licence Throa changing his clothes.</p>
        <p>Lads' Club Bazaar.—The Drill Hall was quite filled on Wednesday, when the Lymm Lads' Club held a most successful cake and candy sale—as a result of which the sum of about £35 was raised in aid of the funds. Besides the stalls, there were several side-shows, including conjuring, fish-pond, rifle range, " cock-fighting," and "bait- ing the badger." Musical selections were taste- fully rendered, including solos by Mrs. Slee and Mr. Odber. During the afternoon and even- ing refreshments were provided at a sinall charge, and later the club members gave the sketch, "The Diamond Thief," in fine style. Great credit is due to the club, and especially to Mr. Farrar, who was a prime organiser of the bazaar.</p>
        <p>Anonymous Gift to the Parish Church.—At a •Consistory Court for the diocese of Chester, held in the Cathedral, on Monday, before the Chan- cellor (Sir Philip Baker-Wilbraham), the Rey. G. B. Thurston, rector of Lymm, asked, on his own and the wardens' behalf, for confirmation of their action in having introduced into the church an oak litany desk; also for permission to remove the font at present in use and to substitute a new one, on a site beneath the tower of the church, the—oid ront to be disposed of as the Rector and' his wardens might approve. —The permission and confirmation sought *ere granted, it having been stated that the lectern formerly standing in the church had been given to the parishioners of Antrobus, and placed in the church there,sand that the cost of the new font would be defrayed by a parishioner who desired to remain anonymous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>_ Sale of Work.—A sale of work was held in the Orford Schoolroom on Thursday afternoon and evening, as a result of which about £3O was raised. The proceeds will he divided amongst the various parochial funds. The sale was de- clared open at 3 o'clock by Mrs. Fish. The stall- holders were :—Vicarage Stall: Mrs. Owen, Mrs. J. Povey, Mrs. Burrows and Mrs. Morris. Sun- day School Stall: Miss S. E. Melbourne and members of the Girls' Junior Bible Class. Chocolate Stall : Misses Amy Ball, Freda Car- penter, Bessie Parsonage and Emily Ward. Re. freshment Stall : Mrs. White, Miss Millington. Miss Millwood, Mrs. Monks, Mrs. Armstrong and Miss Wollner. Market Garden Stall: Mrs. Richardson. A rifle range WAS in charge of Mr. Stacc - Owen.</p>
        <p>YAkc</p>
        <p>;ey ,AT HOME !ROletak,. el op ( OW' AT THE FRONT 74, uk THE kR; NATIONAL fhPSTAVD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>FACE AT THE STY!</head>
        <p>THE FACE AT THE STY!</p>
        <p>Why a Horse Bolted, and the Damage It Did.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before his Honour Judge Gurner, Harry Sam Saville, iron broker, The Cross, Lymm, claimed X 44 6s. lid. from Messrs. I'eter Walker and Sons, Ltd., brewers, Warrington and Bur- ton, in respect of damage done to his motor car by a horse belonging to the defendant company, which bolted from a farmyard at Thelwall and dashed into the car.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Browne appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. G. Jordan, of Manchester (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) for the defendants.</p>
        <p>Horse Dashed Out " Like a Flash of Lightning."</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said the plaintiff was the owner of a Ford motor car, which, on October Ist, was being driven by his son, Thomas, on the high- way at Thelwall. The son was accompanied by Miss Molyneaux, and the car was travelling at about 10 to 15 miles per hour. When passing the road near Bridge Farm the defendants' horse dashed out "like a flash of lightning," and crashed into the bonnet of the car, doing very material damage to it and severely injur- ing the young lady. She was conveyed to a cot- tage, and then defendants' servant, Davenport, who had charge of all the horses at the brewery, and who was out with the horse that did the damage, came up and said: "I suppose you will hold' me responsible?" and Mr. Saville replied "Yes."</p>
        <p>"Send in the Bill and My ' Boss' Will Foot It."</p>
        <p>Davenport then said: "Send in the bill and my 'boss' will foot it." The car had to be dragged to Lymm and sent to the Ford Works at Old Trafford. Mr. Saville lost use of the car, which lie sometimes lent out at .C 3 3s. per day. They had put that loss down at £2 10s. per week for four weeks. Mr. Hutton, one of the heads of the firm, along with Davenport, called on Mr. Saville, sen., and during a conversation Mr. Hutton said: "I suppose you will expect our people to make good the damage?" Mr. I Saville replied: "Certainly; you are legally and morally responsible. If the conditions had been reversed I should have been liable to pay." Mr. Hutton then said: "Send in, your bill, but don't make it too large." .o!t Little Girl Left in Charge. Mr. Molyneaux, the father of the young lady, and Mr. Thomas Saville had a conversation with a little girl at the farm, from which it appeared that Davenport had left her in charge of the horse whilst he went to see a Mr. Lloyd. The girl had a little child to look after, and, whilst she was doing that, the horse bolted. The contention was that Davenpdrt was negli- gent in not making the horse fast, or seeing that it was properly attended. It was suggested that a pig looking over a sty had caused the horse to bolt.</p>
        <p>His Honour; It depends on the countenance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
