      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</p>
        <p>went into another. We lost both our Maxim guns, I am sorry to say.</p>
        <p>British Lines impenetrable. "But I firmly believe," the Private con- cluded, "that the Germans will never get through the British lines. They showed no signs of doing so when I was carried away, sick, from Ypres; although by then we had lost all our officers !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PEN K ETki</head>
        <p>PEN K ETki</p>
        <p>Vicar Addresses Wesley Guild.—Before a good attendance of the Wesley Guild on Tuesday evening, the Rev. J. R. Jones, vicar of St Mary's, Great Sankey, gave a most interesting paper entitled "The individual and the war."</p>
        <p>Concert for Soldiers' Comforts.—A very suc- cessful concert in aid of the fund out of which the members of the Penketh and Sankey Sew- ing Class are purchasing material to work up into soldiers' garments, was given in the Wes- leyan School room on Friday evening of last 1 Week. The artistes, who gave their services free, were in specially good form, and a most enjoyable evening was spent by a large dudi- ence; with a contribution of ...t5 9s. 04d. to the 1 fund as the result. The programme, which was much lengthened by demands for encores, was as follow,:—Solo, " Mifanwy," Miss Dodds; solo, "The veteran's song," Mr. Clay; imita- tion of George Formby (the Lancashire come- dian) in "I'm taking my father's tea," Mr. Harrison; quartet, Penketh Tannery Band; solo, "Friend o' mine," Mrs. Sanders; solo, "England," Miss Tanner; song, "How are yer," Mr. Harrison; solos, "Somewhere a voice is calling" and "Little grey home in the West," Miss Dodds; solos, "A sergeant of the Line" and "Motherland," Mr. Clay; monologue, "The coward," Mr. Harrison; solo, "My dear soul," Mrs. Sanders (who received an encore); quartet, Penketh Tannery Band; solos, "There is a land" and "Little Boy Blue," Miss Tanner; song, "When mother dropped a ha'penny in the 'bus," and recitation, "The night watchman," Mr. Harrison. The Chairman, Mr. E. A. Murphy, moved a vote of thanks to the artistes, which was warmly accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>"IT'S MURDER, NOT WAR!" Thelwall Corporal _Writes. of "Hell</head>
        <p>"IT'S MURDER, NOT WAR!" Thelwall Corporal _Writes. of "Hell</p>
        <p>with the Lid Off."</p>
        <p>Corporal A. Beckett, a former scholar at the The'wall School and at one time a member of the village choir, is at present lying in hospital at Leeds, haying been invalided home. "I came back in the hospital ship Carisbrooke Castle with a crowd of wounded," he writes to his father, "hut lam not wounded. I con- tracted a nasty cold, so I was put into a French field-hospital at a place called Haze- brouck, but a German aeroplane came over and dropped three shells, and almost wrecked the place, though, barring the shock, nobody was the worse."</p>
        <p>"It is murder out there, not war !" he writes. "To put it in a nutshell, it's 'hell with the lid off.' taste of powder off my chest yet. We have been along the firing-line about a dozen times, and the sights I have seen have stamped a mark on my memory for life. . . . The worst 'do ' we got was in Belgium, when outside Ypres our convoy was exposed to fire for six hours. • • . . It was night . . . . but the place was illuminated with bursting shells. We all thought our last hour had come, but we got through all right." In one of his letters—penned in France—the Corporal says: "To-day I have had my meals with a Latchford boy. . . . Now don't worry about me! I would never have rested content if I had not come over here and seen a bit of it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>"ENJOYING THE HOLIDAY." Warringlonian AVho Prefers Fighting</head>
        <p>"ENJOYING THE HOLIDAY." Warringlonian AVho Prefers Fighting</p>
        <p>to Furlough.</p>
        <p>Writing to his father, who lives at 32, Orford- avenue, Private F. W. Hawthorne, a driver in the A.S.C., who, before "joining up," was em- ployed by Messrs. Richmond, says:— " The only cover we have is a cart sheet, hung up on poles, but it makes a very efficient cover. We have very little chance of finding out the customs, &amp;c., of the French pepple, but you would not notice in them anything different from the English, if it were not for their speech. It does make you feel sorry that you can't speak the 'lingo,' and so fraterniiii" it bit more with them, as they do try to be so friendly. Tell everybody who so kindly asks about me that I am doing champion and am enjoying the holi- day. I don't want any furlough. I had a bit of luck the other day. A shell burst about 20 yards from my horse and cart, but it did noth- ir.,-; more than kick up some dirt in the field at the right of us. . . . The immensity of the operations only strikes you when, every morn- ing, you see the thousands of motors and horse- driven carts lined up behind one another, ready to start for the firing line. "We pass columns of troops going .to or com- ing back from the front, the former all life and spirits, the latter with unwashed faces, a month's growth of beard, and—officers as well as men—absolutely 'jiggered up.' In a week, however, they are as fit as kings, and ready for another do.' "</p>
        <p>Private Hawthorne has two brothers on ser- vice, Private Robert L. Hawthorne and Private Hugh Hawthorne, the former with the Terri- torials and the latter with the R.F.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>" SWANKER " WITH THE "FAGS."</head>
        <p>A " SWANKER " WITH THE "FAGS."</p>
        <p>Appreeiathe "Tommy" Returns Thanks for "Comforts."</p>
        <p>Rifleman T. Helsby, of the 3rd Battalion Rifle Brigade, has sent a letter to. Messrs. John Mel- bourne, Ltd., iron founders, Warrington—with Whom he was employed before taking up mili- tary duties.—in which he gives an interesting description of trench life. A number of cigar- ettes had been sent to him, besides mittens, and in expressing his thanks and appreciation, he writes :</p>
        <p>'I was quite a swanker ' with the 'fags'— On a level with the sergeants—and the mitts are fine. They don't interfere with my shooting. I have asked about sending a sample of the Ger- man Jack Johnsons ' and shrapnel, but we can- not send anything home. I picked up a piece of a 'Johnson ' five minutes after it burst. It was about 30 yards away. I was on sentry-go at the time, and it fell short of its mark, and the hole it made in the ground was fully 12 feet across and easily 3 feet deep: You can hear The shells coming quite a time before they burst. I can tell you they are not very welcome bruete,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Treat to Get Under 3 rt oof Again !</head>
        <p>A Treat to Get Under 3 rt oof Again !</p>
        <p>_ . We have come out of the trenches this week- end for a rest and a wash, and it is quite a treat to get under a roof again. The weather keeps very cold, and it makes it very dirty in the trenches. We are 350 yards from the German trenches. The enemy sing songs at night and in the morning. They seem to know English songs. I am quite well myself, and in as good a spirit as when I left—or better, now that lam used to rifle and artillery fire! I was rather timid at first, when I heard bullets whizzing past me, and especially when I could hear the Johnsons' coming, but that feeling soon wears off one. . . . Well, I was like a lot more over here, expecting to get back home again by Christmas, ; but I think it has gone too far for triat now. You would take us for mudlarks if you could see us coming out of the trenches—whiskers as long as laces, and faces the colour of the earth."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PHILOSOPHIC "TOMMY." " Nothing Troubles Us At All."</head>
        <p>PHILOSOPHIC "TOMMY." " Nothing Troubles Us At All."</p>
        <p>enemy's flank, but this they were unable to do, owing t 6 the thickly-wooded nature of the country—throughout the whole experience of Private Brent, this feature greatly impeded the progress of the cavalry. They, therefore, hung on to their position until relieved by the Ist Royal Dragoons, and then passed through Pocuspell and made their way towards Rum- beke village. There they halted, watered and fed the horses, and also "had a snack" them- selves. After resting for half an hour, they again proceeded along the road to Isingham, where they remained for the night. At half- past three the next morning orders were re- ceived to saddle up and go to the support of the 4th Hussars.</p>
        <p>Loss of a Squadron. During the battle which ensued, the regi- ment lost " C" Squadron, which got 'entirely cut off from them, and to whose assistance they were unable to go, as they were "blown" out of their position by "Jack Johnson" shells. They passed through Ypres before it Was destroyed, and, said Private Brent: "I may say that it was one of the most beautiful towns I have ever seen." They then won and took up a position just behind Zellebeke, where they had "a very warm reception" from the enemy's fire. Whilst they were escorting the " K " Battery, Royal Horse Artillery, an 181 b. shell burst overhead, and a piece of shrapnel, striking down, cut the nose-bag, from which Private Brent's horse wad then feeding, in two. This happily stopped the force of the blow and saved the charger. "It was a nar- row escape for me, I can tell you," added the Private, with a laugh, as he showed our repre- sentative the very piece of shrapnel referred to.</p>
        <p>" Another Narrow Shave." "Then," continued Private Brent, "I had another narrow shave, for a bullet passed through the back of my 'serge,' and I was very lucky not to be hurt. "We passed on to Ypres, and it was the hottest place I have been in. However, we then got relief with the escort—and the reliev- ing regiment came through very fortunately, for it only had a small number of casualties. During the night, we took up a position in the trenches, where we remained for four days— the longest time, so far as I know, the cavalry have spent in the trenches. Whilst we were here, the enemy were shelling us left and right. I was 'blown' out of ray trench, and</p>
        <p>Private F. Appleton, of Padgate, who is with the 4th Corps M.T.A.S.C., which is attached to the Bth Division Supply Column of the Expedi- tionary Force in France, has written a cheery letter home, in the course w hien he says • "We arrived in France on November 9th, after a fair passage, but where we are now I am not allowed to say. lam in the best of health, and have nothing to complain about. We are having plenty of fun trying to make the people understand what we want. You have heard some people talk about the roar of the cannon. Well, thunder isn't in it with the guns! At present lam on duty every night, as eight of us have to run the engines every hour, as the frost is very keen where we are. But we get round the fire about 12 p.m., and start cooking steak, chips, and all sorts of 'grub.' You would laugh if you could see us; in fact, you would think we were out for a picnic. Nothing troubles us at all except the tobacco, as the French tobacco is awfully bitter! I have just finished a feed of steak and chips, and can assure you that the French women are fine cooks."</p>
        <p>AVlty Not Temperance Public-houses? Addressing a recent meeting of the Bewsey- road Wesley Guild, the Rev. T. T. James advo- cated temperance public-houses. We were sometimes tempted, he said, to think that people who frequented public-houses only dis- cussed subjects of sport or the news of the day, but he had heard :nen coming out of these houses discuss religion and other social ques- tions of the hour, and they have been on the right tack, too. Wise words and deep thoughts have come from men who have reasoned about these subjects in their own minds in their great sober moments, and if we could transform some of the public-houses into temperance re- freshment rooms where men could go and sit, lie doubted not that the same company of men who resorted to the public-houses would go to the same places if they could be worked on temperance lines, for- the love of association often led a man to go and sit and enjoy the company of his friends and fellow-workmates rather than the desire always for strong drink.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>PAWNED MS ARMY GREAT-COAT</p>
        <p>Field Artilleryman in Trouble.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on ThurschlY;.: Robert Williamson, of 59, Lowe-st t, reet, S' Helens, was charged with pawning an overcoa the property of the military authorities. d. The Chief Constable asked for a weeVe journment of the case. Williamson, ile Plained, enlisted in the Royal Field ArtilleT and on Tuesday came away on leave, being tt. vided with his railway ticket and a gretLt4-I,en The latter he pledged. in Warrington, and, taxed by a detective, denied having done t; The pawnbroker, however, having noticed ed military mark on the coat, ran out and returtiliat the coat, taking back his money (60. :rend morning Williamson had admitted the re. said he had never done anything like it belhe He (the Chief Constable) suggested that. T prisoner should be handed over to the waits., authoritiett The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Life Guardsman Tells of a Narrow Escape.</head>
        <p>Life Guardsman Tells of a Narrow Escape.</p>
        <p>One of the wounded warriors now recuper- ating at Raddon Court is Private Brent, for- merly of the 3rd Dragoon Guards. At the beginning of the war, Private Brent was trans- ferred to the Ist Life Guards, to make up the regiment, which, a fortnight afterwards, left England. The troops were escorted across the North Sea, to Belgium, by fourteen destroyers. After three days they arrived at Ostend, where they disembarked their horses and waggons, and rested for the night along the dock-side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Long Drive.</head>
        <p>A Long Drive.</p>
        <p>Next day, commencing on a long drive, they scoured the country all around Ostend, but had no sight of a German for miles. After four days' continuous searching, however, they came across the enemy-19,000 strong! General Kavanagh, who was in command of the bri- gade, immediately set up an opposition. Turn- ing his men round, he opened fire upon the enemy, meanwhile getting them into position. The Germans, however, proving too many for them, they were compelled to retire; during which the enemy shelled the village of Moor- slede. Valuable work was here done on the side of the Allies by the "K" Battery of the Royal Horse Artillery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>"Too Thickly Wooded."</head>
        <p>"Too Thickly Wooded."</p>
        <p>After retreating for about two miles, the English took up a fresh position and waited for reinforcements to come up. Presently they were relieved by their infantry. Leaving these they then attempted to get round on the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>VICTORIA C OSS</head>
        <p>VICTORIA C OSS</p>
        <p>da</p>
        <p>BATTERY-SERGT.-MAJOR .(NOW SECOND-LIEUT.) G. T. DORRELL, " L " Battery, Royal Horse Artillery. SERGEANT D. NELSON (NOW SECOND-LIEUTENANT), "L" Battery, Royal Horse Artillery.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL C. E. GARFORTH, rsth (The King's) Hussars. BOMBARDIER E. G. HARLOCK (NOW SERGEANT), I I3th Battery,</p>
        <p>, Royal Field Artillery. LANCE-CORPORAL C. A. JARVIS, 57th Field Company, Royal Engineers.</p>
        <p>- LANCE-CORPORAL W. FULLER, 2nd Battalion, Welsh Regiment. LANCE-CORPORAL F. W. HOLMES, 2nd Battalion (The King's Own) Yorkshire Light Infantry. ___</p>
        <p>PRIVATE S. F. GODLEY, 4th Battalion, Royal Fusiliers. PRIVATE G. WILSON, 2nd Battalion, Highland Light Infantry. DRIVER J. H. C. DRAIN, 37th Battery, Royal Field Artillery. DRIVER F. LUKE, 37th Battery, Royal Field Artillery.</p>
        <p>There is room for</p>
        <p>our name on this</p>
        <p>Roll of Honour.</p>
        <p>THESE HEROES would never have won the VICTORIA CROSS by staying away from the RECRUITING OFFICE. They enlisted for their Country's sake, and fought as only brave men do.</p>
        <p>Is your name to be known from one end of the world to the other as one of the Empire's bravest sons ?</p>
        <p>ENLIST TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The more men we have, the sooner the war will end.</p>
