      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Successful Wesleyan Concert.—An excellent concert was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Sankey Bridges, on Saturday evening, a goodly- sized audience assembling and thoroughly, enjoy- ing the programme prepared for their delecta- tion. Miss Annie L. Fry opened the concert ith a pianoforte solo, and she also officiated as accompanist. Mr. J. Thwaite won applause for his treatment of Squire's dainty song "Moun- tain lovers" and for a rendition of Ganz's "I seek thee in every flower." Miss B. -Brough was successful with a number from "The Arca- dians," and later on with Coven's "A birth- day," and a quartet of finely played violin solos by Mr. Bradford Barton were rapturously received. Madame E. A. Sanders was heard to great advantage in " Friend o' mine" and "My dear soul," and Mr. E. A. Sanders contributed a couple of humorous sketches, both of which proved "big hits." At the close the artistes were accorded a hearty vote of thanks, on the proposition of the Rev. 11. T. Dixon, seconded by Mr. Miles. Mr. G. Davey-Smith was in the chair.</p>
        <p>G.F.S. Concert for War Fund.—A concert in aid of the local War Fund, arranged by the members of the Girls' Friendly Society, was held in Sankey Sunday School on _Thursday evening, when the Rev. J. R. Jones presided over a very good audience. The artistes were: Miss Dodds (mezzo-soprano), Miss Livesey (soprano), Mr. W. Ray (bass), Mr. J. Platt (humorist), and Mrs. John Dodds (accompan- ist). A number of the members of the G.F.S. gave a dialogue and also presented some really good tableaux. The Vicar proposed the usual vote of thanks, which was seconded by Mr. E. Dumbill and carried with applause.</p>
        <p>MERSEY! .</p>
        <p>A map of the United Kingdom published in the Berlin "Lokalanzeiger" depicts the Mer- sey as being located in the West of Ireland. Frankly we are surprised at the Germans show- ing any Mersey anDrwhere.—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>N.C.O's and</head>
        <p>N.C.O's and</p>
        <p>who have won</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Roll of Honour.—The following young men, formerly connected with the Pad- gate Wesleyan Church, are at present serving with the colours: Frank Appleton, James Apple- ton, Arthur Appleton, George Brough, Percy Duckworth, Harold Kitchen, Reginald Lunt, Charles Milne, and Joseph Birchall.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening and was presided over by Mr. F. Wright. An address on "Hebrew, Greek,. and Prayer," was given by the Rev. W. Thompson Derry. Mrs. Stewart gave a sweet rendition of the solo "Faith," and Mr. H. Tomlin was thanked for his gift to the Guild of an illumin- ated roll of honour, on which will be placed the names of those 'who have left the church to join the colours.</p>
        <p>Women's Relief Association.—A successful whist drive and dance, organised by A committee of young men, on behalf of the local Women's Relief Association, took place in the Church of , England Schools on Thursday evening. Twenty- ' five tables were occupied for whist, and the winners of the prizes—which were the gifts of the committee and Messrs'. P. Royston, W. D. Turner and T. Ludlow, and were formally pre- sented to the successful competitors by Mrs. Collier—were :—Ladies 1, Miss Whitlow; 2, Mrs. Starkey. Gents: 1. Mr. Jos. Smith; 2, Mr. C. Tt. Sandiford. The dance music was played by Mr. W. Wainwright, and Messrs. F. Garner and R. Hankinson were the M.C.'s. There was a large attendance and the arrangements were admirably carried out by the committee, with Mr.\ W. Fryar as its secretary and Mr'. F. Pom- frey as its treaSurer.</p>
      </div>
