      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>lothing New.</p>
        <p>I daresay many of my younger readers suppose the movement in favour of an eight hours' Working day to be quite modern. It is really ilot so at all. There was vigorous and persist- ent agitation of that very " reform " upwards Of 80 years ago. As I read last week's review of -Perris' "Industrial History," I thought of -a little town in which I lived some years. It had worthy monument to a distinguished native, Mr. John Fielden, who, as M.P. for Oldham, was the chief instrument in the passing of the Ten Hours Bill. Mr. Fielden, so long ago as 1833, strongly advocated an eight hours' day, and was prominent in the formation and one of the most active and earnest meinbers of the National Regeneration Society that had this re- form for its main object. They had specially in Mind the cotton trade, the operatives in which, as they were-at considerable pains to show, had then no leisure for recreation and self-improve- tuna. Those who have imagined the movement in question to be of the 20th century may well be told that they do unintentional injustice to the memory of disinterested and self-sacrificing tnen of a former era who laboured hard for it in the interests of the workers. Mr. Fielden was a large employer of labour who considered ftret the well-being of the employed.</p>
        <p>"Sheep on Grass." In the agricultural column last week I found under this heading an extract from " Far M—and }Tome," in which a Somerset farmer attributes the diminution of sheep to decrease of arable land. "Sheep and arable go together," declares he. Well, I presume it must be so in " Zum- Inerzet," but, if my experience be of .any value— and it is with all due deference and diffidence that I interpose in such a controversy—it is not necessarily so in our northern latitudes. I have been most accustomed to the Lancashire moun- tain breed known to the shepherds and dealers as " Lonks," and, as I have previously stated in this column these are simply turned on to the illimitable moors and for long intervals left to 'fend" for themselves. I have known many Yorkshire and Lancashire farmers who were able to maintain large flocks of sheep and geese for ttext to nothing. The former browsed content- edly on the coarse herbage of the moors, to which tho freeholders had right of access. I take it (or rather, like Rosa Dartle, I ask for informa- tion) that such land can hardly be termed " arable."</p>
        <p>I mmortal ity—Of Sorts. Last week's story of the professor Who decided to signalise the long and faithful service of his (:ook by naming after her a beetle which he had lately discovered, reminded me of a similar honour conferred on a working man mycologist who lived in a district in which I worked years 4go—l made some mention of him here when he died, lie came upon two specimens of fungi that were quite new to science, and they have ver since been known to the world of mycolo- Elias by the name of this humble cottager (he Was a moulder by trade), and so appear in the eatalogues and text-books—in a Latinised form, of course. •</p>
        <p>xatnples of Another Kind. By way of contrast with the foregoing, let me epeat a little story which I heard long ago and</p>
        <p>lvflich may possibly one of these days- come to have some bearing upon a home problem. I qijoyed the friendship of the late Postmaster Of Rockville (111., U.S.A.), who fought in the Civil War, but never claimed a pension, 41thoug1i it was notorious that hosts who had tezidered no such .service were benefiting from the fund. There was also a National Soldiers' itotne to the secretary of which I am indebted , .</p>
        <p>for my story. Said ho: "They are great wags, the old soldiers in our Johnson City Home. I neard one of them describe a very fierce and famous action. Two hundred men had been Pitted against three hundred, and after the fight- ing only sixty brave fellows—thirty on each Side—remained alive. Then the old soldier Daused solemnly. 'Of that sixty, boys,' he said, there only survive to-day-==—.' Overcome, he blev; his nose violently. There only sur- vive to-day, by actual statistics, 417.' "</p>
        <p>'seas a Blessing.</p>
        <p>, What happened at the last meeting of the ,Warrington Rural District Council set me think- of the subject generally. I recalled how, in " Empire Review," Dr. Eliz. Sloan Ches:ser hailed the twentieth century as " the era of public health, when disease will be not cured, r)u,t prevented." Then I turned up a back num- 4er of the "Grand Magazine," in which the vir- tues of disease were uphelii by no less an Authority than Sir Fredk-. Treves. He goes so as to declare, indeed, that if it were not 1?1' disease the human race would soon be ex- ...</p>
        <p>,thet. In support of this startling proposition, `llO eminent scientist takes the case of a man ll'ho is wounded in the hand by an unclean in- '3trainent. The one dread fear is that inflamma- !ion may set in. Yet the inflammation is noth- 'llg but a blessing in disguise. Sir Frederick Droceeds to describe in detail what has hap- -I)ned in the man's hand. First, an army of 4lalignant germs has entered into the wound, ~.Dossibly to multiply itself by millions in a day. piO invasion is met by a rush of blood to the Illvaded part, so that it becomes swollen and in- 'lamed ; and the corpuscles in the blood, called 4licocytes, which are born microbe-killers, fall I,lPon the enemy. Millions are opposed to mil- ! ions, and the fight is to the death. The arena ,a 3 Piled up with the dead, until at last the the dead, the poisoning, and the poisoned, ,4,re thrown out in the form of what is known as matter" or pus, and the trouble, probably, ends. Should the invading force beat down the 'll-st line of defence and find a way into the !'hannels of the body, then a stand as vigorous made at the second circle of entrenchments— liM lymphatic glands. A common cold is re- ed to as another instance of a maligned _ _ _</p>
        <p>ease. We are all painfully familiar with. the 'Ytoptoms. The cold germ enters through the 11.4' passages, promptly develops, and soon brings e'oout sneezing, catarr4, sore throat, coughing, 4/14 malaise. The doctor cheerfully bids us loioice and be glad on account of these, going on '0 show that without these concomitants a corn- bleu cold might prove fatal. ‘4.rhe Mount of Olives."</p>
        <p>At the recital of sacred music in aid of the 111 )gian Refugees' Fund tri. St. Mary's Church Thursday evening, the " fiallelujall" number ~l'ora Beethoven's "Mount of Olives" was in- This has been well described as a true °,l:atorio chorus ,Of praise and thanksgiving to Highest—a fugal chorus positively unsnr- Ttesed in grandeur and massive effect by any of Ills kind. "One feels that a great battle has -Nut fought and won—that the triumph is I.4deed iival and unalterable." I quote Rutland ;-',4ughton, who wrote of it thus in "Musical 'l&gt;ition" many years ago. I subscribed to that tai3e-r in those days, and, although I know nut hat has become of the rest, I have taken good of that, excellent article on the work of the itilumer which had timely exposition here ors hnrsday night. 4</p>
        <p>trnetuality. !'nere and There" paragraph on The „'lse wife chooser" reminded me of an ,old ,Nistratee clerk in a Yorkshire town that 2' Zed in 30 years ago who prided himself greatly his punctuality. In other places of which I had experience the justices' clerk has in- ta,bly awailed their worships' pleasure, and Crer ventured to precede them into court; but wie of whom I now write particularly always L't his room the moment ti,e Town Hall clock to chime the hour in thp,t he might kr,Rated at the post of duty while yet it was ; If there were no "beaks" in readiness itccompany him at the precise moment—well, ott' Great Unpaid had a lesson in punctuality '.4 the salaried functionary. I have in mind ;.4411er case in which the conditiens were often ersed. The clerk was a busy lawyer, who, to te the American phrase, had bitten off more 411 he could chew. In addition to his private etice, he was town clerk, registrar a the tltY Court, and I know not what beside; BO when there was a police court sitting, he 7it difficult to be in his place there at the tllnted time. The magistrates would take seats Am the bench, and when their legal tiier entered with apologetic air the chair- Wonld ocenstotially 111 IP]. n iiii I I reinoii- c,:;'ll-e in an undertone; but (lie habit COlll- - of was incurable,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NATIONAL WEALTH.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL WEALTH.</p>
        <p>Various Standards by Which it</p>
        <p>is Measured.</p>
        <p>A HUMAN VALUATION.</p>
        <p>Work and Wealth. A Human Valuation. By J. A. Hobson. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd. Pp. xv. 367. 8/6 net.</p>
        <p>Wealth, in its most familiar aspect, is re- garded from the pecuniary standpoint, that is, as measurable in terms of money. When we speak of the national income, although we mean the various goods and services of which it consists, we speak of it in terms of an accepted standard, namely, the gold standard. And although this standard is by no means per- fect for the work of monetary measurement, it is stable and has a single definite meaning to all men. It enables us to estimate the rates of growth or decline in our industry, and to com- pare the growth of our national wealth with that of other nations.</p>
        <p>But is this the only, or the safest, standard by which to measure wealth? Does it enable us to bring our national wealth into intelligible relation with our national welfare? Does it supply us with any conception of an end, an ideal, towards which we may work in the way of social betterment? Is it a standard which supplies us with a criterion by which we may safely and intelligently judge whether or not our social system works harmoniously? It has not seemed so to social reformers in the past, nor does it seem so now. Years ago John Ruskin attempted a different method of measuring wealth. Wealth, according to Ruskin, is life, and there is no other form of wealth. Value, he says, is the life-giving power of anything; cost the quantity of labour required to produce it. This was a valid criterion by which to measure the wealth of the community at any moment. And, indeed, some such criterion or standard is implied in the efforts of every states- man, social reformer, philanthropist and public- spirited citizen to improve the conditions of national life. No progress would be possible if we attempted to use the monetary standard as the measure of social accomplishment, or as affording an ideal towards which to direct our efforts. ECONOMIC AND HUMAN VALUES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobson, in the volume before us, takes up the task, or only part of it, as he is careful to inform Rs, as indicated by Ruskin, viz., "to apply to industry the vital standard of valua- tion, or at any rate to improve the instru- ments of vital survey." The method Mr. Hob- son follows is to translate economic into human values; by reference to a standard of organic welfare. This standard of organic welfare is based on the view that society must be con- ceived, not as the sum of the individuals who comprise it, or as a set of social relations, but " as a collective organism, with life, will, pur- pose, meaning of its own, as distinguished from the life, will, purpose, meaning, of the indi- vidual members of it." To those who raise ob- jections to the extension of the biological term " organism" to society, Mr. Hobson replies that difficulties which arise from this extension are such as arise whenever an extension of boundaries occurs in the intellectual world. "What is necessary," he says, " is that some term should be used to assist the mind in realising clearly that all life proceeds by the co-operation of minds working, not each for it separate self, but for the whole, and attaining their separate well-being in the proper function- ing of that whole. As the structure of the organic cell, the Organ and the organism, illus- trate this co-operative and composite life, so with the larger groupings which we call socie- ties. An animal organism is a society of cells." Now, for the purpose of a human valuation of industrial; society—and that, of course, is Mr. Hobson' k object in this book—the import- ance of this theory of the social organism is obvious. It requires that, in considering the vital casts and satisfactions involved in the production and consumption of goods, we have regard to their effects, not only upon the indi- viduals who produce and consume the goods, but upon the city, nation, or other society to which they belong. It requires that produc- tion and consumption be viewed, not as separ- ate and distinct arts, but as functions equally necessary to the health of the organism, aspects of one process which is, of course, the satisfac- tion of the needs of the social organism. With this standard of organfc welfare, based on the theory of the social organism, Mr. Hob- son proceeds to survey, the actual iiosition of industrial society. EXCHANGEABLE VALUES.</p>
        <p>The usual commercial way of looking at in- dustry is'as follows: In the process of manufac- ture, or commerce of any kind, whether of a company or private individual, the production of the particular commodities or services con- cerned involves various expenses, such as capital, labour, B:c.' which together form the cost of production. The exchangeable values of these commodities or services represent this cost plus the profit obtained on the various transactions, which is the entrepreneur's re- muneration. But when we look at the matter from the human point of view, it assumes a. rather different aspect. Work, for instance, viewed from the employer's point of view, is all cost, because it has to be paid for. But from the human standpoint, some work is desirable because otherwise humanity would not fulfil its natural functions, and its development would be impossible. Such work as is •necessary for the satisfaction of human needs is perfectly natural, and is not cost from the human .point, of view. But work is of very varied kinds. Some work is of a highly skilled and interesting character, involving the exercise of an immense amount of intelligence, which of itself produces a certain amount of satisfaction, apart alto- gether from its financial reward. The work of the artist, save for a certain amount of effort unavoidably irksome, no doubt, though this is very often dependent on his temperament, is 'a pure joy to him. He must work, for his work is the expression of himself, to which he is im- pelled by the bent of his nature. This work is not cost to ,him. But to the machine-tender, who has to submit to a nerve-destroying routine, to the miner, the forgeman, and to all such workers, their work is almost all cost, and the more unhealthy the work is—by reason of its intensity, or the length of the working day— , the greater the human cost. But this does not complete the analysis. In estimating the cost of production, we have to consider the distribution of work and its form. Supposing that the work entailed in the pro- duction of the necessaries of life is so distri- buted that one portion of the community is engaged in work which involves a maximum of human costs, and the other portion is idle or contributes little towards the general effort needed, then the distribution, from the organic, or human, point of view, is bad and must be condemned. The cost falls too heavily on the one portion and too lightly on the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>PRODUCTION AND CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>PRODUCTION AND CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>In the past economists have devoted their attention to the productive arts of industry almost to the evlusion of the consumptive arts. But consumption is equally important as pro- duction. It is necessary, therefore, fpr the purposes of Mr. Hobson's human valuation to make an analysis, parallel to the analysis of production, of the consumptive processes, that is, into the distribution of these goods and services which constitute the national income. And the results show thict the present state of industrial society, judged by Mr. Ilobson's organic standard, would be condemned. We may quote a passage here which shows how some of the goods and services which figure in official iitittistics as "wealth" must be viewed from the humanist point of view : "A considerable proportion of the goods and services included in the real income which ire are analysing must, from our standpoint, be classed not as wealth, but as illth,' to adopt Ruskin's term. What proportion we should Place in the categor4 will, of course, depend upon the degree to which we hold that the actual evolution of the arts of consumption has been distorted from its 'natural' course. But everyone will ad-mit that many sorts of market- able goods and services are injurious alike to , the individuals who consume them and to society. A large proportion of the stimulants and drugs winch absorb a growing share a income •in many civilised cominunities, bad literature, art and recreation, the services of prostitutes and flunkeys, are conspicuous in- stances. Not merely .does no human utility correspond to the economic 'utility ascribed to such goods, but there is a large positive dis- utility. The aggregate human value of a grow-</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>TIM EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</head>
        <p>TIM EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</p>
        <p>the Chairman (Mr. ,Aylmer Frost) had not de- tailed to them so minutely what is being done at this splendid hostel of help and recuperation, where British Tommy and Belgian Pierre have been and are being nursed back to that health and strength of which enemy bullets have—tem- porarily only, it is to be hoped—deprived them. The sum of between £3O and £4O was raised, and it will prove most serviceable, for the hos- pital costs between ..t2O and &lt;R3O a week to run, and, so far, the finding of that money has been left to local public spirit—local gratitude and admiration for Tommy and his " loose-trousered parley-vooing pals."</p>
        <p>THE CHILDHOOD OF A GREAT HEROINE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ridgway began by reminding her hearers that the heroine of her lecture was born in the fair city of Florence, on the banks of the Arno, on May 12th, 1820, and went on to describe the Nightingale family circle—the father, a man of gentle pity and tender piety; the mother and the sister—and the childhood of the woman whose name is revered in every British house- hold. They were told, said the lecturer, that Florence was a sensitive and somewhat morbid child—grave and introspective, self-absorbed and shy. She once said, "If I Were sure nobody would observe me I should be quite happy." The home circle, not understanding the little girl's wistful yearning after something beyond her grasp, pronounced ,her a little difficult." Her childhood and young womanhood were passed in peaceful surroundings, and as she grew out of adolescence she heard the angels calling her to some special work for God. The lovable child became an elegant and distin- guished-looking woman, gifted in an extraordin- ary degree, and first charmed and then astounded those with whom she was brought into contact by her intellectual power. Although so well-fitted by nature and education to shine in the most cultivated society, though so grace- fully dowered by God with so many _gifts raid dwelling in a home of refined luxury; yet Flor- ence was not happy. THE CALL COMES TO THE YOUNG WOMAN. .</p>
        <p>Always, ever present, in her mind was the consciousness of the vocation which awaited her. She had herself spoken of a crisis in her inner life, which occurred early in 1837, when, she said, God called her to His service, and no longer could she be heedless. Then began her struggle for independence and liberty to act as she thought right; she was destined to be a pioneer, and, as with the majority of pioneers, obstacles beset her path. The lecturer pro- ceeded to deal with Miss Nightingale's earlier work—as the superintendent of an establish- ment for gentlewomen during illness, and later as superintendent of the nursing staff at the Kemp College Hospital—and then went on to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"THE LADY OF THE Lite."</head>
        <p>"THE LADY OF THE Lite."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ridgway's Lecture on Florence Nightingale.</p>
        <p>£3O FOR RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>" Florence Nightingale The words sound as music in our ears, and, in very truth, the life history of this noble-hearted woman was a sweet melody, with strong, dominant chords, and the melody still echoes in our hearts and still remains a precious heritage for gencrations yet unborn. Her life was a song that nerves the nation's heart. . . . It is not too much to say that the welfare . . . of the British Army to-day, and, incidentally, of the Navy also, are due to a masterly and importunate woman—a woman of commanding genius, who, behind the scenes, worked with firm resolve and iron will, until something real and tangible was the result. . . . The Lady of the Lamp—she stands in the great history of our land, a noble type of good, heroic womanhood."</p>
        <p>Thus, succinctly, did Mrs. Ridgway, of Lymm, sum up, in graceful phrase—for the benefit of a large and interested audience in. the Parr Hall on Monday evening—the attributes and achieve- ments of one of the greatest and sublimest of our national heroines, the indomitable pioneer of Red Cross work, whose labours in the great base hospitals, to which were brought the victims of the Russian guns before Sevastopol, the hurt and maimed from the deathless vharge of Balaclava, the disease-wrecked "phantoms of former men" from the icy, pestilential trenches of Inkerman, laid the foundations of that merci- ful system of succour that to-day has in a measure robbed war of, at least, some of its terrors. Yes, a lecture on the life and work of Florence Nightingale is peculiarly appropriate in these days—days when, once again in the his- tory of the most turbulent Continent, great nations are in death grips; days when once more the call goes forth for noble women—for "min- istering angels" to smooth out the pain-and anguish-wrung brow, to watch over and soothe the dying moment, to tend and cheer and re- hearten " the temporary victims. 'or high Prus- sian anger and Krupp's devilish ingenuty," as one expressive writer has phrased it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>OVER £2O A WEEK TO RUN RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>OVER £2O A WEEK TO RUN RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ridgway's lecture was given in aid of the Raddon Court Hospital, and that the cause was a worthy one everybody present knew, even if</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>directing her staff and attending the worst cases herself, and on one occasion she spent eight hours on her knees dressing wounds and administering comforts.</head>
        <p>directing her staff and attending the worst cases herself, and on one occasion she spent eight hours on her knees dressing wounds and admin- istering comforts.</p>
        <p>THE "QUEEN OF NURSES" GOES HER</p>
        <p>ROUNDS.</p>
        <p>The lecturer proceeded to paint a powerful word-picture of "the Lady with the Lamp" going her rounds among the pain-wracked sol- diers as they lay in the. bare wards—" she goes to the first bed and seeing that the sufferer sleeps, turns, a look of gratitude on her face, to the next bed. Here a man in agony is trying to stifle his groans. The lady puts down the lamp, .and with her own handkerchief wipes away the death-dew from the dying soldier's forehead. He looks up to her as at a goddess, a question in his eyes, which he finds himself scarcely able to articulate. Look, the nurse is on her knees, -bending over her patient. 'Tell mother!' says the dying soldier, then stops, unable to continue. 'Yes, I will,' says the comforter; 'l'll tell your dear mother how brave you were, and that her love and prayers have reached you even here, and that you will meet her again, and that she is not to grieve.' Into the eyes already dim comes a bright look . . . and with a smile on his face the soldier passes into the unknown:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>AMID THE DYING AND THE DEAD.</head>
        <p>AMID THE DYING AND THE DEAD.</p>
        <p>"The Lady-in-Chief gently smoothes the pillow, lays her hand on hisliead, and with a short, but inaudible prayer for the soul which has gone forth on its pilgrimage, takes up her lamp and passes slowly along the dreary ward, going to each bed in turn. She pauses at the bedside of a man, fever-wracked, and moistens the swollen blue lips, and the sympathy that gleams from her calm eyes finds an answering chord in the sufferer's heart Here a soldier clutches at her robe to make sure that she is of flesh and blood. There a man kisses her shadow as it flickers across his bed, thanking God for the vision of ministering love which makes even this</p>
        <p>place of horror a gateway of Paradise. . . . The self-sacrificing example of the Lady of the Lamp has been, and is being, followed gladly to-day by numbers of other women, whose great- est aim in life is to follow in the footsteps of the 'Queen of Nurses.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RARE MEMBER OF A RARE SECT.</head>
        <p>A RARE MEMBER OF A RARE SECT.</p>
        <p>" And whilst Florence Nightingale was doing this great work in the hospitals of the East," added the speaker, "small and narrow-minded people at home in England were quarrelling about her religious views. In the "Times" and " Standard " appeared letters attacking and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>KING AT THE FRONT. Tommy (having learned the language) : " Vive le .En</head>
        <p>THE KING AT THE FRONT. Tommy (having learned the language) : " Vive le .En</p>
        <p>:SIR JOHN .FRENCII 111 INOURED.</p>
        <p>• During the King's visit to his Army in the Field his Majesty was able to see practically all the Troops, except those actually in the trenches. _</p>
        <p>The King visited the Headquarters of all the</p>
        <p>Army Corps am! Divisional Commanders, and inspected the different departments of the General Headquarters. - .</p>
        <p>- His Majesty visited many of the base hosiii- tals, receiving -hospitals, and field hospitals. The King visited the King and Queen of the Belgians, and conferred upon his Majesty the</p>
        <p>describe how in the spring of 1854 England and France, bound closely together then, as declared war against Russia, who had already destroyed the Turkish fleet. The reports from the seat of war were heartrending—the scarcity of necessities, the thousands of men left nn- cared for, untended, dying in agony, amid filth and dirt indescribable—and they, coupled with the appeal for nurses sent to "The Times" by Wm. Russell, roused Florence Nightingale to action and she wrote offering her services in the hospitals at Scutari. This decision was reached on October 15th, and with characteristic energy and method she at once set to work, and in one week had her first contingent of nurses in marching order.</p>
        <p>'TI-1E FRUITS OF A GREAT EXAMPLE. From the time when Florence Nightindale set the example, women of birth and education had devoted themselves to the alleviation of suffering and to carinkfor the dying. Miss Nightingale was, if not the founder, the.promoter of women nursing. in time of war, and the Red Cross Socie- ties throughout the whole world were the direct outcome of her labours in the Crimea. Super- intendent of the Nursing Staff in the tast was the official designation under which Florence Nightingale set out on her mercy mission, but the, title by which she was universally known was that of Lady-in-Chief. - -</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ridgway' Went on to describe the hos- pital at Scutari as Florence Nightingale found it—"plague and cholera, patients lay by the side of the wounded; not even the common decencies or necessaries of life were to be had . . . and, greatest of horrors, vermin covered everything; rats overran the whole building and attackTl the sick as they lay in suffering help- lessness "—and how, under her wise administra- tion, a great change was wrought; how "in the appalling darkness and gloom, suddenly a woman appeared, and the silver lining to that black cloud became visible." Florence Nightin- gale was known to spend twenty hours at a time on the arrival of the sick, distributing stores,</p>
