      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NATIONAL WEALTH—continue.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL WEALTH—continue.</p>
        <p>lug national inccane may easily be reduced by any increase in the proportion of expenditure upon such classes of goods, and tendencies of distribution which lead to such proportionate increase may even invalidate the assumption that social welfare upon the whole grows with the growth .of the national dividend."</p>
        <p>_ The humanist analysis of consumption dis- closes the costs involved in the 'consumptive processes which are apt to be overlooked, and the application of the organic criterion to both Production and consumption, regarded as re- lated aspects of one general process, results in the conclusion that a. considerable portion of the goods and services, commonly included in estimates of the national income as "wealth," is, as we have seen, not wealth at all, but a heavy cost to society, and to be deducted from a humanist estimate of the national wealth. From the organic standard of welfare, Mr. Hobson deduces a human law of distribution which, in its application to industry, "aims to distribute wealth, in relation to its produc- tion on the one hand, and its consumption on the other, so as to secure the minimum of human costs and the maximum of human utility." This law would secure to each man a sufficient reward for the efforts which he puts forth (from the organic standpoint) on behalf of society. He may get more than will repay him for the actual human ct of his labour, hut, if so, this is surplu§, and it is in the gradual reduction, and possibly the absorp- tion, by the community as a whole, of the sur- plus (particularly the surplus element in the huge prefits o enceessful commercial men) that Mr Hpim.9o sees the holm of reform. Mr. Elubson's book is a remarkable one. We think it indicates the lines on which the most fruitful social criticism and constructive re- form in the innnethite future must proceed, and e recommend it most heartily to .all interested in the social problems of the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Order of the Garter.</head>
        <p>Order of the Garter.</p>
        <p>His Majesty conferred the Order of Merit upon Field-Marshal ir John French. While in FrallUe the King conferred the Vic- toria Cross, Distinguished Service Orders, and Distinguished Conduct Medals upon a number ,of officers, non-commissioned officers and men.</p>
