        <p>enough to make them miserable. That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no poison or narcotic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>CHRISTIANITY AND WAR.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND WAR.</p>
        <p>The Difficulty of Realising the Ideal.</p>
        <p>At the Frieds' Meeting House on Sunday evening Mrs, ,Edith. J. Wilson, of Birkenhead,</p>
        <p>gave a public address on "Christianity and War," there being a,fair attendance.</p>
        <p>THE CHRISTIAN'S SPECIAL DUTY.</p>
        <p>In the course of her remarks Mrs. Wilson said that the subject might be considered from at least three points of view, viz., things as they were to-day; things as they always had been, so far as history could tell; and things as they believed they would be—nay, were going to be— in the light of the Gospel message. They would extend their sympathy to those who had to deal with things as they were—the politicians and the statesmen and those who were obliged to take things just as they found them at the moment. They would realise what a difficult thing that was to guide affairs, from the ideal standpoint. The historian, the sociologist, the student of things as they had been, all pointed out what war had done down the ages, and, to sum it up, they said that, as _war always had been, so they could see no hope but that it would continue to play its part in the working, out of human affairs. But to her, it seemed to be the special duty of Christians, in the light of Christianity, to look at that question not in the light of things as they were, or had been, but from the point of view of things as they should be, and so looking, they stood convinced that war was one of the things that must pass away—the system of war, which had still a larger place in the affairs of the world than they thought a few short months ago, must be- come a thing of the past. Public opinion, on the matter of war, had already gone a great way beyond the stage when the working idea was: "They shall take who have the power, and they shall keep who can." That fact was exemplified in the present war, where they had the spectacle of a whole nation—their nation—fighting for the weak against the strong; for high ideals against low ideals; for justice against might. They could thank God for that moral stand- point, and they said that that standpoint hav- ing been reached, God had some further truth that he was ready to reveal to His people. Thankful as they might be for all attempts that had been made to make war more humane, for all ristraints that had been imposed upon its methods, for the fact that their own nation was fighting for high ideals, yet they did plead and urge that, viewed in the light of the Universal Spirit of God and the brotherhood of mankind, war stood condemned. TIME MANKIND FOUND AN ALTERNATIVE.</p>
        <p>After nineteen hundred years of the Christian message, it was time that men and nations found some other method of adjusting their differences than the terrible slaughter that was going on to-day. As members of the Society of Friends, they said that in the light of God's teaching, revealed in Christ, they felt that war was wrong; that it was contrary to the will of God. People told them that when they took that stand they were neither logical in their arguments nor con- sistent in their lives. There was a great deal of truth in that, for the world in which they lived was so complicated that it was, in many ways, impossible to live up to the very highest that they knew. The fact of the matter was that the great failure had come in because in that great environment of mankind—that en- vironment of his fellow man—no such progress had been made as had been made in the great field of Nature. In their human relationships they were back again in primitive times; they did not trust each other. True they had learned a certain amount of mutual trust, but it was not complete yet. They had not learned how to adjust their human relationships; they had not dared to apply the great forces of love, brotherhood and co-operation. Instead, they laid it down that one-half of the nation should manage the other half, if they got enough votes; that one section of the population should govern and control the remainder of the people, if they had got more wealth; and that one nation should dominate the other nations if it had got more machine guns, more ships and more men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>HIGHER RELATIONSHIPS.</head>
        <p>THE HIGHER RELATIONSHIPS.</p>
        <p>They had not yet realised the higher relation- ships of life, but the time was coming when God's great laws of love, kindness, mutual un- derstanding, fellowship and brotherhood would le introduced. She believed that there was waiting for the human race as wide and as vast a field of development in the laws of human re- lationship as had been discovered in the laws of Nature. If only the same measure of in- genuity, courage; patience and sacrifice that had been put into discovery, invention and the pur- suit of knowledge, were put into the study of human relationships, there could be no telling what a glorious time was in store for the world. If only they could get rid of all that stood in the way of higher progress; if they could shake themselves free from shackles such as the pres- ent war—which stood before them as a great proof of their failure—there was before them a wealth of beautiful experience such as no words could describe.</p>
      </div>
