      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>Scrappy and Uninteresting Play at Wilderspool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>AFTERNOON OF LOST OPPORTUNITIE2.</head>
        <p>AFTERNOON OF LOST OPPORTUNITIE2.</p>
        <p>Fresh from their triumph over Batley, Swinton came to Warrington on Saturday. They came, they saw, they strove, but they did not conquer; instead, they returned home beaten by five clear points, after what was, Perhaps, the most scrappy and uninteresting game that has been seen at Wilderspool this season. Maybe local flthusiasta scented a Wretched exposition of football, or maybe, on the other hand, they found the fireside more attractive than the rope-side on a bleak, chilly afternoon, with a nose-reddening, toe-nipping tang in the air; but, whatever the cause, the "gate" was a small one, barely a thousand People "dubbing up" the price of admission at the turnstiles. Those who did face the uncomfortably-wintry coldness found little in the play to set their blood a-coursing, but, on the contrary, they got a Most depressing game —a game that gave one hardly an incident to enthuse over, but was ofttimes monotonous in big streaks, and, taken all through, was of singularly poor quality.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON START WITH THE WIND. A useful wind swept down the field, and, winning the toss, Warrington elected to take advantage of it whilst they were fresh. Soon after the start they were in the visitorg' quar- ters, and Clare was giving the opposition some- thing to think about; something to sit up and take notice of. Twisting and dodging, he made his way to Swinton's " 25," and then passed to Taberner, who, however, was ta.ckled before he had a proper chance to carry on the good work. Just afterwards the Warrington for- wards got going and they forced the ball right up to the Swinton line before they were re- pulsed. Thereafter came a brief spell of un- exciting work near the half-way line, until Young infused something like excitement into matters by taking the ball down-field on his own. However, he and the leather soon parted company, and Warrington were penalised for off-side. Dawson provided a few mild thrills by heading a run from the Warrington line, but he was nilht allowed to get near enough to be really dangerous. The next incident of note was provided by Tranter, who embarked on a daring individual effort, which promised well until Frodsham put the brake on. Taberner gave his side a chance to get going, but they were too slow in following up his hefty kick.</p>
        <p>AN UNFORTUNATE FORWARD PASS. Brook-es raised high the hopes of the home supporters when he got away with the leather firmly pressed to his ample bosom, but he spoilt his effort by a forward pass to Taberner, a good chance thus being lost. Play then went to they other end of the field, where Leah and Board- man became conspicuous, but were pulled up ere they had had time to make the defenders feel anxious. A free-kick was awarded to War- rington. Taberner took it, but the goal-posts were not wide enough for him by a long chalk, so he missed. Play being taken into War- rington's territory, Young gave a sample of his best and brightest play, and got the ball up to the "25" line before he was forced to part up. The home team hovered for some minutes in . the vicinity of the Swinton citadel, but at this stage Frecliahavan., was playing. a safe game. Jolley, in attempting to feed his forwards, more than once misjudged the strength of the wind. Bradshaw livened matters up with a fine dash on the right wing, but lie allowed the ball to roll into touch.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>Undismayed, the same player immediately afterwards again tried conclusions with the defenders, and again smart tackling brought him low.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>-BUT UNSUCCESSFULLY.</head>
        <p>-BUT UNSUCCESSFULLY.</p>
        <p>At this stage of the game the Swinton for- wards were working hard for points, and War- rington had all their work cut out to hold them at a safe distance, but they successfully accomplished their task. Dainteth broke through the visitors' ranks and swung the ball over. Tranter received it and took it across the half-way line, but lost the chance that pre- sented itself by preferring to kick rather than to pass on. For some minutes or so play hov- ered in the vicinity of the visitors' citadel, but the defence proved impenetrable, and at length a free-kick awarded to Swinton sent play across the dividing mark. The ball soon travelled back again, however, and for the remainder of the time play was mostly confined to the visi- tors' half. Some keen tackling was seen on both sides, and just before the close Jolley dis- tinguished hinaSelf by a fine piece of individual work. When the call of time came—and on the whole nobody was desperately sorry when it did come—there had been no alteration in the half-time score, victory, therefore, resting ' with Warrington by five points to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE PLAYERS PLAYED.</p>
        <p>Warrington, deserved their win, though it has to be confessed that, taken on the whole, their exhibition was nothing to go into ecstasies over. Still, Swinton's was even more 'mediocre./ One or two brilliant pieces of indi- vidual play stood out in contrast to the general deadly-dullness, as, for instance; when Brookes broke through in the first half, and when Young "sold the dummy" to Bailey, and by so doing enabled Bradshaw to score the only try of the match. Jolley played a mixed game; new good, now bad. In the first half he kicked dead four times, but he made amends by pul- ling up more than a few ugly rushes, whilst he was also responsible for one or two clever runs. Young, too, played a fluctuating game, and Dainteth was anything but his usual self. The threequarter line as a whole were below par, Taberner being the only one of the quartet who came anywhere near to shining. In the van Clare did a lot of useful work, but Critch- ley and Mather Were also handy men. Swin- ton's forwards worked hard, but their oppon- ents were too heavy for them, and they also lacked support from their rear lines; indeed, it was in the back ranks that the visitors' chief weakness lay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>WARRINGTON "A " ALSO WIN. Try-to-Nit Victory Over SN't inton's</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " ALSO WIN. Try-to-Nit Victory Over SN't inton's</p>
        <p>Second String.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" went to Swinton•on Satur- day, and, after a strenuously contested game, ran out victorious by the narrowest possible margin, viz., a try to nil. The muddy state of the ground made the going hard, and, incident- ally, also made the ball greasy and difficult of control. The Swintonians started with a dash and found the visitors' defensive lines plenty to V.in the first few minutes, but they proved equal to the task imposed uptin them. The attacks lacked method and finish, but neverthe- less, the Warrington line was nearly Crossed more than once. The first half was unproduc- tive of points, but it was not without its moments of excitement. After changing ends Warrington put more spirit into their play and had a fair share of the attacking, but it was not until the end of the game was within sight that they managed to secure the only points of the afternoon, Baker, their right wing, getting over and registering a try that went unim- proved.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. WARRINGTON v. BARROW. Warrington journey to Barrow to-clay. This will be the second meeting between the Wilders- pool lads and the Barrovians this season, the first encounter—a Lancashire Cup-tie—ending in a win for Warrington by 10 points to nil. The following have been selected to represent Warrington. Jolley. Bradshaw, Tranter, Brookes, Taberner. Dainteth, Young. Cox, Cunliffe, Mather, Green, Fairhurst, Clare. RYLANDS' RECS. v. PENDLETON. At Pendleton : Rylands' Rees.: F. Horrocks; J. Griffiths, A. Horrocks, H. Povey and C. Povey; G. Warren and J. Gallop; J. Brandwood, J. Whittaker, F. Potter, E. Smith, W. Shaw and F. Baguley. Reserve, A. Rowlinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. WIGAN HIGHFIELD. This match was played on Saturday at Wigan, before a fair attendance. Wigan Highfield, win- ning the toss, elected to play with the wind and up the brow. Rylands, who were short of 11. Povey, kicked off, Nicholls returning. Wigan began to press, and Banks kicked a goal. After this reverse the Warrington men attacked, but they could not break through. 'llighfield's heavier forwards controlled the pack, and en- abled their half-backs to keep their threequar- ters on the run. Banks again scored and Nicholls goaled. Shortly afterwards good play by Nicholls ended in him scoring under the posts. He also added the extra points. half- time arrived with the score: Wigan Highfield. 3 goals 2 tries (12 points); llyiands' Highfield-commenced the second liajf. BYlands' Pressed and Smith passing to Potter, that Player scored, Warren adding the extra points. Good passing among the Bighfiellu's snd ml in a try, the goal kick failing. Rylands' forwards were at this juncture playing a good game, but they were beaten in the matter of weight. From a forward rush close in. Atherton scored at the corner, the goal kick failing.- From the STi6C- tators' point of view, it was a grand match, the passing being excellent, despite the fact that a gale was blowing. The final score read: Wigan ifighfield, 3 goals 4 tries (18 'points); Ryfands* Recs., 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. D. L. For. Ag't. Ps Dolton C. 0.8. St. James' 0.8. Parochial 0.8. _</p>
        <p>Wycliffe 0.8. Beamont U. 0.8. Froghall 0.13. 7 4 0 3 61 23 8 Sacred Heart 0.8. St. Mary's 0.8. Arpley-street 0.8. Silver-street 0.8. St. Ann's 0.13. Beamont Ath. 0.8 5 2 0 3 16 81 4 St. Barnabas' 0.8. St. Alban's "B" 0.8 6 1 0 5 24 137 2 St James' Jun. 0.8 6 1 0 5 40 148 2 Heatbside Bewsey 0.8. 6 0 0 6 2 195 0 St. Alban's "A" 0.8 7 0 0 7 0 318; 0</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS. Bolton 0.13., 14; St. Barnabas' 0.8., 0. Sacred Heart 0.8., 8; St. Mary's 0.13., 3. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>- . • 2.15 p.m.—Paroclual 0.8. N . Wycliffe 0.B.; referee, Mr. W. Bibby. 3.15 p.m.—St. Alban's "A" 0.8. V. Heart 0.B.; Mr. F. Heesom.</p>
        <p>Sacred</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park. Kick-off at 2.30 p.m. St. Ann's 0.8. v. Heathside Froghall 0.B.; Mr. J. Beardsell.</p>
        <p>Arpley:street 0.8. V. St. Mary's 0.B.; Mr. J. Flynn.</p>
        <p>Beamont United 0.8. v. Beamont Athletic 0.B.; Mr. J. Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>WARRINGTON HAVE THE LION'S SHARE OF PLAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HAVE THE LION'S SHARE OF PLAY.</p>
        <p>At this stage Warrington were doing most ' of the attacking, but they could not penetrate . the Swinton defence, mainly owing to the lack of co-operation which marked their efforts. Kilgarry managed to break through the on - pressing cordon and he swung the ball across to Holland, who, however, lost it, Jolley gath- ering it up and making considerable headway before the player who had dropped it made amends by tackling the sturdy home back and recovering it. Warrington gathered for a great effort, but it was nipped in the bud. Dainteth and Young repeatedly got their threequarters on the move, but the passing of that line was lamentably slow and the good work of the halves was in vain. Leah infused some life into the Swinton threequarter line and made a mark, which •proved handy, in that it re- lieved a difficult situation for his side. Tranter, -Tolley, and Taberner figured in a neat passing Movement, which secured ground for Warring- ton, but yielded little real advantage, as the ball was soon back again at the other end of the field.</p>
        <p>HOW THE ONLY TRY WAS SCORED. A free-hick which was awarded to Warring- ton was entrusted to Taberner, who, using fine judgment, kicked a splendid goal. Hardly had the players got going again when Swinton were penalised for "feet up," and Trauter found touch in the home "25." Swinton were being hard pressed, when Dawson and Board- Man brought them relief and took the ball well into Warrington's territory. However, it did not stay there long, Jolley transferring with a mighty kick. Dainteth took up the running With a splendid dribble, but he was deprived, and clare next came into prominence for a neat bit of dodging, but, on the whole, War- rington were putting up a very moderate show, several good chances being allowed to go a-begging. A free-kick awarded the home men, for obstruction, was taken by Taberner, but once again he shot wide of the mark. The only try of the match came just before half- time. Young raced away and passed to Brad- thaw, who got off at a fine pace on the right wing, and, eluding the grasp of several out- stretched hands, crossed over. Taberner was entrusted with the goal-kick, and, once again, his marksmanship was bad. When the time came for changing ends the score-board read : Warrington, 1 goal 1 try (5 points); Swinton, _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>SWINTON TRY HARD-</head>
        <p>SWINTON TRY HARD-</p>
        <p>With the wind in their favour Swinton put i More life into their play n the opening stages of the second half. With a fine kick Kilgarry sent the ball to the Warrington "25," and smart following-up by the Swinton forwards nearly caught Jolley napping. - However, help Was at hand, and, between them, the War- rington defenders managed to 'keep their lines Intact, though Leah came very near to scoring, being brought down just in the nick of time 133' Dainteth. At this stage of the game War-1 rington-were getting possession from the pack, but scrambling play and the slippery state of the ball and the heavy condition of the turf all mitigated against anything like brilliant Play. Taberner got off from a scrum, and, Misjudging his pass, sent the ball to Frodsham, Who, however, knocked on. Thereafter followed a spell of comparatively uminteresting and inci- dentless play, matters only livening up when the Swinton forwards, by a concerted effort, forced the leather into Warrington's half, Where Dawson tried to break through, only to , find the Warrington tackling too keen for him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>THE SEASON ENDING.</p>
        <p>The season 1914 is now drawing to a close with specials yet to be won. The set board has some very good specimens marked up, yet there is still a good chance of one or two specials being won before December 31st. This is the first year we have been open until the end of the year, and it is to be hoped that members will take advantage of the privilege during the Christmas holidays. . _</p>
        <p>The Cooper's Arms has now opened a list for members during 1915. There are already about 25 on the books. At a recent meeting the club elected Mr. H. Hope president, Mr. C. Loy treas- urer, and Mr. A. Swarbrick secretary. Vice- presidents up to the present are Messrs. W. Ashton, W. Patrick, E. Brookes, F. Barber, G. Hamblett, and M. J. Cox. Meeting nights, third Monday in every month. The secretary will .postcard all members on the first meeting night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>N.A.A. GENERAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>N.A.A. GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>At this meeting in the Junior Conservative Hall, Market-place, there were about 200 pres- ent. Mr. Atherton, of Wigan, took the chair. The Secretary's report of the year's working pointed out that the membership had increased by 1,000, and the Association was quite sound in finance. The Gowy had been a great advantage to Warrington and Chester members. He ad- vised them to transfer the defence fund to the general fund, as the defence fund had served its purpose. At the same time he assured the mem- bers that they would always be defended by the N.A.A. in cases where it was required. The balance sheet was then put before the meeting, and, after one or two items was explained to the members, it was passed unanimously. The suggestion of the Secretary as to the de- fence and general funds was carried. The membership now stands at 8,081; the balance in the Manchester and County Bank is .1:74 2s. Bd.; and the defence fund account ..tBB ss. 6d.. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Lord (Bolton) expressed the opinion that the increase of membership was due to the N.A.A. coming into line with other societies with the close season question. Mr. Lees (Oldham) raised the close season question, but nothing came of his effort that will interest Warrington Members, and we will yet have to wait results of the F.F.P.L. to see what steps they are going to take with our Warrington anglers. The delegates and officials Eat down to tea at A tkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street, and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Warrington Centre and their patrons for the kind and gen- erous manner in which they provided for the N.A.A. at the big meeting and the officials afterwards.</p>
        <p>Club night at headquarters, Gladstone. Arms, on Monday next. The secretary will be down to receive specials and subscriptions not yet come in. The winners of the specials will be Published at the end of the season, as marked up on the set board. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. St. Barnabas' v. Newton Cons.; referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. _</p>
        <p>Hollins Green v. Bank Quay Recs.; Mr. G. Knight. Orford White Star v. Fairfield Athletic; Mr. J. Hadkiss.</p>
        <p>Paddington v. Earlestown Y.M.C.A.; Mr. R. G. Brandreth. Fairfield United v. Elmwood Cons.; Mr. L. Thompson. Longford United v. Earlestown St. John's Mission; Mr. J. Smith. NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. • Stockton Heath v. Partington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ITEMS.</p>
        <p>Fairfield United gained a couple of valuable points at Hollins Green. They have got a new right winger in Clayton, who scored a goal which was declared offside. _</p>
        <p>Tasker missed the train, and Clayton filled the vacancy. The United have now got a four points' lead over their rivals—the White Star—in the League chart. The White Star lost a point at Newton. Stubbs scored with a great shot for the White Star.</p>
        <p>Tithers defended finely when playing agains. the wind. . _</p>
        <p>Caldwell was missing from the ranks of the Stars.</p>
        <p>Devonport, in goal for the "Saints, against the Mission Lads, was in great form. On the general run of the game the Saints" did not deserve a point. Shaw gave a penalty which enabled the Mission to equalise. Challinor and Aldridge played well in their respective positions. Vernon signalised his re-appearance by scor- ing a grand goal. If the spectators at Hollins Green do not behave differently they may get their club into trouble.</p>
        <p>Cleworth was in greAt for it t•entie•half.</p>
        <p>Woodhouse defended finely for the 17nited.</p>
        <p>Latchford • Baptists played their second team against the Central. • Wilkinson gave a good display in goal. Mason was the pick of the Central team. Paddington had to acknowledge defeat from Longford by a penalty kick. Chamberlain proved himself the best half- back on the field. Wrench was the best forward on the visitors' side. Longford, on their play, did not deserve both points. Elmwood Cons. created the greatest surprise last week. _ . .</p>
        <p>They actually scored six goals! But three were disallowed. Hard lines for Ashton! Holt transferred his services from the Cen- tral to the Cons., and he gave a splendid exhi- bition for them. • • •</p>
        <p>His good work must have given his colleagues encouragement. Stockton Heath snatched a mint from Mackennall's Institute, the North Cheshire League leaders. "'Pommy" Smith missed a penalty kick. Starkey also missed scoring when in front of goal. Lloyd. Starkey and Chesters were the pick of the "Heathens." Cowsgill and Skelhorne were the best for the Institute.</p>
        <p>The "Heathens" have now missed scoring from penalty kicks in three successive matches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <p>CLARKE'S B 41 PILLS can be relied upon to cure in either sex all acquired or constitutional dis- charges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established up- wards of 50 years. In boxes 4s. Gd. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throughout the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers— The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Co., Lin- coln.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <p>One of the keenest contests was that decided on the Hollins Green enclosure, where the League leitaers were the visitors. They were unfortunate in having to play withoat Mill- ward, owing to him missing the train. The United, in the ,first portion, had the wind at their backs, but they failed to adopt them- selves to the conditions of the day, and the home defenders prevented them scoring. In the second half the game was in favour of the home team, but they blundered in the same manlier as their opponents had previously done. The United only paid occasional visits to the home end, and, from a free-kick. Cole managed to score a splendid goal. This was not received in a very sportsmanlike manner by a section of the onlookers, and it was with some difficulty that the home officials and players subdued their excitement. Under the conditions the visitors may be considered a little fortunate in gaining the verdict by the one goal scored.</p>
        <p>The Orford White Star visited Newton Cons. and the home team had the benefit of the gale of wind in the first half and scored twice. After the interval the wind abated somewhat and the game was more evenly contested. Dagnall, the home custodian, was unfortunate in placing the ball from a corner-kick through his own goal, and the equaliser Was a fine effort by Stubbs. Both teams were afforded chances of gaining the lead before the finish, Meachin having a very easy opportunity, with only half a minute to go. Everything taken into consid- eration, the result—two goals each—was a fair criterion of the game.</p>
        <p>Earlestown Mission had a visit from the St. Barnabas' team, and another drawn game was the result. The visitors held the lead until a few minutes from time, when Shaw gave.am ay a penalty kick, from which the Mission equal- ised. The visitors have to thank Devonport, their custodian, for being able to share the points, as he had three times as much work as Massey to do, and he came out of the ordeal N:ith great credit.</p>
        <p>The match on the ground behind the Horse and Jockey, between Longford United and Paddington, was another close affair, Longford gaining the verdict from a penalty-kick a few minutes from time. The visitors were not at full strength, and Wrench had to turn out at the last minute. The visitors gave a better display all round than the United and did not deserve to lose, but it is the goals that collar the points in the League table.</p>
        <p>The Elmwood Cons, are to be congratulated on their victory over Fairfield Athletic. They made no mistake about it, for they put the ball between the uprights six times, although three times the point was disallowed. The home team only responded once. In Holt the Cons. appear to have mtide a splendid capture, for his display in goal was, in a great measure, the means of their success, as up till now the goal has been the weakest part of the team.</p>
        <p>At length the Latchford Baptists have suc- ceeded in turning out a team in the League, and on Saturday they were visited by the Central. At the close cif play the visitors had the bigger share of the four goals scored.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath paid a visit to the champions of the North Cheshire League—Mackennall's Institute—and were successful in making a drawn game of it. This was all the more creditable to the "Heathens" from the fact that they were not able to put their full strength in the field. Also, another penalty- kick was missed, this being the third in suc- cession. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Goals,—</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Ps. Fairfield United Orford White Star Longford United St. Barnabas' lO 5 2 3 19 12 13 Earlestown St. J. M 10 5 3 2 26 15 12 Paddington Bank Quay Recs. Newton Cons. Earlestown Y.M.C.A 9 4 4 1 22 18 9 Hollins Green Warrington Central ll 3 7 1 17 32 7 Fairfield Athletic Elmwood Cons. Latchford Baptists</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN v.•FAIRFIELD UNITED. These teams opposed each other on Saturday on the ground of the first-naiiied club, and, owing to the fact that the Unitrd are at the head of the League there was a good muster of spectators, despite the unpropitious elements. The following players turned out : Hollins Green: Ellame; Atherton and Clarke; Buckley, Cleworth and Mellor; Atkinson, Bil- lington, Martin, Taylor and Wright. United : Potter; Cox and Woodhouse; Gleave, Lewis and Bebbington; Clayton, Warburton, Allen, Hunter and Cole.</p>
        <p>The referee was Mr. G. Bomber. The visitors won the toss and played with the wind and slope in their favour. Martin set the ball rolling and the game opened very keenly, the homesters soon being put on the defensive. The visitors, however, failed to completely adapt themselves to the conditions, and the home backs had not much difficulty in repuls- ing their efforts, Ellams in the home goal not having any particularly difficult shots to deal with. The home forwards improved in their tactics, but Woodhouse and Cox proved too good for them and drove them back to their own half. The visitors then applied pressure on the home goal, and Clayton put the ball through, but the referee judged him to be off-side, and the point was disallowed. The home defence held out, , and at the interval there was no score.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the home forwards quickly be- came aggressive, and some exciting tussles in the visitors' half were witnessed. However, Woodhouse and Cox defended stubbornly. The homesters played better with the wind than their opponents had done, and it was only by an occasional breakaway that the United be- came dangerous. In one of these rushes they were awarded a free kick, and the ball being well placed, Cole gained possession and was success- ful in scoring. The homesters made strenuous efforts to get on equal terms, but, owing to ex- citement, they missed several openings. Result : Fairfield, 1 goal; Rollins Green, nil.</p>
        <p>NEWTON CONS. V. ORFORD WHITE STAR. These clubs met on Saturday on the Cons.' ground, when, owing to the rough elements, there were ony a few spectators present. The following players turned out : Newton Cons.: Dagnall; Lawrance and Tithers ; Lennox, Clark and Aitken; Jones, Dodd, R. Clark, Rubotham and Gleaves. White Star: Millar; Pilling and Evans; R. Gifford, Denny and Gregory; J. O'Day, Meachin, Stubbs, Dunbobbin and Hankinson. Mr. J. Smith was the referee. The home captain won the toss and played with the very strong wind in his favour. From the kick-off the game was contested chiefly in the visitors' half, Pilling and Evans kicking well against the wind. and Millar making several good clearances in goal before he was defeated with a grand effort by Dodd, the home centre.: forward. The ball hit the underside ef the cross-bar, and rebounded through the goal. From</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S MISSION v. ST. BARNABAS'.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the Mission ground on Saturday in boisterous weather, and there were not many spectators present when the follow- ing players turned out : M ission : Massey; Winders and James; Rodgers, Aldridge and Theobald ; Wallas, G. Jones, Walker, Sherlock and Plumpton. St. Barnabas' : Davenport; Jefferson and Long; O'Neil, Challinor and Shaw; Oakley, Sherring- ton, Barrett, Vernon and Ray. Mr. W. Dennett was the referee. Walker kicked off for the home team, and for fully ten minutes the visitors were kept on the defensive. The Mission forwards were ably assisted by their half-backs, and kept up a con- tinuous attack. Davenport gave a clever dis- play between the posts, and in order to save a fast, low shot from Sherlock, fell at full length , across the goal and turned the ball round the post. The resultant corner kick was cleared, but directly afterwards another dangerous raid was foiled by off-side. This brought relief to the Saints, and their forwards paid a visit to the home quarters:' Massey was called upon to rave, but he had not much difficulty with the shots, and cleared easily. Even play followed and no score had been registered at the inter- val. ,</p>
        <p>On resuming the visitors at once made fracks for the Mission goal, but Winders and James played a sound game and drove them back. Not to be denied, however, the visitors gained ground by, good play, Sherrington being prominent in initiating the movement, but the ball was played in touch. Following the throw-in Vernon got possession with only the goalkeeper to beat, and he availed himself of the opportunity, Massey having no chance to save. This reverse roused the Mission lads and they at once made a raid on the visitors' goal, which several times came within an ace of being captured. In one of these attacks Shaw handled the ball inside the penalty area, and Jones equalised from the re- sultant kick. Both teams now struggled desper- ately to gain the lead. Massey ra,ri out to clear when his backs were beaten, and at the opposite end the homesters failed to beat Davenport. Result: Earlestown St. John's Mission, 1 goal; St. Burnabas', 1 goal.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD UNITED v. PADDINGTON.</p>
        <p>This match was played on Saturday on the ground of the former. The following players</p>
        <p>ined out:—</p>
        <p>Longford: Sturgess; Lawrance and liason ; Holden, Stevens and Fearnley • Richards, Sher- ratt, Bennett, Hands and Hughes. Paddington: Harrison; Holmes and Spencer ; Holmes, Taylor and Chamberlain; 'Brown, Car- nett, Wrench, Smitli and Pickering. The referee was Mr. B. Howell. After the opening exchanges, the visit Ors assitmed the aggressive, but Mason was con- spicuous with good defen'sive play. The home forwards invaded the visitors' territory, but off- side against Richards spoiled the movement, and the free kick enabled the visitors to lead up another attack. Wrench and Garnett combined well together and Brown received a well-jut-4M pass, but Mason robbed him of the ball. Richards received again when in an off-side position, and from the free kick Wrench gained the ball, and, after mamovring for position, passed the leather out to the left, where Picker- ing raced down, but eventually ran the ball over the line. When the interval arrived no score had been made. On resuming, the homesters showed to better advantage and Sherratt and Bennett worked the leather down into the visitors' quarters. There Chamberlain intercepted the ball and drove it well up the field, but the home de- fenders easily returned it. Richards sent across a splendid centre from the right, but the effort was spoilt by Sherratt fouling Harrison. the visitors' custodian. The visitors' hale-backs were playing a strong, bustling game, and upset the tactics of the home forwards, Chamberlain being the most prominent. During the closing stages of the game both teams made efforts to secure a deciding point. A few minutes before the end Sherratt had possession of the ball, when he was brought down by one of the visiting backs inside the area. The resultant penalty was entrusted to Stevens, who made no mistake with the kick, and thus gained the verdict for the home team. Result: Longford United, I goal; Paddington nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS v. WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS v. WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>The above clubs were represented by the fol- lowing players on Saturday, on tfie ground of the first-named :</p>
        <p>Latchford: Wilkinson; Walker and Jackson; Iron, Bradbury and Smith; Homey, Fenton, Starkey, Bradbury and Wright.• Central: Williams; Glover and Emson ; Goulden, Williams and Brown; Farrell, Hands, Holmes, Collin and Mason. The referee was Mr. M. J. Kite.</p>
        <p>The home team were not fully represented. They won the toss and started with a strong wind in their favour, and Fenton ran over the goal line. From the goal kick Farrell secured and raced away, but was robbed by Walker when in a good position for opening the score. Owing to good play by Collins and Mason, the visitors kept up the attack on the home goal, and the latter registered the first goal after twenty minutes' play. The home team then forced the game, and gained several corners, but failed to turn them to advantage. Eventually, however, Starkey equalised the score with a , long shot, Williams allowing the hall to pass over the line. At half-time the score board registered one goal each. On resuming, the visitofs quickly took up the offensive, and the home goal WAS hotly bom- barded, and further goals were secured for them by Glover (from a penalty kick) and Mason. The efforts of the home team were easily frus- trated by the visitors' half-backs and backs, Wilkinson. in goal, Walker, Wright and J. Bradbury being the pick of the Baptists, whilst the Central were best represented by Glover, Mason and Farrell. Result: Central 3 goals; Baptists,. 1 goal.</p>
        <p>-FAIRFIE-LD ATHLETIC V. ELMWOOD CONS.</p>
        <p>The above teams opposed each other on the Orford-road ground, the following players lining up:— Fairfield : Warburton; Johnson and Webb; Leigh, Challinor and Longhorn; Lawless, Roberts, Short, Roberts and Bates. Elmwood Cons.: Holt; Joe Phoenix and Jas.</p>
        <p>Phcenix; Pritchard, Mellor and Finch; W. Phcenix, Ashton, Kendrick, Smith and Wells. The referee was Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. The Cons. won the toss and Roberts set the ball rolling. Kick and rush tactics were adopted and the Cons. did most of the pressing. Kendrick shot wide, and the goal-kick gave the home defence momentary relief, but the Cons. were soon pressing again and Finch scored a splendid goal. Following this reverse the Athletic endeavoured to get on equal terms, but missed several openings. They kept up the pressure and Holt made several good saves and conceded a corner, which was taken by Lawless; but the Brothers Phcenix cleared the danger away, and, from a free-kick, the Cons, increased their lead through Ashton, who headed the ball between the uprights. The home team now played up keenly and attacked strongly, but Holt performed very well between the sticks, although he conceded corners to clear. The Cons.' forwards besieged the home goal, where Warburton bad to bestir himself. Ashton secured the third goal for the Cons. On resuming the visitors soon became aggres- sive, but were driven back by means of free- kicks for offside. Fairfield improved in their game, but owing to over-keenness thy missed several good openings. Nevertheless they con- tinued to have the best of the game and Holt was called upon to handle many times. Short scored Fairfield's only goal. The game con- tinued mostly in favour of the home team, but Holt and his backs defended well; and, at the end, Short missed a very easy chance of scoring again. Result: Elmwood Cons., 3 goals; Fair- field Athletic, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
