      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 12, 1914.</p>
        <p>I .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>MERRY FARCE AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>"A rollicking, boisterous comedy," says one of the posters advertising "Three of a Kind." It is all that—and a bit more; it is a positive riot of mirth and merriment—a two-hour-long scream of laughter. Small wonder is it that packed houses lidve been the order of the week. The fun, not the plot, is the thing in a produc- tion of this kind, and of fun Mere is a pleni- tude. But there is, also, a plot, and all ingen- ious one at that. It revolves round an iacnti- fication puzzle, and is ingeniously conceived. Young Tom Flarup—a good cognomen that for ' the scapegrace of a jolly farce !—has been cut off with a reduction on the proverbial shilling by his chutney-tempered father, and he attempts to re-ingratiate himself by disguising himself with a ruddY wig and even ruddier whiskers, and "pitching the tale." Unfortu- nately for his little plan, two others of the same cast of countenance and with simiiar facial adornments (save the nark!) make their appearances at the most inopportune moments, and, not only "upset his apple-cart," but also greatly perturb the choleric old son of Mars, who is brought to the verge of distraction by the bewildering comings and goings of (ice ginger-haired trio. Still, the penultimate com- pensation is the old soldier's, for the gay de- ceivers bring down upon their own heads "a bundle of trouble," and 'ere the curtain drops it is a case of, to pervert a familiar proverb, "He laughs loudest who laughs last and has best cause for his chortles." Oh, aye! "Three of a Kind" is a cheersoine trifle, and if you aro in search of a merry-and-bright evening, don't miss it! The piece is played with a swing I/ a capable little company headed by Mr. George Abel as the peppery General, and Miss Ethel Arden as the lachrymose Mrs. Whimpers, who, true to her name, whimpers and wails all the way through the piece. The "Three of a Kind" are Tom Iflarup and Dick and Harry Whimpers, parts ably taken by Messrs. Frank Harris, Mr. H. P. Owen and Mr. W. West, respectively. Miss Elsie F. Hewitt, as Pollie Giggles, and Mr. F." Newman as Dr. Wagner, add largely, to the merriment, and Miss Jess Percival makes a vivacious Daisy Armitage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Wilson Barrett Drama Next Week.</head>
        <p>A Wilson Barrett Drama Next Week.</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. W. V. Garrod and his com- pany, which includes Miss Bertha Kingston, will appear in "A Wife for a Day," and "The Sledge Hammer." The first piece—a powerful melodrama—will be played, on Monday (after- noon and evening) and Wednesday, and the second on Thursday, Friday and Saturday. "The Sledge Hammer," which is translated from the Flemish, was introduced to England by the late Wilson Barrett, and scored a huge success. "A Wife for a Day" is from the pen of Mr. Garrod himself and contains a striking last scene, said by competent critics to be one of the best things in contemporary melodrama. There will be no performance on Tuesday even- ing, ,as tie theatre has been let for the purposes of a reeruiting meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>OLD FRIENDS AND NEW AT THE</head>
        <p>OLD FRIENDS AND NEW AT THE</p>
        <p>HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Local lovers of vaudeville renew their acquaintance with several old favourites at the Hippodrome this week, whilst those artistes who pay a first visit will be given a cordial reception when they come to Warrington again. Patrons of the Barbauld-street house have been convulsed with laughter on previous occasions by the quaint and wholesome comedy of Mr. George Lill, and although he intro- duces a large measure of familiar "business" in the comedy sketch, " Millions "—presented by Walter Bird's West End Company—he is, none the less, successful in keeping the audi- ence bubbling with merriment all the time he is on. He appears as a bogus heir to many millions of pounds, and the manner in which he receives his instructions from an adventurer and carries them out is extremely ludicrous. The bedroom scene is a " scream " fforn start to finish. J. H. Scotland, the popular basso and singer of humorous old " Zummerzet " and Irish dialect-songs, earns the ovation which he receives at each performance, and on the occasion of our visit he had to respond to an encore with "The Mountains of Mourne." The Comedy Meisters please in harmony and comedy and on Monday night responded to an encore with an excellent rendition of "Comrades in Arms." Randolph Sutton is a clever light comedian, who chooses his songs with taste, and renders them in a breezy fashion. Alan Martin and Walter O'Brien deal in ragtime, but the manner in which they present it is very different from the ordinary way, and much more acceptable. As an "extra" they sing "Get out and get under," with an appro- priate verse relative to the Kaiser. Rowlands and Ford are smart patter comedians, and The Zanonis entertain with a clever exhibition of club swinging and juggling. The war pictures are exceedingly good.</p>
        <p>Big Attraction for Next Week. What has undoubtedly proved one of the biggest sensations of the modern variety stage, viz., the morality playlet, "Should a Woman Tell?"—written by the Rev. A. J. Waldron. vicar 'of Brixton—has been secured at big cost as the premier attraction at the Palace and Hippodrome, Warrington, for next week. This playlet has created universal controversy, touching, as it does, upon one of the greatest social problems of the day. The pros and cons involved have been eagerly discussed in both London and provincial papers, wherever the playlet has been presented, and it is safe to</p>
        <p>say that no production has ever been reviewed to the extent Of this sketch, almost the entire Press of the United Kingdom being unanimous in praise of the Vicar's handling of such a delicate subject. The cast has been carefully selected and comprises such sterling artistes as Messrs. E. Story Gofton, Arthur Bawtree and Charles V. Sandford, and Miss Mabel Burrell. Other attractions in the hill include engage- ments of Johnny Dwyer, versatile comedian; Mdlle. Millie Dagmar, with performing dogs and pony; the Nicol Bros., comedy cyclists; Morris and Moore, step dancers; Rene Grahame, "the beau ideal boy," &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>" STAR " FILMS AT STAR KINEMA. The pictures shown during the week at this popular house have been as good as ever. The programme for the first three days included "The Char Lady," the amusing story of a wayward, husband. "The Training of the Italian Cavalry Officers" gave an instructive glimpse into the military life of one of the few European countries still enjoying "piping times of peace." whilst "Pathe's,. Animated Gazette" kept one in touch with all the latest war news. The "Song of the Sea" was illus- trated with a series of patriotic slides, and proved a great attraction. "The Sea Dog," which film occupied the premier place in the programme, was not only at once pathetic and interesting, but it also taught a valuable lesson. "Willie" id-lowed how a milksop learnt to he a man, by going to a ranch and living the rough-and-tumble life. The second excellent programme, concluding to-night, is well worth seeing. It includes " Woe to the Conquered" and a variety of other star-films.</p>
        <p>Next Week's Big Attractions. "The Dead Past Recalled" is the alluring title of the big film booked for the first three (lays next week, whilst the Star "star" attrac- tion at the week-end will be "A Million Bid.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>TO-NIGHT'S BELGIAN CONCERT.</head>
        <p>TO-NIGHT'S BELGIAN CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosiield and What He Will Play. A GREAT SUCCESS ASSURED.</p>
        <p>Our readers will be glad to know that the particularly attractive concert which Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield are giving to-night in aid of the Belgian Funds in Parr Hall, under the auspices of toe Working Men's Infirmary Committee, will be a great success, as tickets have sold remark- ably well for all parts of the house. Our representative saW Mr. Crosfield this week to ask him what pieces he himself would play. The programme simply states that he will give pianoforte selections. "My idea," said Mr. Crosfield, "at present is that my short selections might as well include a little of the grave and a littl6 of the gay, so I propose to start with a piece which is very grave indeed. It is a Polonaise by Chopin, which has a great deal more of the character of Beethoven than of Chopin's customary style. It is a piece containing not only pathos, but sombre grandeur, and so it bas occurred to me that it might not, be unsuited to the tragic events of these terrible times, especially as two remarkable passages positively seem to suggest the thundering of the guns." "And after that you thought of giving some- thing in the lighter vein?" "Precisely," said Mr: Crosfield. "I have been urged by way of contrast to try any hand at Mendelssohn's 'Bee's Wedding.' By •the way., though, Spinning Song' is the name sometimes given to this piece, which is, of course, one of Mendelssohn's series of songs without words. I know there are people who protest against any such descriptive names being given to a particular piece of music on the grounds that of all the charms of music, one of the greatest is the very fact that it suggests so much and appeals to the imagination in so many different ways. There is a great deal to be said for that point of view, but I certainly think there are exceptions, and this is surely one, because through the greater part of the piece you can almost hear the bees humming, with an extra buzz now and then when the queen bee goes in or comes out of the hive. If the Polonaise represents the present tragic times, let us hope the Bee's Wedding' is symbolic of the coming summer and the glorious peace we hope it is to herald." TORCHLIGHT PARADE AFTER THE</p>
        <p>CONCERT. , After the concert a monster torchlight pro- cession will be marshalled outside the Parr Hall, and, headed by Crosfield's Band, and detachments of local Boy Scouts, and members of other organisations, will parade the main streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>OUR BELGIAN FRIENDS.</head>
        <p>OUR BELGIAN FRIENDS.</p>
        <p>Sacred Recital at St. Mary's Church. A recital of sacred music was given in St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-street, on Thurs- day evening, the proceeds, raised by the sale Of programmes, going to the Belgian Refugees' Fund. There was a large congregation. The special feature was the singing of Haydn's magnificent Third Mass, the inspired music of which was rendered by an excellent choir, under the baton of Mr. J. Eckersley, and with the following as soloists: Mrs. Mac- beth (soprano), Mrs. Johnson (contralto), Mr. J. M. Bate ktenor), and Mr. J. W. Bibby (bass), Mr. W. J. Wallington being at the organ. Choir and principals gave a most praiseworthy and reverent rendition of the glorious music, the magnificent Benedictus and Angus Dei re- ceiving specially fine treatment. Mr. Wallington interpolated a quartet of organ solos—St. Saens' "Synod March," Wag- ner's " Trauersmarsch," Massenet's "The Last Sleep of Our Lady," and Beethoven's " Halle- lujah" (from "The Mount of Olives ")—and in each of the selections displayed the thor- oughness of his mastery over his instrument. After the recital came a short service of Benediction of the Blessed Sacrament. SACRED CONCERT AT ROYAL COURT. A high-class sacred concert in aid of the funds of the Belgian Red Cross Society will be given in the Royal Court Theatre to-morrow (Sunday) night. A splendid programme has been arranged, and among the contributors, who are sure of a specially warm welcome, will be: Mons. Jules Colbert (from the Grand Theatre of Verviers, Belgium), Mons. Ant De \Telly (first tenor from Theatre Royal, Ant- werp), and Mme. Helene Feltesse (prima donna of the Royal Opera House, Brussels). These talented Belgian artistes will be assisted by 311.. Iv. V. Garrod (the well-known actor, who will recite "The Day," by Chappell), Miss Elsie Wilkinson (who will sing "Land of Hope and Glory "), Miss Sara Silcock, and Mrs. E. A. Sanders. An orchestra of about twenty per- formers, under the direction of Mr. James Roy, will play the overture from the opera " Mirella 4" the ballet "Egyptian," and the National Anthems of the Allies. The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peacock), Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and Mrs. Smith, Mr. A. IL Cros- field, and Mrs. Crosfield and party have pro- mised to attend. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>THIN BLOOD AND INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>THIN BLOOD AND INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>Thin-blooded people generally have stomach trouble. They seldom recognise the fact that thin blood is the cause of their indigestion, but it is. Thin blood is one of the most common causes of stomach trouble; it affects the digestion very quickly. The glands that furnish the digestive fluids are diminished in their, activity, the stomach muscles are weakened, and there is a loss of nerve force. In this state of health noth- ing will more quickly restore appetite, digestion and normal nutrition than good, rich, red blood. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills act directly on the blood, 'asking it rich and red, and this enriched blood strengthens weak nerves, stimulates the tired muscles, and awakens to normal activity the glands that supply the digestive fluids. The first sign of returning health is an improved appetite, and soon the effect of these blood-mak- ing Pills is evident throughout the system. You find that what you eat does not distress you, and that you are strong and vigorous, instead of irritable and listless. You are now on the road to health, and care in your diet is all you need. Restore your appetite now by beginning Dr. Williams' Pink Pills at once. Of any dealer, or send 2s. 9d. for one box (13s. 9d. for six) to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-via- duct, London. Business as usual.</p>
        <p>FREE.—" What to Eat" is the title of a help- ful diet book free to all who send a postcard to Offer Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>PICTIMEDROME'S POPULAR</p>
        <p>PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>This week's programmes are a credit to the management. The first included the dramas "King's Messenger" and "Little Senorita"; the comedies, "Star Boarder," "A Merry Night"' and "Neighbours"; and the educa- tional and topical films, "The Gaumont Journal" and "Lunch at the Zoo." The fine bill of fare which concludes this evening includes the following: "For Her Child," "A Traitor to His Country," and "Claim No. 3" (dramas); " Winky Takes Up Farming," "Little Billy's Triumph," and "Oh! Those Relatives" (comedies); "Gymnas- tic Exercises at Karlberg" and "The Gaumont Journal" (educational). Every film lover should see these fine thug. Two fine Alms have been booked to top the bills at this favourite picture house next week, viz., "The Seven Sealed Orders" and "One Who Loved Him Best."</p>
        <p>FIRST-RATE FARE AT THE PICTURE HOUSE. The excellent sets of films shown during this week at the Picture House fully maintain its high standard. The first programme consisted of "Comedy and Tragedy" (a filmed version of Sir W. S. Gilbert's famous play) and "The Pendulum of Pate"—two thrilling dramas; and the comedies, "Slippery Sim's Inheri- tance," "Something to Adore," and "Dupin as Laundrymatti." The second programme, which can ,be seen for one night more; is specially strong on the' dramatic side, including as it does "The Un- seen Witness," "Maria's Sacrifice," "The Re- formation of the Gang," and "The Fatal Letter." "Susannah's Suit" (comedy). and "Johannesburg Baridits" (war) are also being shown For Next Week.</p>
        <p>Nest week's chief .a.t&amp;C'tiOn; will be "When a Woman's Forty" and "Motor Buccaneers," two films that are among the best recently released.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF THE GLOVES AND MITTENS SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Warrington continues to make splendid pro- gress in connection with the Grand Duke Michael's glove and mitten scheme, for, during the three weeks it has been in progress, upwards of 1,200 pairs of gloves and mittens have been dispatched for the use of the soldiers. A special class is held at the local headquarters—the Cr- field Memorial Memorial Hall—every Tuesday evening, and is always well attended. ,In addition to this, contributions of mittens and gloves are made by those connected )4, itn tie following works: Messrs. Crosfield, Armitage and Rigby, the Alliance Box Co., Richmond's, Locker's, Rylands', Hardy, and Stubs; the Salvation Army, and the Women's Liberal Association. In the cases of those firms at which only men are employed, their womenfolk form the work- ing parties.</p>
        <p>Each of these firms has two representatives on the general committee. The central com- mittee consists of Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, Mrs. Osborne, Miss Marsh, Mr. Willett and Mr. Hammersley. Everybody willing to help in making the glove 4 and mittens is heartily invited to attend the classes at the Crosfield Memorial Hall. The scheme is non-political, and its object is to send a quarter of a million of 'these comforts to the Front. Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield very kindly provide the whole of the wool required for the scheme by Warringtonians, and have offered a supply to all the large works of the town. Mr. Felix Maginn very kindly forwarded 250 packets of cigarettes, one of which has been fastened in each lof 250 pairs of gloves for- warded, during the week, to the distributing authorities.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODISTS HELP. The ladies connected with Friars' Green Inde- pendent Methodist Church held a sale of work on Wednesday, the object of it being to aid some of the many agencies to provide comforts for our soldiers and sailors. The sale was opened by Mrs. Peter Lawton, who, in a neat speech, strongly urged everybody to do their best., at this crisis in our national life, for those who are fighting our battles. During the evening there was a short concert, after which a numerous company partook of a hotpot supper. The sum realised was £lB, which is being divided between Lady Jellicoe's Fund for Sailors and the Fund for Warrington men at the Front. to whom the ladies have already dispatched parcels of suitable warm clothing.</p>
        <p>A LARGE PARCEL "FOR THE FRONT." Mrs. Edelsten gives notice that there will be no meeting at the Town Hall on Thursday, December 24th. She has to acknowledge many welcome gifts of suits, overcoats, blankets, &amp;T., for the wounded. The Borough Treasurer has reported a generous don'ation of £lO from the Warrington and District Teachers' Association. A sum of .25 has been allocated from the War- rington Fund towards the Christmas festivities at Fazakerley Hospital. • Mrs. Pemberton has asked .for mufflers for the men guarding Walton Arches, and Miss Broadbent has obtained body belts for the veterans.</p>
        <p>A large parcel, labelled For the Front," has been sent to Mrs. Ireland Blackburne. Letters of thanks' have been received from the 'Officer Commanding at Fazakerley, the Matron at the Warrington Infirmary, and Mrs. Black- . burne.</p>
        <p>WELCOME PARCELS FROM WYCLIFFE # CHURCH. Forty-five parcels have this week been dis- patched from Wycliffe Church to her "lads" who are serving in the Army and Navy, either at home or abroad. The parcels, which have been sent in the name of the members of the church and congregation, the young people, the boys and girls, and the Wycliffe P.S.A., contain tobacco, cake, mince-pies, Bovril, woollen hel- mets and socks, vaseline, and a variety of other articles, delectable and useful.</p>
        <p>HOW TWO.GIRLS HELPED. The sum of 12s. 6d. has been forwarded to Princess Mary's Fund in the names of Eva Waring and Florrie Green, of 13 and 15, Wind- sor-street, respectively. The two girls hit upon the happy idea of makirkg pin cushions and selling them at a penny each, and by such means raised the sum mentioned.</p>
        <p>PICTURE PATRONS' PRESENT TO "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>_ Over 31,000 cigarettes have so far been col- lected at the Star Kinema House, and these have been dispatched to the proper quarter for distribution—in the name of :Warrington living. picture lovers—among the "Tommies" at the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD'S QUOTA FOR THE FRONT. Up to date over seventy Burtonwood men are either serving in the Regular Army or have given themselves for service during the period of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>INVALIDED HOME..</head>
        <p>INVALIDED HOME..</p>
        <p>- Private William Barker, of the East Lanca- shire Regiment, and formerly of Warrington, has now been invalided home to the Masonic Hall Hospital, Bromley, Kent. Three weeks ago Private Barker was transferred from India —where he had served for seven years—to the front</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>REV. W. BRACECAMP." ON DUTY."</head>
        <p>REV. W. BRACECAMP." ON DUTY."</p>
        <p>At a special service for the members of the Tunbridge Wells and Rusthall Drill Clubs, held at Rusthall Church on Sunday, the special preacher was the Rev. W. Bracecamp, chaplain of the 4th Battalion Prince of Wales' Volun- teers (South Lancashire Regiment), stationed at Tunbridge Wells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL OLD _BOYS.</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL OLD _BOYS.</p>
        <p>- - . In response to his recent appeal, Mr. E. S. A. Robson, headmaster of the Warrington Second- ary School, continues to receive the names, par- ticulars of regiments, &amp;c., of many of the Old Boys who have enlisted. So far there are about 60 names on the school roll of honour, and the Headmaster trusts that all who have not yet forwarded their names will do so with- out delay.</p>
        <p>FROM TOWN CLERK'S OFFICE TO A.S.C. Messrs. Wilfred H. Wadsworth and Joseph Lawton, both until recently on the clerical staff in the Town Clerk's office, and who, about a month ago, joined the Army Service Corps, have both been selected to fill clerkships on the Headquarters,Staff of the Supply Section of the Army Service Corps. Their appointments speak well for the excellent business training they re- ceived under the guidance of the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle).</p>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL AND "TOMMIES'" WIVES. On Monday. night a representative delegate meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federa- tion of Trades Councils passed a resolution indignantly protesting against the police super- vision of soldiers' wives, and calling upon the Government to withdraw it immediately. The Warrington Trades and Labour Council had the matter before them on Tuesday evening, and decided to join, in the protest, Mr. Dikes re- marking that nothing was more calculated to make sensitive men consider their position before giving their services to the country than the knowledge of this slur upon their women- folk.</p>
