        <p>of the pig, 'whether it frightened it or not. (Laughter.) The Judge's Little Joke. Mr. Browne: If a cock made a noise the horse, being a spirited one, would bolt. His Honour: It may have an objection to pigs for all you know. (Laughter.) Mr. Browne :, Perhaps so, and Davenport, knowing this, was guilty of negligence in not providing against the possibility of the pig start- ling it. Thomas Saville, Miss Vera bfolyneaux, of the Plough Hotel, Lymm, and Mr. Harry Sam Saville gave evidence in support of this state- ment, and Mr. Robinson, of the Ford Motor Company, said that the account for repairs was £32, which was very reasonable, considering the extent of the damage. Maggie Chapman (15) said she was in charge of the horse, which took fright at the pigs and bolted. The horse was a quiet one and she had minded it before. Mr. Jordan submitted that there was no evi- dence of negligence. He denied that either Mr. Button or Davenport had admitted liability, as stated by plaintiff. "Quietest Horse That Ever Looked Through Bridle." Davenport said the, horse was the quietest that had ever looked through a bridle. He had never acknowledged he was to blame to anyone, and in his presence Mr. Hutton told Mr. Saville that although they were not liable they would do their best if the bill were not made heavy. In answer to Mr. Browne, witness said he al- ways took what precautions were necessary with horses. Mrs. Lloyd, Bridge Farm, Thelwall, said the pigs were making a noise because it was their feeding time. Mr. Hutton denied having said that the firm were liable. He said: "If we are liable send in your bill, but don't make it heavy." His Honour's Judgment. His Honour held that there was negligence, and awarded plaintiff £4l 6s. 11d.</p>
