        <p>assailing this heroic woman, who was giving her all—and what an all !—for this country. . . . I like the answer of that Irish clergyman, who said: 'To what sect does Miss Nightingale be- long? She belongs to a sect which is, mirortu- nately, very rare—the sect of Good Samari- tans.' " The, great work that Florence Nightingale accomplished for the Army and Navy, the speaker went on, could never he calculated. Her determination to succeed in precuring proper sanitation, proper fond, and care in health and sickness, her collection of evidence and imposing statistics, were, after many struggles, rewarded, and she was to the British soldier in India, as at'home, a saviour. Britain would be, to the end of all time, deeply indebted to Florence Nightingale for her tender pity for the sons who go forth to war. As they thought of the beautiful life of this grand woman, two words came to their minds—aspiration and attainment! The Mayor proposed, Canon WillTs and the large audience carried with hearty ap- plause, a vote ofl thanks to Mrs. Ridgway for her splendid lecture, a similar compliment being paid to the chairman, on the proposition of Alderman Bennett, seconded by the Bev. G. C. Briggs. Both before and after the lecture a fine male voice choir rendered selections, which included "Comrades in arms" and the National Anthems of the Allies. Mr. and Mrs. Sefton also sang a couple of charming duets.</p>
