        <p>of destruction mowing men down in thousands, and with paper-boys shouting about regiments "wiped out." Yet, if the Gospel meant any- thing, it meant that God gave Hiniself in Christ "hot merely that some day the world might run smoothly and happily, but because here and now the worth of every individual soul was infinite and priceless. To re-state that belief, and to hold it as a vivid conviction, would do at least two things. It would first make impossible the spirit of mere bitterness and. malice and hatred: the things would go—and they ought to go! To stand for honour and justice and liberty, and to strike strong blows for such ideals, did not mean to stand for violence and meanness of spirit and primitive passion. The Hand of God. Further, in this hour of international and national crisis, they must believe in the over-rirling hand of God con- trolling the world and leading it on- ward and upward. Courage and fortitude came from the conviction that above all temporal and visible powers, above the rise and fall of nations, above the clash of material forces, there ever remained the unshaken Throne of the Universe, and One occupied it Whose Kingdom was an everlasting Kingdom.</p>
